window.DEEP = {"en.skinner":{"k":"Behaviorism","t":["B. F. Skinner — Operant Conditioning","سكنر والاشتراط الإجرائي"],"body":[["Skinner extended behaviorism with **operant conditioning**: behavior that is followed by a pleasant consequence (**reinforcement**) becomes more likely; behavior followed by an unpleasant consequence becomes less likely. In *Verbal Behavior* (1957) he described language itself as behavior shaped by reinforcement.","السلوك الذي يتبعه تعزيز يتكرر، والذي يتبعه عقاب يقل. وفي كتابه «السلوك اللفظي» عدّ اللغة سلوكاً يتشكّل بالتعزيز."]],"pts":[["**Positive reinforcement** = adding something pleasant (praise) to increase a behavior.","التعزيز الإيجابي: إضافة شيء محبوب"],["**Negative reinforcement** = removing something unpleasant to increase a behavior — it is NOT punishment.","التعزيز السلبي: إزالة شيء مزعج، وليس عقاباً"],["Classroom link: **Audio-Lingual Method** drills (repetition, substitution, chain drills).","الربط الصفي: تدريبات الطريقة السمعية الشفهية"]],"traps":[["Negative reinforcement ≠ punishment: both change behavior in opposite directions.","التعزيز السلبي ليس عقاباً"],["Chomsky's 1959 review criticized Skinner's *Verbal Behavior* — not the other way round.","تشومسكي انتقد سكنر لا العكس"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.pavlov":{"k":"Behaviorism","t":["Ivan Pavlov — Classical Conditioning","بافلوف والاشتراط الكلاسيكي"],"body":[["Pavlov showed that a neutral stimulus (a bell) paired repeatedly with food comes to trigger salivation by itself. Learning here is an **automatic association** between two stimuli — the learner does not choose the response.","ارتباط تلقائي بين مثيرين: الجرس مع الطعام حتى يكفي الجرس وحده."]],"traps":[["Classical conditioning = involuntary response to a stimulus; operant = voluntary behavior shaped by its consequences.","الكلاسيكي استجابة لا إرادية، والإجرائي سلوك تشكّله النتائج"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.chomsky":{"k":"Nativism","t":["Noam Chomsky — LAD and Universal Grammar","تشومسكي وجهاز اكتساب اللغة"],"body":[["Chomsky argued that every child has an innate **Language Acquisition Device (LAD)** containing **Universal Grammar** — principles shared by all human languages. Input only 'switches on' the settings (parameters) of the child's particular language.","لكل طفل جهاز فطري فيه مبادئ مشتركة بين اللغات، والمدخلات تضبط إعدادات لغته فقط."],["He also distinguished **competence** (the speaker's internal knowledge of the language) from **performance** (actual use, which includes slips and hesitations).","الكفاية: المعرفة الداخلية باللغة. الأداء: الاستخدام الفعلي بأخطائه."]],"traps":[["Hymes added **communicative competence** as a reaction to Chomsky's narrow 'competence'.","هايمز أضاف الكفاية التواصلية رداً على تشومسكي"],["Critical Period Hypothesis is **Lenneberg**, not Chomsky.","الفترة الحرجة لِلينبرغ"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Yule, The Study of Language","pts":[["**Poverty of the stimulus**: the input children hear is too limited to explain the rules they end up knowing, so language cannot be pure imitation.","المدخل أفقر من أن يفسر القواعد التي يعرفها الطفل"]],"ex":[["A four-year-old says 'I goed to the park'. No adult said 'goed' — the child is applying a rule, which is evidence against pure imitation.","الطفل يقول goed مع أن أحداً لم يقلها: يطبق قاعدة ولا يقلّد فقط."]]},"en.piaget":{"k":"Cognitivism","t":["Jean Piaget — Stages of Cognitive Development","بياجيه ومراحل النمو المعرفي"],"body":[["Piaget saw children as active thinkers who build understanding in stages. New experiences are either **assimilated** into an existing schema or force the schema to change (**accommodation**).","الطفل يبني فهمه على مراحل: يستوعب الجديد في مخططه (تمثّل) أو يعدّل مخططه (مواءمة)."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Sensorimotor","ar":"⁧الحسية الحركية (0–2)⁩","items":[{"n":"Object permanence","ar":"⁧ثبات الشيء⁩"}]},{"n":"Preoperational","ar":"⁧ما قبل العمليات (2–7)⁩","items":[{"n":"Egocentrism","ar":"⁧التمركز حول الذات⁩"}]},{"n":"Concrete operational","ar":"⁧العمليات المحسوسة (7–11)⁩","items":[{"n":"Conservation","ar":"⁧الاحتفاظ والتصنيف⁩"}]},{"n":"Formal operational","ar":"⁧العمليات المجردة (11+)⁩","items":[{"n":"Abstract reasoning","ar":"⁧التفكير المجرد والفرضيات⁩"}]}]},"traps":[["'What if…?' hypothetical thinking belongs to the **formal operational** stage.","التفكير الافتراضي من المرحلة المجردة"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.zpd":{"k":"Social Constructivism","t":["Vygotsky — Zone of Proximal Development","فيجوتسكي ومنطقة النمو القريبة"],"body":[["The **ZPD** is the distance between what a learner can do **alone** and what they can do **with help** from a more knowledgeable other (MKO) — a teacher or a stronger peer. Teaching is most effective inside this zone.","المسافة بين ما يفعله المتعلم وحده وما يفعله بمساعدة شخص أعلم منه، والتعليم أنجح داخلها."],["**Scaffolding** (a term introduced by Wood, Bruner and Ross, 1976) is the temporary support given within the ZPD and gradually removed as the learner becomes independent.","السقالات: دعم مؤقت يُسحب تدريجياً حتى يستقل المتعلم."]],"traps":[["ZPD is not what the learner already masters alone.","ليست ما يتقنه المتعلم وحده"],["Scaffolding is **temporary** — permanent help is not scaffolding.","السقالات مؤقتة"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Lightbown & Spada","ex":[["A stronger student guides a partner through writing a paragraph, then gradually lets the partner do more — peer **scaffolding** within the ZPD.","طالب متمكن يساعد زميله ثم يترك له المهمة تدريجياً."]]},"en.lenneberg":{"k":"Nativism","t":["Lenneberg — The Critical Period Hypothesis","لينبرغ وفرضية الفترة الحرجة"],"body":[["In *Biological Foundations of Language* (1967) Eric Lenneberg argued that language acquisition is **biologically linked to age**: there is a limited window, from early childhood to about **puberty**, when the brain is ready to acquire language naturally. He tied the end of this period to brain **lateralization**. The idea supports **nativism**, because it treats language as a biological capacity with its own timetable.","اكتساب اللغة مرتبط بيولوجياً بالعمر: هناك فترة محددة تنتهي تقريباً عند البلوغ يكون فيها الدماغ مهيأً للاكتساب الطبيعي. وهذا يدعم الفطرية."]],"pts":[["Evidence often cited: children deprived of language in early childhood (such as the case of 'Genie') never fully acquired grammar.","حالات أطفال حُرموا اللغة صغاراً ولم يكتمل نحوهم"],["In L2 learning, the age effect is clearest in **pronunciation**: late starters usually keep a foreign accent.","أثر العمر في اللغة الثانية أوضح في النطق"],["Older learners are not hopeless: they often learn faster at first, but rarely reach a native-like accent.","الكبار يتقدمون أسرع في البداية لكن نادراً ما يصلون لنطق أصلي"]],"traps":[["The Critical Period Hypothesis supports **nativism**, not behaviorism.","الفترة الحرجة تدعم الفطرية"],["It is linked to **Lenneberg**, not Chomsky or Krashen.","الفرضية لِلينبرغ"]],"src":"Lenneberg, Biological Foundations of Language; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.ausubel":{"k":"Cognitivism","t":["David Ausubel — Meaningful Learning","أوزوبل والتعلم ذو المعنى"],"body":[["Ausubel contrasted **meaningful learning**, where new material is linked to concepts already in the learner's mind (he called this **subsumption**), with **rote learning**, where items are memorized as isolated facts. Meaningful learning lasts; rote learning is quickly forgotten because nothing holds it in place. For Ausubel, the most important factor in learning is **what the learner already knows**.","التعلم ذو المعنى يربط الجديد بما في ذهن المتعلم فيبقى، والتعلم الآلي حفظ معلومات منفصلة فيُنسى سريعاً. وأهم عامل في التعلم ما يعرفه المتعلم مسبقاً."]],"pts":[["**Advance organizer**: a short introduction (a chart, a question, a summary) given **before** new material to connect it to existing knowledge.","المنظم المتقدم: تمهيد يسبق الدرس ويربطه بالمعرفة السابقة"],["Classroom link: pre-reading and pre-listening activities that activate background knowledge.","أنشطة ما قبل القراءة والاستماع"]],"traps":[["Rote learning is the **opposite** of meaningful learning.","الآلي عكس ذي المعنى"],["Ausubel is a **cognitivist**, not a behaviorist.","أوزوبل معرفي"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching","ex":[["Before a reading text about recycling, the teacher shows a short chart linking it to what students know about waste — an **advance organizer**.","مخطط قبل النص يربط الموضوع بما يعرفه الطلاب: منظم متقدم."]]},"en.bruner":{"k":"Social Constructivism","t":["Jerome Bruner — Discovery and the Spiral Curriculum","برونر والاكتشاف والمنهج الحلزوني"],"body":[["Bruner saw learners as active builders of knowledge. In **discovery learning** they work out principles for themselves from examples, with the teacher guiding rather than telling. In *The Process of Education* (1960) he proposed the **spiral curriculum**: key ideas are revisited again and again, each time at a deeper and more complex level.","المتعلم يكتشف القاعدة بنفسه بتوجيه من المعلم. والمنهج الحلزوني يعيد الموضوع نفسه مرات بعمق متزايد."],["He also described three modes of representation, **enactive** (action), **iconic** (images) and **symbolic** (language), and with Wood and Ross (1976) introduced the term **scaffolding**.","وصف ثلاثة أنماط للتمثيل: بالفعل، وبالصورة، وبالرمز. وهو مع زميليه صاحب مصطلح السقالات."]],"ex":[["Students meet the past simple in Grade 7 and return to it with more complex uses in Grades 8 and 9: a **spiral curriculum**.","العودة للزمن نفسه بعمق أكبر كل سنة منهج حلزوني."]],"traps":[["Bruner = discovery learning and the spiral curriculum; operant conditioning is Skinner.","برونر للاكتشاف والحلزوني"],["Discovery learning is guided; it does not mean leaving learners without help.","الاكتشاف موجَّه لا عشوائي"]],"src":"Bruner, The Process of Education; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.maslow":{"k":"Humanism","t":["Abraham Maslow — Hierarchy of Needs","ماسلو وهرم الحاجات"],"body":[["Maslow arranged human needs in a pyramid: **physiological** needs (food, sleep), **safety**, **love and belonging**, **esteem**, and at the top **self-actualization**, reaching one's full potential. Lower needs must be reasonably met before higher ones can drive behaviour. A hungry, frightened or isolated student is not ready to focus on learning.","الحاجات بالترتيب: الجسدية، ثم الأمان، ثم الانتماء، ثم التقدير، ثم تحقيق الذات في القمة. ولا تحرّك الحاجة العليا السلوك قبل إشباع الدنيا، فالطالب الجائع أو الخائف لا يتعلم جيداً."]],"pts":[["Classroom link: a safe, welcoming class and respect for every student come before demanding tasks.","صف آمن ومحترم قبل المهام الصعبة"]],"traps":[["**Self-actualization** is at the top of the pyramid; physiological needs are at the base.","تحقيق الذات في القمة"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.rogers":{"k":"Humanism","t":["Carl Rogers — Learner-Centred Learning","روجرز والتعلم المتمركز حول المتعلم"],"body":[["Rogers, a psychologist, carried his client-centred therapy into education (*Freedom to Learn*, 1969). Learning is most effective when it is **learner-centred**, personally meaningful and happens in a **non-threatening** climate. The teacher is a **facilitator** who creates that climate and trusts learners to direct much of their own learning, not a transmitter of facts.","نقل روجرز فكرة العلاج المتمركز حول العميل إلى التعليم: التعلم أنجح إذا تمركز حول المتعلم وكان ذا معنى له وفي جو آمن. والمعلم ميسّر لا ناقل معلومات."]],"pts":[["[[Community Language Learning|en.meth.humanistic]] (Curran) is built on Rogers' counselling model: the teacher acts as a 'counsellor'.","التعلم الجماعي للغة مبني على نموذج روجرز"]],"traps":[["Rogers = teacher as **facilitator**; this places him in **humanism**.","المعلم ميسّر: روجرز والإنسانية"],["Methods such as CLL and Suggestopedia are classed as **humanistic** methods.","CLL والإيحائية طرق إنسانية"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods in Language Teaching"},"en.ling.phonbranches":{"k":"Phonetics","t":["Phonetics, Phonology and the Branches of Phonetics","علم الأصوات وفروعه"],"body":[["**Phonetics** describes speech sounds as physical events and can describe the sounds of any language. **Phonology** studies how one language organises its sounds into contrasts (phonemes) and patterns.","علم الأصوات يصف الأصوات كأحداث فيزيائية في أي لغة، وعلم وظائف الأصوات يدرس نظام لغة بعينها."]],"pts":[["**Articulatory**: how the speech organs (lips, tongue, teeth, palate, vocal cords) produce sounds — the basis of place, manner and voicing.","النطقي: كيف تنتج أعضاء النطق الصوت"],["**Acoustic**: the physical properties of sound waves (frequency, duration, intensity).","الفيزيائي: خصائص الموجات الصوتية"],["**Auditory**: how the ear and brain receive and perceive speech sounds.","السمعي: الاستقبال والإدراك"],["**Forensic**: applying phonetics to legal cases, e.g. identifying a speaker from a recording.","الجنائي: تحديد المتكلم من تسجيل"]],"traps":[["Sound waves = **acoustic**; perception = **auditory**; speech organs = **articulatory**.","موجات / إدراك / نطق"],["The actions of the speech organs in producing sounds = **articulation**.","النطق"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language; Roach, English Phonetics and Phonology","ex":[["Describing how /θ/ in 'think' is made (tongue tip between the teeth) is **phonetics**; noticing that 'sink' instead of 'think' changes the word is **phonology**.","وصف النطق صوتيات، وتغيّر الكلمة وظائف أصوات."]]},"en.ling.connected":{"k":"Connected speech","t":["Assimilation, Elision and Linking","المماثلة والحذف والوصل"],"body":[["In natural speech, words run together and sounds adjust to their neighbours to make articulation easier. These changes are systematic, and learners need them mainly for **listening**: a learner who expects every word in its dictionary form will not recognise them in fast speech.","في الكلام الطبيعي تتأثر الأصوات بجيرانها، ويحتاجها الطالب للاستماع أكثر من النطق."]],"pts":[["**Assimilation**: a sound becomes more like a neighbour — 'ten boys' /tem bɔɪz/, 'handbag' /hæmbæg/, 'did you' /dɪdʒu/ (/d/ + /j/ merge into /dʒ/).","مماثلة: صوت يشبه جاره"],["**Elision**: a sound disappears — 'next day' /neks deɪ/, 'last night' /lɑːs naɪt/, 'you and me' /juː ən miː/.","حذف صوت"],["**Linking**: words join smoothly — linking /r/ in 'far away'; **intrusion**: a sound not in the spelling is added — 'law and order' /lɔːr ənd/, 'go on' /gəʊ wɒn/.","وصل وإقحام"],["**Weak forms**: function words reduce in unstressed position — can /kən/, to /tə/, and /ən/.","صيغ ضعيفة"]],"traps":[["A sound that *changes* = assimilation; a sound that *disappears* = elision; an extra sound = intrusion.","يتغير / يختفي / يُضاف"],["Stress, rhythm and intonation are **suprasegmental**, not connected-speech processes.","النبر والتنغيم فوق مقطعية"]],"src":"Roach, English Phonetics and Phonology; Field, Listening in the Language Classroom"},"en.ling.morpheme":{"k":"Morphology","t":["Morphemes: Free and Bound","المورفيمات: الحرة والمقيدة"],"body":[["A **morpheme** is the smallest unit of meaning or grammatical function. 'Tourists' contains three: *tour* (meaning), *-ist* (person who does) and *-s* (plural). Morphemes are classified in two steps: first free vs bound, then by job.","المورفيم أصغر وحدة معنى أو وظيفة نحوية، ونصنّفه أولاً حر/مقيد ثم حسب وظيفته."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Free","ar":"⁧حرة: تقف وحدها⁩","items":[{"n":"Lexical","ar":"⁧محتوى: book, sad, open⁩"},{"n":"Functional","ar":"⁧نحوية: the, and, in, it⁩"}]},{"n":"Bound","ar":"⁧مقيدة: لا تقف وحدها⁩","items":[{"n":"Derivational","ar":"⁧تصنع كلمة: un-, -ness, -ment⁩"},{"n":"Inflectional","ar":"⁧تضيف نحواً: -s, -ed, -est⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["**Lexeme**: wolf/wolves = one lexeme; grill/grilling = one lexeme.","الوحدة المعجمية تجمع الصيغ"],["**Allomorph**: different forms of one morpheme — plural /s/ cats, /z/ dogs, /ɪz/ buses, and even -en in oxen.","صور مختلفة لمورفيم واحد"],["**Bound roots** carry meaning but cannot stand alone: -ceive (receive), -mit (transmit).","جذور مقيدة"]],"traps":[["Lexical morphemes = content words (verbs, nouns, adjectives); articles and prepositions are functional.","المعجمي محتوى"],["Functional morphemes are **free**, not bound.","الوظيفي حر"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language"},"en.ling.inflderiv":{"k":"Morphology","t":["Inflectional vs Derivational","التصريفي والاشتقاقي"],"body":[["**Inflectional** morphemes mark grammar inside the same word: number, possession, tense, aspect, comparison. **Derivational** morphemes create a new word with a new dictionary entry, often in a new word class.","التصريفي يبقي الكلمة نفسها ويضيف نحواً، والاشتقاقي يصنع مدخلاً معجمياً جديداً."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"The 8 inflections","ar":"⁧التصريفية الثمانية⁩","items":[{"n":"Noun: -s, -'s","ar":"⁧الجمع والملكية⁩"},{"n":"Verb: -s, -ed, -en, -ing","ar":"⁧الغائب والماضي والتام والمستمر⁩"},{"n":"Adj: -er, -est","ar":"⁧المقارنة والتفضيل⁩"}]},{"n":"Derivational","ar":"⁧الاشتقاقية⁩","items":[{"n":"Class-changing","ar":"teach → teacher, metal → metallic"},{"n":"Meaning-changing","ar":"happy → unhappy, write → rewrite"}]}]},"pts":[["Inflections are **always suffixes** in English; derivational affixes can be prefixes or suffixes.","التصريفية لواحق دائماً"],["Inflections come **last**: teach-er-s, care-less-ness (no inflection), modern-ize-d.","التصريفي يأتي آخراً"],["Derivational suffixes by class: nouns -ment, -ness, -tion, -er/-or; adjectives -ful, -less, -ic, -able; verbs -ate, -ify, -ize, -en.","لواحق حسب الصنف"]],"traps":[["-er in **taller** = inflectional; -er in **teacher** = derivational.","taller / teacher"],["-or in visitor, -less in hopeless, -ment in government are all derivational.","كلها اشتقاقية"],["Inflectional suffixes never change the word class — but some derivational ones don't either (child → childhood, friend → friendship), so also ask: is it a new word?","التصريفية لا تغيّر الصنف أبداً، وبعض الاشتقاقية كذلك"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language; Fromkin, Rodman & Hyams, An Introduction to Language"},"en.ling.wordclass":{"k":"Syntax","t":["Word Classes: Key Distinctions","أقسام الكلام: فروق مهمة"],"body":[["**Content (open) classes** — nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs — carry meaning and readily accept new words. **Function (closed) classes** — articles, pronouns, prepositions, conjunctions, auxiliaries — are small fixed sets that signal grammatical relations.","أقسام المحتوى تحمل المعنى ويدخلها الجديد، وأقسام الوظيفة مجموعات محدودة تربط الجملة."]],"pts":[["Adjectives: **attributive** before a noun (an expensive watch); **predicative** after a linking verb (the watch is expensive).","صفة قبلية / خبرية"],["Adverbs modify verbs (drives fast), adjectives (very tall) and other adverbs (too quickly); intensifiers such as very and too are adverbs.","الظرف يصف الفعل والصفة والظرف"],["Pronouns: reflexive (myself), reciprocal (each other, one another), demonstrative (this, that, these, those), relative (who, which, that).","أنواع الضمائر"],["Auxiliaries: primary (be, have, do) and modal (can, may, should, must, will). Interjections (Ouch!, Wow!) express emotion.","الأفعال المساعدة وأدوات التعجب"],["'charming', 'friendly' are **adjectives** (the -ing and -ly endings mislead).","charming و friendly صفتان"]],"traps":[["'friendly', 'lovely', 'lonely' end in -ly but are **adjectives**.","صفات رغم -ly"],["'each other' is **reciprocal**, not reflexive.","تبادلي"],["English has two indefinite articles (a, an) and one definite article (the).","أداتا تنكير وأداة تعريف"]],"src":"Swan, Practical English Usage; Crystal, The Cambridge Encyclopedia of the English Language"},"en.ling.transitivity":{"k":"Syntax","t":["Verb Patterns and Sentence Elements","أنماط الأفعال وعناصر الجملة"],"body":[["Clause elements are **Subject, Verb, Object, Complement, Adverbial**. The verb chooses which of them are needed: this is its **valency** or transitivity. An optional adverbial that can be removed without breaking the clause (nearly, yesterday, in the garden) is an **adjunct**.","عناصر الجملة: فاعل وفعل ومفعول وتكملة وظرف، والفعل يحدد ما يلزم منها. والعنصر الاختياري الذي يُحذف دون أن تنكسر الجملة هو adjunct."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Intransitive","ar":"⁧لازم⁩","items":[{"n":"She arrived.","ar":"⁧بلا مفعول⁩"}]},{"n":"Transitive","ar":"⁧متعدٍّ⁩","items":[{"n":"He kicked the ball.","ar":"⁧مفعول مباشر⁩"}]},{"n":"Ditransitive","ar":"⁧متعدٍّ لمفعولين⁩","items":[{"n":"I gave Sami the photo.","ar":"⁧Sami غير مباشر، photo مباشر⁩"}]},{"n":"Linking","ar":"⁧فعل ربط⁩","items":[{"n":"She seems tired.","ar":"⁧تكملة تصف الفاعل⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["**Finite** verbs show tense and agree with the subject (she works); **non-finite** forms do not (to work, working, worked).","متصرف / غير متصرف"],["Linking verbs: be, seem, become, look, appear, feel — followed by an adjective or noun complement.","أفعال الربط"],["**Phrasal verbs** = verb + particle with one meaning (look after, give up); errors like 'look after of' or 'looking to my wallet' show the learner has not stored the verb + particle as one fixed unit.","الأفعال المركبة وحدة واحدة"]],"traps":[["In 'I gave Sami the photo', Sami (the receiver) is the **indirect** object.","المستلم غير مباشر"],["'My child is friendly': after a linking verb we need an adjective, not an adverb.","بعد فعل الربط صفة"]],"src":"Swan, Practical English Usage; Thornbury, How to Teach Grammar","ex":[["Rewrite 'I gave Sami the photo' as 'I gave the photo **to** Sami': the noun that can move after to/for is the indirect object.","ما ينتقل بعد to/for هو المفعول غير المباشر."]]},"en.ling.lexrel":{"k":"Semantics","t":["Lexical Relations","علاقات المعنى بين الكلمات"],"body":[["Speakers often explain a word by relating it to another: 'conceal' means 'hide' (synonymy), 'shallow' is the opposite of 'deep' (antonymy), a 'daffodil' is a kind of flower (hyponymy). These relations organise the mental lexicon and are a powerful way to teach vocabulary.","نشرح الكلمة غالباً بربطها بأخرى: مرادف، ضد، نوع من. وهذه العلاقات تنظّم المعجم الذهني."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Synonymy","ar":"⁧ترادف⁩","items":[{"n":"big – large","ar":"declare – announce"}],"trap":"⁧الترادف التام نادر: big sister ≠ large sister⁩"},{"n":"Hyponymy","ar":"⁧اشتمال: نوع من⁩","items":[{"n":"banana → fruit","ar":"⁧fruit هي الكلمة الشاملة⁩"}],"trap":"⁧الكلمتان (apple, banana) أنواع مشتركة (co-hyponyms)⁩"},{"n":"Meronymy","ar":"⁧جزء من كل⁩","items":[{"n":"branch → tree","ar":"wheel → car"}],"trap":"⁧ليس 'نوعاً من'⁩"},{"n":"Collocation","ar":"⁧تلازم لفظي⁩","items":[{"n":"heavy rain","ar":"make a mistake, strong coffee"}],"trap":"⁧تلازم عادة لا علاقة معنى منطقية⁩"}]},"pts":[["**Superordinate** = the general term (fruit); **hyponym** = the specific term (banana); same-level members are **co-hyponyms**.","الشامل والمشمول"],["**Metonymy**: naming a thing by something closely associated with it — 'The White House said…', 'drink a bottle'.","الكناية بالمجاورة"]],"traps":[["banana–fruit = hyponymy; branch–tree = meronymy.","اشتمال / جزء"],["see/sea are homophones, not synonyms.","متجانسان صوتياً"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language; Saeed, Semantics"},"en.ling.antonyms":{"k":"Semantics","t":["Types of Antonyms","أنواع التضاد"],"body":[["Not all opposites work the same way, and the exam asks you to name the type. The quickest tests: can you say 'very' or 'more'? Does 'not X' automatically mean Y? Does one word describe the same event from the other side?","اختبارات سريعة: هل تقبل very؟ هل نفي الأول يعني الثاني؟ هل يصفان الحدث نفسه من جهتين؟"]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Gradable","ar":"⁧متدرج⁩","items":[{"n":"hot / cold","ar":"⁧very hot, warmer — بينهما درجات⁩"}],"trap":"not hot ≠ cold"},{"n":"Complementary","ar":"⁧تكاملي (غير متدرج)⁩","items":[{"n":"alive / dead","ar":"male / female, pass / fail"}],"trap":"not alive = dead"},{"n":"Converse","ar":"⁧عكسي (علائقي)⁩","items":[{"n":"buy / sell","ar":"teacher / student, above / below"}],"trap":"A sells to B = B buys from A"}]},"pts":[["**Reversives** describe the reverse of an action: tie/untie, enter/exit, pack/unpack.","العكسي في الفعل"]],"traps":[["male/female and alive/dead are **complementary**, not gradable.","تكاملي"],["Saying the opposite of what you mean is **irony**, not antonymy.","التهكم ليس تضاداً"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language; Saeed, Semantics"},"en.ling.homonymy":{"k":"Semantics","t":["Homonyms, Homophones, Homographs, Polysemy","المشترك اللفظي والمتجانسات وتعدد المعنى"],"body":[["These terms describe words that share **form**. The key questions are: is it the sound, the spelling, or both that is shared — and are the meanings **related** or not?","السؤال: ما المشترك، النطق أم الكتابة أم كلاهما؟ وهل المعاني مترابطة أم لا؟"]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Homophones","ar":"⁧نطق واحد، كتابة مختلفة⁩","items":[{"n":"see / sea","ar":"stake / steak, meat / meet"}]},{"n":"Homographs","ar":"⁧كتابة واحدة، نطق أو معنى مختلف⁩","items":[{"n":"lead / lead","ar":"⁧الرصاص / يقود⁩"}]},{"n":"Homonyms","ar":"⁧شكل واحد ومعانٍ غير مترابطة⁩","items":[{"n":"bank","ar":"⁧ضفة النهر / المصرف⁩"}]},{"n":"Polysemy","ar":"⁧كلمة واحدة بمعانٍ مترابطة⁩","items":[{"n":"head","ar":"⁧رأس الجسم / رئيس الشركة⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["Test for polysemy: can you see a shared core meaning? head of body and head of company = 'top, controlling part'.","معنى مشترك = تعدد معنى"],["Lexical ambiguity often comes from homonyms or polysemous words: 'I'll give you a ring'.","مصدر الغموض المعجمي"]],"traps":[["bank (river / money) = **homonym**; head (body / company) = **polysemy**.","bank مشترك، head تعدد"],["stake/steak = homophones; lead (metal) / lead (guide) = homographs.","متجانس صوتي / كتابي"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language"},"en.ling.cohesion":{"k":"Discourse","t":["Cohesion vs Coherence","الترابط الشكلي والترابط المعنوي"],"body":[["**Cohesion** (Halliday & Hasan, 1976) is the network of visible ties between sentences — you can point to the words that create it. **Coherence** is in the reader's mind: the sense that the ideas belong together and make sense in context. Cohesion helps coherence but does not guarantee it.","الترابط الشكلي روابط تشير إليها بإصبعك، والمعنوي إحساس القارئ بأن الأفكار منطقية ومتصلة."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Reference","ar":"⁧إحالة⁩","items":[{"n":"Anaphoric","ar":"⁧للخلف: Ali… He⁩"},{"n":"Cataphoric","ar":"⁧للأمام: When he arrived, Ali…⁩"}]},{"n":"Substitution & ellipsis","ar":"⁧استبدال وحذف⁩","items":[{"n":"do so, one","ar":"I'll take this one"},{"n":"Ellipsis","ar":"I can swim and Sara can too"}]},{"n":"Conjunction","ar":"⁧أدوات ربط⁩","items":[{"n":"however, so","ar":"⁧previously, meanwhile = زمنية⁩"}]},{"n":"Lexical cohesion","ar":"⁧ترابط معجمي⁩","items":[{"n":"repetition","ar":"⁧تكرار، مرادف، كلمات من حقل واحد⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["A text can be cohesive but incoherent (linked sentences about unrelated things), or coherent with few ties: 'That's the phone.' — 'I'm in the shower.' — 'OK.'","قد يوجد أحدهما دون الآخر"],["**Exophoric** reference points outside the text to the situation ('Look at *that*!'), so Halliday & Hasan do not count it as a cohesive tie.","إحالة خارجية لا تُعد ترابطاً شكلياً"]],"traps":[["Anaphoric and cataphoric reference = **cohesion**, not coherence.","الإحالة ترابط شكلي"],["'The degree to which a text makes sense' = **coherence**.","منطقية النص = ترابط معنوي"]],"src":"Halliday & Hasan, Cohesion in English; Yule, The Study of Language"},"en.meth.levels":{"k":"Methods","t":["Approach, Method, Technique","المدخل والطريقة والأسلوب"],"body":[["Edward Anthony (1963) proposed a three-level hierarchy. The **approach** is axiomatic: a set of assumptions about the nature of language and learning. The **method** is procedural: an overall plan for presenting language that rests on the approach. The **technique** is implementational: a specific classroom trick or activity that must be consistent with the method.","أنتوني (1963): المدخل افتراضات، والطريقة خطة، والأسلوب تطبيق داخل الصف."],["Richards and Rodgers reworked the model: **approach** (theory), **design** (objectives, syllabus, roles of teacher and learner, materials) and **procedure** (classroom techniques). In their model, 'method' is the umbrella that covers all three.","ريتشاردز ورودجرز: مدخل ← تصميم ← إجراء، والطريقة مظلة تجمعها."]],"pts":[["Approach = 'what we believe'; method = 'how we plan'; technique = 'what we do today'.","نعتقد ← نخطط ← ننفّذ"],["A **strategy** is broader than a technique: a set of techniques used to reach an aim.","الاستراتيجية أوسع من الأسلوب"]],"ex":[["A teacher believes language is learned through communication (approach), follows a task-based plan for the unit (method), and today runs an information-gap activity in pairs (technique).","معتقد تواصلي = مدخل، خطة بالمهام = طريقة، نشاط فجوة المعلومات = أسلوب."]],"traps":[["'Theoretical positions and beliefs about language and learning' = approach, not method.","المعتقدات النظرية = مدخل"],["A dictation or a drill is a technique — one activity, not a whole method.","الإملاء أسلوب لا طريقة"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods in Language Teaching; Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.meth.gtm":{"k":"Methods","t":["The Grammar-Translation Method","طريقة القواعد والترجمة"],"body":[["Grammar-Translation (sometimes called the Classical Method) treats a foreign language as a body of rules and words to be learned and then used for translation. Its aim is to read the literature of the language and to develop mental discipline.","تعامل اللغة كقواعد ومفردات تُحفظ ثم تُستخدم للترجمة، وهدفها قراءة الأدب."]],"pts":[["Medium of instruction: the **mother tongue**.","اللغة الأم وسيلة الشرح"],["Grammar taught **deductively**: rule first, then practice.","القاعدة أولاً (استنباطي)"],["Bilingual word lists, literary texts, translation in both directions.","قوائم مفردات ونصوص أدبية وترجمة"],["**Accuracy** is the standard; little or no attention to pronunciation or speaking.","الدقة أولاً ولا اهتمام بالنطق والكلام"],["Teacher-centred; the same treatment for all learners.","متمركزة حول المعلم"],["**No single founder**: it grew in 19th-century German (Prussian) schools — in the US it was first called the 'Prussian Method'. Its best-known textbook writers: **Seidenstücker, Plötz, Ollendorf, Meidinger**.","لا مؤسس واحد لها: نشأت في مدارس بروسيا، وأشهر مؤلفيها زايدنشتوكر وبلوتس وأولندورف وماينيغر"]],"traps":[["Grammar-Translation = deductive; the Direct Method and ALM = inductive.","الترجمة استنباطية، والمباشرة والسمعية استقرائية"],["'Ignores speaking' or 'focuses on reading and writing' points to GTM.","إهمال الكلام = القواعد والترجمة"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Larsen-Freeman & Anderson, Techniques and Principles in Language Teaching"},"en.meth.direct":{"k":"Methods","t":["The Direct Method","الطريقة المباشرة"],"body":[["Reformers at the end of the 19th century argued that learners should connect meaning **directly** to the new language, without passing through the mother tongue. Schools such as Berlitz made the approach popular. Because it imitates first-language learning, it is also called the **natural method**.","ربط المعنى باللغة الجديدة مباشرة دون المرور باللغة الأم، ولذلك تُسمّى الطريقة الطبيعية."]],"pts":[["Classes are conducted only in the target language.","اللغة الهدف فقط"],["Concrete words through objects, pictures and demonstration; abstract words through association of ideas.","المحسوس بالأشياء والمجرد بربط الأفكار"],["Grammar taught **inductively**; oral skills built through question-and-answer exchanges.","قاعدة استقرائية وسؤال وجواب"],["Correct pronunciation and everyday vocabulary are emphasised.","النطق الصحيح ومفردات الحياة اليومية"],["Who's who: **Gouin** (the earlier Series Method — sequences of related actions), **Sauveur** (natural, oral teaching in Boston from the late 1860s), **Berlitz** (spread it through his schools).","غوان (طريقة السلاسل)، وسوفور، وبيرليتز الذي نشرها بمدارسه"]],"traps":[["Direct Method ≠ Natural Approach: the first is a 19th-century method; the second is Krashen & Terrell (1983).","الطريقة الطبيعية غير المدخل الطبيعي"],["It emphasises listening and speaking — not reading and writing.","التركيز استماع وكلام"],["'Gouin and Berlitz' → the **Direct Method** family, not Audio-Lingual.","غوان وبيرليتز = المباشرة"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.meth.alm":{"k":"Methods","t":["The Audio-Lingual Method","الطريقة السمعية الشفهية"],"body":[["ALM developed in the United States in the 1950s, partly from intensive army language programmes. It combined **structural linguistics** (language as patterns of sounds and structures) with **behaviorist** psychology (learning as habit formation).","نشأت في أمريكا في الخمسينات من اللسانيات البنيوية والسلوكية."],["Its decline came when Chomsky's ideas showed that language is rule-governed and creative, not a set of imitated habits.","تراجعت حين بيّن تشومسكي أن اللغة إبداعية تحكمها قواعد"]],"pts":[["Dialogue memorisation (mim-mem) and **pattern drills**: repetition, substitution, transformation, chain, backward build-up.","حفظ الحوار وتدريبات الأنماط"],["Errors are avoided; correct responses get immediate **reinforcement**.","منع الخطأ والتعزيز الفوري"],["Grammar through examples — no explicit rules.","القاعدة من الأمثلة دون شرح"],["Minimal pairs for pronunciation; order L → S → R → W.","الثنائيات الصغرى وترتيب المهارات"],["Who's who: **Charles Fries** (University of Michigan, structural linguistics), **Robert Lado** (also Contrastive Analysis), **Nelson Brooks** (coined 'audiolingualism', 1964); learning theory from **Skinner**'s behaviorism.","فريز ولادو وبروكس، والنظرية من سلوكية سكنر"]],"traps":[["Behaviorism + habit formation + drills = ALM (not CLT).","سلوكية + عادات + تدريبات = السمعية الشفهية"],["In ALM the teacher is the model and controller; learners imitate.","المعلم نموذج ومتحكم"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Larsen-Freeman & Anderson, Techniques and Principles"},"en.meth.humanistic":{"k":"Methods","t":["Humanistic Methods: Silent Way, Suggestopedia, CLL","الطرق الإنسانية"],"body":[["The humanistic (designer) methods that spread in the 1960s–70s treat the learner as a whole person: feelings, confidence and relationships matter as much as the language itself. Each one attacks a different obstacle to learning.","تتعامل مع المتعلم كإنسان كامل، وكل طريقة تعالج عائقاً مختلفاً."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Silent Way","ar":"⁧الطريقة الصامتة — Gattegno⁩","items":[{"n":"Teacher silence","ar":"⁧المعلم يصمت ليكتشف الطالب⁩","kw":["teaching subordinated to learning"]},{"n":"Tools","ar":"⁧قضبان كويزنير ولوحات الألوان⁩","kw":["Cuisenaire rods","Fidel charts"]}],"trap":"⁧الطلاب يتكلمون أكثر، لا أن المعلم ممنوع من الكلام⁩"},{"n":"Suggestopedia","ar":"⁧الإيحائية — Lozanov⁩","items":[{"n":"Desuggestion","ar":"⁧إزالة الحواجز النفسية⁩","kw":["relaxation"]},{"n":"Setting","ar":"⁧موسيقى باروك وكراسٍ مريحة وملصقات⁩","kw":["Baroque music","peripheral learning"]}],"trap":"⁧الموسيقى والراحة = الإيحائية⁩"},{"n":"Community Language Learning","ar":"⁧التعلم الجماعي — Curran⁩","items":[{"n":"Counselor","ar":"⁧المعلم مرشد والطالب عميل⁩","kw":["counseling-learning"]},{"n":"Procedure","ar":"⁧ترجمة وتسجيل محادثة ثم تحليلها⁩","kw":["recorded conversation"]}],"trap":"⁧دور المعلم في CLL = مرشد (counselor)⁩"}]},"pts":[["Silent Way: the teacher models very little; learners discover the language and correct each other.","الطالب يكتشف ويصحّح زميله"],["CLL: learners are 'clients'; the teacher translates what they want to say until they can manage alone.","المعلم يترجم حتى يستقل الطالب"]],"ex":[["Using coloured rods, a teacher silently points to a red rod and a blue rod; students try 'a red rod and a blue rod', and classmates correct each other while the teacher only gestures.","قضبان ملونة ومعلم صامت وتصحيح بين الطلاب: الطريقة الصامتة."]],"traps":[["Founders: **Gattegno** → Silent Way; **Georgi Lozanov** → Suggestopedia; **Charles Curran** → Community Language Learning.","غاتينو الصامتة، لوزانوف الإيحائية، كوران الجماعي"],["Cuisenaire rods and colour charts → the **Silent Way**.","القضبان الملونة = الصامتة"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Brown, Teaching by Principles; Larsen-Freeman & Anderson"},"en.meth.tpr":{"k":"Methods","t":["Total Physical Response (James Asher)","الاستجابة الجسدية الكاملة"],"body":[["TPR coordinates speech and action. Asher observed that children understand a great deal before they speak, and much of what they hear is commands. In TPR the teacher gives commands, models the action, and learners respond physically; speaking comes later, when learners feel ready.","ينسّق الكلام مع الحركة: المعلم يأمر ويمثّل، والطلاب يستجيبون بأجسادهم، والكلام يأتي لاحقاً."]],"pts":[["Comprehension before production; low anxiety.","الفهم قبل الإنتاج وقلق منخفض"],["Movement helps memory (the 'trace' idea).","الحركة تقوي التذكر"],["Best for beginners and young learners; weak for abstract language.","مناسبة للمبتدئين والصغار"]],"traps":[["'Simon Says' and 'listen and do' = TPR.","لعبة سايمون يقول = TPR"],["TPR is a listening-first method; learners are not forced to speak early.","لا يُجبر الطالب على الكلام مبكراً"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Larsen-Freeman & Anderson"},"en.meth.natural":{"k":"Methods","t":["The Natural Approach (Krashen & Terrell)","المدخل الطبيعي"],"body":[["Published by Krashen and Terrell in 1983, the Natural Approach turns Krashen's theory of acquisition into classroom practice. Class time is spent giving learners **comprehensible input** about things that interest them, in a low-anxiety atmosphere, so that acquisition happens naturally.","يحوّل نظرية كراشن إلى ممارسة: مدخلات مفهومة وجو منخفض القلق ليحدث الاكتساب طبيعياً."]],"pts":[["Acquisition (subconscious) matters more than learning (conscious rules).","الاكتساب أهم من التعلم الواعي"],["Input at **i+1**: slightly above the learner's current level.","مدخل أعلى قليلاً من المستوى"],["A **silent period** is respected; speech emerges in stages.","احترام فترة الصمت"],["Low **affective filter**: anxiety blocks input.","مصفاة وجدانية منخفضة"]],"traps":[["Natural Approach = Krashen & Terrell — not Chomsky, not Asher.","كراشن وتيريل"],["It is not the same as the Direct Method's nickname 'natural method'.","ليست الطريقة المباشرة"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.meth.clt":{"k":"Communicative Approach","t":["CLT and Communicative Competence","التدريس التواصلي والكفاية التواصلية"],"body":[["Chomsky's 'competence' meant knowledge of grammar. **Dell Hymes** (1972) argued this was too narrow and proposed **communicative competence**: the knowledge and ability to use language appropriately in social contexts. Canale and Swain (1980) divided it into grammatical, sociolinguistic and strategic competence, and Canale (1983) added discourse competence.","هايمز وسّع مفهوم تشومسكي إلى الكفاية التواصلية، وقسّمها كانال وسوين (1980) إلى ثلاثة مكونات، ثم أضاف كانال (1983) الكفاية الخطابية."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Grammatical","ar":"⁧النحوية⁩","items":[{"n":"Form","ar":"⁧قواعد ومفردات ونطق⁩","kw":["linguistic competence"]}]},{"n":"Sociolinguistic","ar":"⁧اللغوية الاجتماعية⁩","items":[{"n":"Appropriacy","ar":"⁧المناسب للموقف والمخاطَب⁩","kw":["register"]}]},{"n":"Discourse","ar":"⁧الخطابية⁩","items":[{"n":"Cohesion","ar":"⁧ربط الجمل في نص متماسك⁩","kw":["coherence"]}]},{"n":"Strategic","ar":"⁧الاستراتيجية⁩","items":[{"n":"Repair","ar":"⁧تعويض النقص وإصلاح التواصل⁩","kw":["paraphrase","gesture"]}]}]},"pts":[["Meaning is primary; language is learned through using it to communicate.","المعنى أولاً"],["Fluency and accuracy are complementary; errors are a natural part of learning.","الطلاقة والدقة معاً"],["Teacher = facilitator and co-communicator; learners = negotiators of meaning.","المعلم ميسّر والطالب مفاوض للمعنى"],["Wilkins' **notional-functional syllabus** was an early CLT syllabus.","منهج المفاهيم والوظائف"],["Who said what: **Hymes** (communicative competence), **Halliday** (functions of language), **Wilkins** (Notional Syllabuses, 1976), **Widdowson** (Teaching Language as Communication, 1978), **Canale & Swain** (components), **Brumfit & Johnson** (The Communicative Approach to Language Teaching, 1979).","هايمز، هاليداي، ويلكنز، ويدوسون، كانال وسوين، برومفيت وجونسون"]],"traps":[["Communicative competence = linguistic competence **plus** the ability to use it appropriately.","ليست القواعد وحدها"],["Communicative competence is Hymes; competence vs performance is Chomsky.","هايمز لا تشومسكي"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.meth.tblt":{"k":"Communicative Approach","t":["Task-Based Language Teaching","التعليم القائم على المهام"],"body":[["A **task** is an activity in which meaning is primary, there is some gap to close, learners use their own language resources, and there is a clear **non-linguistic outcome** (a plan, a list, a decision). An exercise, by contrast, practises a form for its own sake.","المهمة: المعنى أولاً، وفجوة، ولغة الطالب الخاصة، ونتيجة غير لغوية. أما التمرين فيتدرب على شكل لغوي."],["Jane Willis (1996) proposed a widely used lesson framework, which reverses PPP: learners use language first and study form last.","إطار ويليس يعكس ترتيب PPP: الاستخدام أولاً والقاعدة أخيراً."]],"pts":[["Who's who: **N. S. Prabhu** (the Bangalore project in India, 1979–84, a procedural syllabus of tasks), **Michael Long** (task-based syllabus, focus on form), **Jane Willis** (lesson framework), **David Nunan** and **Rod Ellis** (task design and research).","برابو (مشروع بنغالور)، لونغ، ويليس، نونان، إليس"]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Pre-task","ar":"⁧قبل المهمة⁩","items":[{"n":"Topic & task","ar":"⁧تقديم الموضوع والمفردات المفيدة⁩"}]},{"n":"Task cycle","ar":"⁧دورة المهمة⁩","items":[{"n":"Task → planning → report","ar":"⁧إنجاز ثم تحضير التقرير ثم عرضه⁩"}]},{"n":"Language focus","ar":"⁧التركيز اللغوي⁩","items":[{"n":"Analysis & practice","ar":"⁧تحليل اللغة المستخدمة وتدريبها⁩"}]}]},"ex":[["Grade 9 groups plan a two-day visit to AlUla: they read short web texts, agree on a schedule and present a brochure. After the presentations, the teacher focuses on 'going to' and 'will' that groups used.","مهمة بنتيجة حقيقية (كتيّب رحلة) ثم تركيز لغوي بعدها: نموذج ويليس."]],"traps":[["TBLT grew out of the communicative approach.","نشأ من التواصلي"],["Posters, brochures and pamphlet projects are described as task-based learning.","الملصقات والنشرات = بالمهام"],["Problem-based learning = small groups solving a real-world problem.","التعلم بحل المشكلات"]],"src":"Willis, A Framework for Task-Based Learning; Richards & Rodgers; Ellis, Task-based Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.meth.cbi":{"k":"Integrated teaching","t":["Content-Based Instruction and CLIL","التعلم بالمحتوى ودمج المحتوى واللغة"],"body":[["In **CBI**, learners acquire language while studying meaningful content. Common models (Brinton, Snow & Wesche, 1989) are **theme-based** (a language course organised around topics), **sheltered** (subject teaching adapted for language learners) and **adjunct** (a language course linked to a content course).","التعلم بالمحتوى: نماذج موضوعية، ومحمية، ومساندة."],["**CLIL** (Content and Language Integrated Learning) is the European term for teaching a subject through a foreign language with dual aims. Coyle's **4Cs** describe its planning: content, communication, cognition and culture.","CLIL: تدريس مادة بلغة أجنبية بهدفين، وتخطيطها بالعناصر الأربعة."]],"pts":[["Organising a course around family, work and weather = **theme-based** model.","موضوعات = نموذج موضوعي"],["Competency-based teaching focuses on what learners **can do** with the language.","القائم على الكفايات = ما يستطيع فعله"],["In CBI and CLIL lessons teachers write both content and language objectives.","هدف محتوى وهدف لغة"]],"traps":[["The fourth C of CLIL is **culture** (sometimes 'community') — not 'correction'.","الرابع ثقافة"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Brinton, Snow & Wesche; Coyle, Hood & Marsh, CLIL"},"en.meth.coop":{"k":"Learner-centred teaching","t":["Cooperative Learning","التعلم التعاوني"],"body":[["Cooperative learning organises small groups so that success depends on everyone. Johnson and Johnson identified the elements that turn group work into cooperative learning.","جونسون وجونسون حددا العناصر التي تجعل العمل الجماعي تعاونياً."]],"pts":[["**Positive interdependence**: the group succeeds only if every member does.","اعتماد متبادل إيجابي"],["**Individual accountability**: each member is responsible for a part.","مسؤولية فردية"],["**Face-to-face interaction**, **social skills** and **group processing** (reflecting on how the group worked).","تفاعل مباشر ومهارات اجتماعية ومراجعة أداء المجموعة"],["Names: **Jigsaw** was designed by Elliot **Aronson** (1971); **Think-Pair-Share** by Frank **Lyman** (1981).","جيكسو لأرونسون، وفكّر-زاوج-شارك لليمان"]],"ex":[["**Think-Pair-Share**: think alone (30 seconds) → discuss with a partner → share with the class.","فكّر، ناقش زميلك، شارك الصف"],["**Jigsaw**: home group → expert group on one part → back to teach the home group.","جيكسو: مجموعة أم ← مجموعة خبراء ← رجوع للتعليم"]],"traps":[["Group work in itself does not guarantee cooperation; roles and accountability make it work.","الأدوار والمسؤولية تصنع التعاون"],["Pair work best fosters respect and friendship; group activities enrich **interpersonal** skills.","مهارات بين شخصية لا ذاتية"]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods; Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.meth.postmethod":{"k":"Modern trends","t":["Post-method Pedagogy and Eclecticism","ما بعد الطريقة والانتقائية"],"body":[["**B. Kumaravadivelu** argued that the search for the best method has ended. Instead of applying a ready-made method, teachers theorise from their practice using three parameters: **particularity** (fit the local context), **practicality** (theory from practice, not only from experts) and **possibility** (awareness of learners' social and political realities).","كومارافاديفيلو: الخصوصية والعملية والإمكان بدل طريقة جاهزة."],["He also proposed **macrostrategies**, such as maximising learning opportunities, facilitating negotiated interaction, promoting learner autonomy and raising cultural consciousness.","واستراتيجيات كبرى مثل تعظيم فرص التعلم وتعزيز الاستقلالية."]],"pts":[["**Eclecticism** = selecting the most suitable techniques from several methods for a purpose (principled, not random).","انتقائية مبدئية لا عشوائية"],["**Dogme** (Thornbury): materials-light, conversation-driven teaching.","دوغمي: محادثة بأقل مواد"],["**Flipped classroom**: input at home, active practice in class — popularised by Bergmann & Sams.","الفصل المقلوب: برغمان وسامز"]],"traps":[["Eclectic teachers pick the **most suitable**, not the easiest or newest, techniques.","الأنسب لا الأسهل"],["Particularity, practicality, possibility = post-method (Kumaravadivelu).","المعايير الثلاثة لما بعد الطريقة"]],"src":"Kumaravadivelu, Beyond Methods; Brown, Teaching by Principles; Richards & Rodgers"},"en.meth.critical":{"k":"Thinking skills","t":["Critical Thinking in the Language Class","التفكير الناقد في حصة اللغة"],"body":[["Critical thinking is reasoned judgment: analysing claims, weighing evidence and reaching a justified conclusion. It sits at the upper levels of Bloom's taxonomy (analyse, evaluate, create), so activities that only require recall or copying do not develop it.","حكم مبرر بالأدلة، ويقع في المستويات العليا من بلوم."]],"pts":[["**Socratic method**: a sequence of probing questions that leads learners to discover ideas.","الطريقة السقراطية: أسئلة متتابعة"],["Useful prompts: 'Why?', 'How do you know?', 'What is an alternative conclusion?', 'Which is better and why?'","أسئلة لماذا وكيف تعرف وما البديل"],["Debates, problem-solving tasks and comparing two texts on the same issue build it.","المناظرة وحل المشكلات ومقارنة النصوص"]],"traps":[["Memorising, translating and copying are not critical thinking.","الحفظ والترجمة والنسخ ليست تفكيراً ناقداً"],["Critical thinking = **evaluating propositions**.","تقييم القضايا"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Anderson & Krathwohl, A Taxonomy for Learning, Teaching and Assessing"},"en.assess.terms":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Test, Assessment, Evaluation","الاختبار والتقويم والتقييم"],"body":[["Think of three circles. The smallest is the **test**: a prepared instrument, given at a set time, that produces a score. Around it is **assessment**: every way a teacher gathers information about learning, formal or informal. The largest is **evaluation**: judging the value of a programme, course or process and making decisions about it.","ثلاث دوائر: الاختبار داخل التقويم، والتقويم داخل التقييم."]],"pts":[["Test = formal procedure; measurement = assigning numbers.","الاختبار إجراء رسمي"],["Assessment = ongoing; includes observation, portfolios, questioning.","التقويم مستمر"],["Evaluation = merit and worth of a programme; course-evaluation surveys are an **indirect** source.","التقييم حكم على البرنامج"]],"ex":[["During a term Mr. Fahad gives two quizzes (tests), observes speaking and checks notebooks (assessment). At the end, the department reviews results and student surveys to decide whether to keep the new reading course (evaluation).","اختبارات + ملاحظة = تقويم، والحكم على المقرر نفسه = تقييم."]],"traps":[["'Judges the value or worth of a programme' = evaluation.","قيمة البرنامج = تقييم"],["Not every assessment is a test.","ليس كل تقويم اختباراً"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers"},"en.assess.formative":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Formative, Summative and Diagnostic","التكويني والختامي والتشخيصي"],"body":[["Formative assessment is assessment **for** learning: evidence is gathered during instruction and used to adjust teaching and give feedback. Black and Wiliam's research showed that this everyday use of evidence raises achievement. Summative assessment is assessment **of** learning: it reports achievement at the end. A third label, assessment **as** learning, describes learners monitoring their own progress through self-assessment.","التكويني للتعلم، والختامي للحكم على التعلم، والتقويم كتعلم يعني أن الطالب يراقب تقدمه بنفسه."]],"pts":[["Formative examples: questioning, observation, exit tickets, pop quizzes, drafts with feedback.","أمثلة التكويني"],["Summative examples: end-of-unit test, final exam, end-of-term project grade.","أمثلة الختامي"],["Diagnostic: before instruction, to plan teaching and remedial work.","التشخيصي قبل التدريس"],["Informal assessment (e.g. watching a role-play) is unplanned and usually ungraded.","غير الرسمي: ملاحظة بلا درجة"]],"traps":[["The same quiz can be formative or summative — it depends on how the results are **used**.","العبرة باستخدام النتيجة"],["Assessment for learning = formative.","التقويم من أجل التعلم = تكويني"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Black & Wiliam, Inside the Black Box"},"en.assess.purpose":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Test Types by Purpose","أنواع الاختبارات حسب الغرض"],"body":[["Hughes and Brown classify tests by the information they provide. The key distinction: some tests are tied to a course (achievement, progress), others are not (proficiency, aptitude).","بعضها مرتبط بمقرر (التحصيلي والمرحلي) وبعضها لا (الكفاءة والاستعداد)."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Not course-based","ar":"⁧غير مرتبطة بمقرر⁩","items":[{"n":"Proficiency","ar":"⁧قدرة عامة الآن⁩","kw":["admission"]},{"n":"Aptitude","ar":"⁧تنبؤ بالنجاح مستقبلاً⁩","kw":["MLAT"]}]},{"n":"Course-based","ar":"⁧مرتبطة بمقرر⁩","items":[{"n":"Achievement","ar":"⁧نهاية المقرر⁩","kw":["final"]},{"n":"Progress","ar":"⁧ما دُرّس مؤخراً⁩","kw":["during the course"]}]},{"n":"For decisions","ar":"⁧لاتخاذ قرار تعليمي⁩","items":[{"n":"Placement","ar":"⁧الصف المناسب⁩","kw":["homogeneous"]},{"n":"Diagnostic","ar":"⁧نقاط القوة والضعف⁩","kw":["remedial"]}]}]},"pts":[["Proficiency example: checking that a student's English is good enough for a university major.","مثال الكفاءة: القبول في تخصص جامعي"],["Achievement = a particular course; progress = recently taught material; aptitude = predicts success in learning a language.","تحصيلي ومرحلي واستعداد"]],"traps":[["Proficiency = present general ability; aptitude = potential to learn.","الكفاءة حاضر، والاستعداد إمكانية"],["Placement groups learners into **similar** (homogeneous) levels.","التحديد يصنع مجموعات متقاربة"]],"src":"Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers; Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment"},"en.assess.nrt":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Norm-Referenced vs Criterion-Referenced","معياري المرجع ومحكّي المرجع"],"body":[["A **norm-referenced** test interprets each score in relation to the group: percentile ranks, the mean and the spread matter, and items are chosen to spread students out. A **criterion-referenced** test interprets each score against a fixed standard or objective: has the learner reached the required level or not?","المعياري يقارن بالمجموعة ويهتم بالترتيب، والمحكّي يقارن بمعيار ثابت ويهتم بالإتقان."]],"pts":[["NRT question: 'How does she compare with others?'","كيف يقارن بغيره؟"],["CRT question: 'Can she do X?' — most classroom tests linked to objectives are CRTs.","هل يستطيع؟ أغلب اختبارات الصف محكّية"]],"traps":[["A cut-off score or set objective = criterion-referenced.","درجة قطع أو هدف = محكّي"],["'Top 10% get the scholarship' = norm-referenced interpretation.","أعلى 10% = معياري"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment"},"en.assess.discrete":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Discrete-Point vs Integrative Testing","اختبار العناصر المنفصلة والاختبار التكاملي"],"body":[["**Discrete-point** testing (associated with Lado and structuralism) breaks language into separate elements — sounds, words, structures — and tests each one in isolation. **Integrative** testing (associated with Oller) argues that language ability is more than the sum of parts, so tasks should require several components at once.","المنفصل يجزّئ اللغة ويختبر كل عنصر وحده، والتكاملي يرى اللغة كلاً لا يتجزأ."]],"pts":[["Discrete-point: choose 'much/many', one tense per item, minimal pairs.","اختيار much أو many"],["Integrative: cloze, dictation, essay, oral interview.","الفراغات والإملاء والمقال والمقابلة"],["**Cloze**: words deleted at regular intervals (e.g. every 7th word) or selected words.","حذف منتظم للكلمات"]],"traps":[["A cloze test is integrative, even though it is scored objectively.","الفراغات تكاملي وموضوعي التصحيح"],["The best single task for integrated skills = an essay.","المقال للمهارات المتكاملة"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers"},"en.assess.principles":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Brown's Five Principles of Assessment","مبادئ براون الخمسة"],"body":[["Every test is a compromise between these principles. A one-to-one interview may be highly authentic and valid for speaking, but less practical and harder to make reliable; a multiple-choice test is practical and reliable but less authentic.","كل اختبار موازنة بين المبادئ: المقابلة أصيلة لكنها أقل عملية، والاختيار من متعدد عملي لكنه أقل أصالة."]],"pts":[["**Practicality**: time, cost, administration and scoring.","العملية"],["**Reliability**: consistent and dependable results.","الثبات"],["**Validity**: measures what it intends to measure — the most important.","الصدق — الأهم"],["**Authenticity**: tasks like real-world language use.","الأصالة"],["**Washback**: the effect on teaching and learning.","الأثر الرجعي"]],"traps":[["A school chooses a test because it is cheap and quick to score = practicality.","رخيص وسريع = العملية"],["Tasks resembling real-world use = authenticity.","تشبه الواقع = الأصالة"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment"},"en.assess.washback":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Washback (Backwash)","الأثر الرجعي"],"body":[["Washback is the influence a test has on what happens in the classroom: what teachers teach, how they teach, and how learners study. Hughes calls it **backwash**. It can be **positive** (beneficial) — a test of real communication encourages communicative teaching — or **negative** — a grammar-only exam pushes teachers to drill grammar and ignore speaking.","تأثير الاختبار على ما يحدث في الصف، وقد يكون إيجابياً أو سلبياً."]],"pts":[["To get positive washback: test the skills you want to encourage, use direct tests, base tests on objectives and make sure students know the criteria.","اختبر ما تريد تشجيعه وبطريقة مباشرة"],["High-stakes tests produce the strongest washback.","الاختبارات المصيرية أقوى أثراً"],["Feedback that helps learners improve is part of washback at classroom level.","التغذية الراجعة جزء من الأثر"]],"traps":[["'The impact of testing on teaching and learning, positive or negative' = washback — not validity or reliability.","أثر الاختبار على التدريس = الأثر الرجعي لا الصدق ولا الثبات"]],"src":"Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers; Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment"},"en.assess.cefr":{"k":"Assessment","t":["The CEFR Levels","مستويات الإطار الأوروبي المرجعي"],"body":[["The Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), published by the Council of Europe in 2001 and updated by the Companion Volume in 2020, describes proficiency in any language with six levels and can-do descriptors. It is a framework of reference: tests, courses and textbooks are **aligned** to it.","إطار مرجعي من مجلس أوروبا تُوائَم عليه الاختبارات والمقررات."]],"pts":[["**A1**: understands and uses very basic everyday phrases; introduces self; interacts simply if the partner speaks slowly. **A2**: simple, routine exchanges on familiar matters (family, shopping, local area).","A1 عبارات بسيطة، A2 مواقف روتينية"],["**B1**: main points of clear standard input on familiar topics; copes with most travel situations; simple connected text; brief reasons and opinions. **B2**: main ideas of complex texts; fluency and spontaneity; explains a viewpoint with advantages and disadvantages.","B1 النقاط الرئيسية، B2 طلاقة وتلقائية"],["**C1**: demanding, longer texts and implicit meaning; flexible use for social, academic and professional purposes. **C2**: understands virtually everything with ease; summarises from different sources; fine shades of meaning.","C1 مرونة، C2 إتقان شبه كامل"],["Descriptors are positive **can-do statements**, reflecting the **action-oriented approach** (learners as social agents).","عبارات إيجابية والمنهج الموجه للفعل"],["The Companion Volume added **mediation** descriptors and a Pre-A1 level, and dropped the 'native speaker' benchmark.","أضاف الوساطة وما قبل A1"],["Uses: aligning curricula and tests to common levels, writing objectives, learner self-assessment (e.g. the European Language Portfolio).","المواءمة والأهداف والتقييم الذاتي"]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Basic user","ar":"⁧مستخدم أساسي⁩","items":[{"n":"A1","ar":"⁧عبارات يومية بسيطة جداً⁩"},{"n":"A2","ar":"⁧تبادلات روتينية مألوفة⁩"}]},{"n":"Independent user","ar":"⁧مستخدم مستقل⁩","items":[{"n":"B1","ar":"⁧النقاط الرئيسية ومواقف السفر⁩"},{"n":"B2","ar":"⁧نصوص معقدة وطلاقة وعفوية⁩"}]},{"n":"Proficient user","ar":"⁧مستخدم متمكن⁩","items":[{"n":"C1","ar":"⁧نصوص طويلة ومعانٍ ضمنية⁩"},{"n":"C2","ar":"⁧يفهم كل شيء تقريباً بسهولة⁩"}]}]},"traps":[["The CEFR is not a test.","ليس اختباراً"],["B1–B2 = independent user; the 2020 update added **mediation** descriptors.","B = مستقل، والتحديث أضاف الوساطة"]],"src":"Council of Europe, CEFR (2001) and Companion Volume (2020)"},"en.assess.alt":{"k":"Assessment","t":["Alternative Assessment: Portfolios, Self and Peer","التقويم البديل: ملف الإنجاز والتقويم الذاتي والأقران"],"body":[["**Alternative** (performance-based) assessment collects evidence from real work over time instead of a single timed test: portfolios, journals, conferences, observation, projects, and self- and peer-assessment. It is strong on **authenticity** and **washback** and gives rich feedback; its weak points are **practicality** (time) and **reliability** of scoring, which clear criteria and rubrics improve.","يجمع أدلة من عمل حقيقي عبر الزمن بدل اختبار واحد: قوي في الأصالة والأثر الرجعي، وأضعف في العملية والثبات، والمعايير الواضحة تعالج ذلك."]],"pts":[["**Portfolio**: a purposeful collection of a learner's work showing **progress over time**, usually with the learner's own reflections.","ملف الإنجاز: عمل مختار يُظهر التقدم مع تأمل الطالب"],["**Self-assessment** builds autonomy and awareness of criteria (assessment **as** learning); **peer assessment** adds an audience and needs training and a checklist.","الذاتي يبني الاستقلالية، والأقران يحتاج تدريباً وقائمة معايير"],["**Conferences** and **journals**: one-to-one talks and written reflections that guide the next step.","المقابلات الفردية واليوميات"],["Portfolios suit programmes with flexible outcomes.","يناسب البرامج ذات النواتج المرنة"]],"traps":[["Portfolios and projects = alternative / performance-based assessment, not standardised testing.","ملف الإنجاز = تقويم بديل"],["Main limitation of self- and peer-assessment: subjectivity (lower reliability).","عيبه الذاتية"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Genesee & Upshur, Classroom-based Evaluation in Second Language Education"},"en.assess.validity":{"k":"Test quality","t":["Types of Validity","أنواع الصدق"],"body":[["Validity is about the meaning and use of scores. Modern testing (Messick) treats it as one unified idea — construct validity — supported by different kinds of evidence. Teacher-education texts still use the traditional labels below.","الصدق يتعلق بمعنى الدرجة واستخدامها، والتسميات التقليدية أدناه هي الشائعة في كتب إعداد المعلم."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Content","ar":"⁧صدق المحتوى⁩","items":[{"n":"Sampling","ar":"⁧يمثّل محتوى المقرر وأهدافه⁩","kw":["table of specifications"]}]},{"n":"Criterion-related","ar":"⁧الصدق المرتبط بمحك⁩","items":[{"n":"Concurrent","ar":"⁧يتفق مع مقياس آخر في نفس الوقت⁩"},{"n":"Predictive","ar":"⁧يتنبأ بالأداء المستقبلي⁩"}]},{"n":"Construct","ar":"⁧صدق البناء⁩","items":[{"n":"The ability itself","ar":"⁧يقيس المفهوم النظري لا غيره⁩","kw":["reading speed ≠ reading"]}]},{"n":"Face","ar":"⁧الصدق الظاهري⁩","items":[{"n":"Looks right","ar":"⁧يبدو مناسباً للمختبَرين⁩"}]}]},"traps":[["Table of specifications → content validity.","جدول المواصفات = صدق المحتوى"],["A placement test that predicts later success → predictive validity.","يتنبأ = تنبؤي"],["A listening test that depends on memory → weak construct validity.","يعتمد على الذاكرة = ضعف صدق البناء"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers"},"en.assess.reliability":{"k":"Test quality","t":["Types of Reliability","أنواع الثبات"],"body":[["Reliability estimates differ in what source of inconsistency they check: time, test version, the items themselves, or the people scoring.","كل نوع يفحص مصدراً مختلفاً لعدم الاتساق: الزمن، أو النسخة، أو الأسئلة، أو المصحح."]],"pts":[["**Test–retest**: same test, same learners, two occasions.","إعادة الاختبار"],["**Parallel (alternate) forms**: two equivalent versions.","الصور المتكافئة"],["**Internal consistency**: split-half, Cronbach's alpha.","الاتساق الداخلي"],["**Inter-rater**: different raters agree; **intra-rater**: one rater is consistent over time.","بين المصححين وداخل المصحح"],["Sources of unreliability: the test (ambiguous or too few items), conditions (noise, timing), the student (illness, anxiety) and especially the **rater** — hence rubrics and two markers for writing and speaking.","الاختبار والظروف والطالب والمصحح"]],"ex":[["A bathroom scale that always shows 2 kg too much is reliable (consistent) but not valid (wrong).","ميزان يزيد دائماً 2 كجم: ثابت لكنه غير صادق"]],"traps":[["A test can be reliable without being valid, but cannot be valid without being reliable.","ثابت بلا صدق ممكن، والعكس لا"],["One teacher marking the same essay differently in October and December → intra-rater problem.","نفس المصحح في وقتين = داخل المصحح"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers"},"en.assess.rubrics":{"k":"Scoring","t":["Analytic vs Holistic Rubrics","السلم التحليلي والشمولي"],"body":[["Both types describe levels of performance, but they differ in what the learner gets back. **Holistic** scoring gives one impression score for the whole performance: quick, good for large numbers, but it hides strengths and weaknesses. **Analytic** scoring rates each criterion separately: slower, but diagnostic and more useful for teaching.","الشمولي سريع ويعطي درجة واحدة، والتحليلي أبطأ لكنه تشخيصي ومفيد للتعليم."]],"pts":[["Main benefit of sharing a rubric beforehand: it makes the **criteria clear** to students.","وضوح المعايير"],["Co-constructing rubrics helps learners see assessment as a learning tool.","بناؤه مع الطلاب"],["Checklist (present/absent) → rating scale (degree) → rubric (described levels).","من القائمة إلى الروبرك"]],"traps":[["Limitation of holistic scoring: no explicit feedback on specific areas.","عيب الشمولي"],["Co-constructing a rubric does not mean lowering standards.","ليس تسهيلاً للمعايير"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Weigle, Assessing Writing"},"en.assess.items":{"k":"Test analysis","t":["Item Facility, Discrimination and Distractors","السهولة والتمييز والبدائل"],"body":[["**Item facility (IF)** = number correct ÷ number of test-takers. 0.90 is very easy, 0.20 very hard; items roughly between 0.15 and 0.85 are usually kept.","السهولة = عدد الصحيح ÷ عدد المختبَرين."],["**Item discrimination (ID)** compares a high-scoring group with a low-scoring group: (high correct − low correct) ÷ (½ × total in both groups). It ranges from −1 to +1; higher positive values are better.","التمييز يقارن مجموعة عليا بدنيا، ويتراوح بين −1 و+1."]],"pts":[["Negative ID: low scorers do better → faulty item: revise or remove.","سالب = معيب"],["A distractor chosen by nobody is not working.","بديل لا يختاره أحد = لا يعمل"],["A distractor that attracts many high scorers may be ambiguous or a second key.","بديل يجذب الأقوياء = غامض"]],"ex":[["Item 7: 9 of 10 high scorers and 3 of 10 low scorers answered correctly. ID = (9 − 3) ÷ 10 = 0.6, and IF = 12 ÷ 20 = 0.6 — an item of medium difficulty that discriminates well.","التمييز 0.6 والسهولة 0.6 — سؤال متوسط الصعوبة ويميّز جيداً."]],"traps":[["IF is also called the difficulty index — but a higher IF means an **easier** item.","معامل أعلى = سؤال أسهل"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers"},"en.assess.feedback":{"k":"Feedback","t":["Effective Feedback","التغذية الراجعة الفعالة"],"body":[["Hattie and Timperley describe feedback as answering three questions: **Where am I going?** (the goal), **How am I going?** (progress) and **Where to next?** (feed forward). Feedback about the task, the process and self-regulation helps; praise directed at the person ('You're clever') does little for learning.","ثلاثة أسئلة: إلى أين أذهب؟ كيف أتقدم؟ ما الخطوة التالية؟ والمديح الشخصي قليل الفائدة."]],"pts":[["Effective: timely, specific, clear, focused, balanced, impersonal, linked to objectives.","صفات الفعالة"],["**Content** feedback (ideas) vs **form** feedback (accuracy).","المحتوى مقابل الشكل"],["Feedback informs both students and teachers.","يفيد الطالب والمعلم"],["Constructive alignment (Biggs): objectives, activities and assessment match.","المواءمة البنّاءة"]],"traps":[["'Correct every mistake' is not effective feedback and can demotivate.","تصحيح كل خطأ يُحبط"],["Long-term achievement is encouraged through **self-evaluation**.","التقييم الذاتي"]],"src":"Hattie & Timperley, The Power of Feedback; Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment"},"en.assess.direct":{"k":"Testing skills","t":["Direct vs Indirect Testing","الاختبار المباشر وغير المباشر"],"body":[["Hughes recommends **direct** testing whenever possible for productive skills: if we want to know how well someone writes, we ask them to write. Indirect tests can be more reliable and practical, but their link to the real skill is weaker, and they can produce negative washback.","هيوز يفضّل المباشر للمهارات الإنتاجية: من أراد معرفة الكتابة طلب الكتابة."]],"pts":[["Writing: clear prompt (purpose, audience, length), rubric, more than one task.","الكتابة: تعليمات واضحة وروبرك"],["Speaking: interview, picture description, role-play, information gap; two raters; record.","الكلام: مقابلة ولعب أدوار ومصححان"],["Reading/listening: items that need the text — information transfer, matching, short answers.","القراءة والاستماع: أسئلة تتطلب النص"],["Listening: avoid items that test memory or reading more than listening; reading: items should not be answerable from general knowledge.","لا تختبر الذاكرة ولا المعرفة العامة"],["Grammar & vocabulary: gap-fill, multiple choice, sentence transformation, word formation, C-test.","القواعد والمفردات"]],"traps":[["Choosing the error in a sentence to judge writing = indirect.","اكتشاف الخطأ = غير مباشر"],["Writing an email invitation = direct.","كتابة رسالة = مباشر"]],"src":"Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers; Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment"},"en.obj.goals":{"k":"Objectives","t":["Goals, Objectives and Outcomes","الغايات والأهداف والنواتج"],"body":[["The three terms move from general to specific. **Goals** express broad, long-term intentions for a course or programme. **Objectives** state, for a unit or lesson, exactly what learners will do. **Learning outcomes** state what learners should be able to do after the instruction.","من العام إلى الخاص: الغاية ← الهدف ← الناتج."]],"pts":[["Goals: general, long-term, not directly measurable.","عامة وطويلة المدى"],["Objectives: specific, observable, measurable, short-term.","محددة وقابلة للقياس"],["Setting clear objectives is the first planning step (fuller course-design models put needs analysis before it).","وضع الأهداف أول خطوة في التخطيط، ويسبقها تحليل الاحتياجات في النماذج الموسعة"]],"traps":[["'Students will understand the plot' = a goal (understand cannot be observed).","يفهم = غاية"],["Goals are **more general** than objectives.","الغايات أعم"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Richards, Curriculum Development in Language Teaching"},"en.obj.smart":{"k":"Objectives","t":["SMART, ABCD and Mager","صيغ كتابة الهدف"],"body":[["Three popular frameworks express the same idea — an objective must be precise enough to check.","ثلاث صيغ لفكرة واحدة: هدف دقيق يمكن التحقق منه."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"SMART","ar":"⁧خمس صفات⁩","items":[{"n":"S-M-A-R-T","ar":"⁧محدد، قابل للقياس، ممكن، ملائم، محدد بزمن⁩","kw":["time-bound"]}]},{"n":"ABCD","ar":"⁧أربعة عناصر⁩","items":[{"n":"A-B-C-D","ar":"⁧الجمهور، السلوك، الشرط، الدرجة⁩","kw":["degree"]}]},{"n":"Mager","ar":"⁧ثلاثة عناصر⁩","items":[{"n":"P-C-C","ar":"⁧الأداء، الشرط، المعيار⁩","kw":["criterion"]}]}]},"pts":[["Good verbs: identify, list, distinguish, compare, write, pronounce, use.","أفعال جيدة"],["Avoid: know, understand, appreciate, learn, be familiar with, be aware of.","أفعال تُتجنب"],["An objective describes the learner, not the teacher: NOT 'The teacher will teach…'.","الهدف عن الطالب لا المعلم"]],"ex":[["'Given a map (condition), students will describe the route to school (performance) with no more than two errors (criterion).'","الشرط والأداء والمعيار في جملة واحدة"]],"traps":[["'With at least four correct' = criterion (Mager) / degree (ABCD).","معيار أو درجة"],["Conditions of good objectives: all **except** general/broad.","ما عدا عام"]],"src":"Mager, Preparing Instructional Objectives; Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.obj.bloom":{"k":"Objectives","t":["Bloom's Taxonomy: Original vs Revised","تصنيف بلوم: الأصلي والمعدّل"],"body":[["Benjamin Bloom and colleagues published the cognitive taxonomy in 1956. In 2001 Lorin Anderson and David Krathwohl revised it: levels became verbs, the two top levels swapped places, and a second dimension — types of knowledge — was added.","بلوم 1956، ثم أندرسون وكراثول 2001: أفعال بدل الأسماء وتبديل القمة وإضافة بُعد المعرفة."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Original 1956","ar":"⁧الأصلي⁩","items":[{"n":"6 nouns","ar":"⁧معرفة ← فهم ← تطبيق ← تحليل ← تركيب ← تقييم⁩","kw":["evaluation = top"]}]},{"n":"Revised 2001","ar":"⁧المعدّل⁩","items":[{"n":"6 verbs","ar":"⁧يتذكر ← يفهم ← يطبق ← يحلل ← يقيّم ← يبتكر⁩","kw":["create = top"]}]}]},"pts":[["Lower-order: remember, understand, apply. Higher-order (HOTS): analyze, evaluate, create.","دنيا وعليا"],["Knowledge dimension: factual, conceptual, procedural, metacognitive.","بُعد المعرفة الأربعة"],["Synthesis (1956) ≈ create (2001): combining parts into a new whole.","التركيب يقابل الابتكار"],["Order: original knowledge → comprehension → application → analysis → synthesis → evaluation; revised remember → understand → apply → analyze → evaluate → create.","ترتيب المستويات في النسختين"]],"ex":[["One text, three levels: 'List the places Ali visited' (remember) → 'Compare Ali's trip with yours' (analyze) → 'Design a one-day tour of Jeddah for a visitor' (create).","نفس النص بثلاثة مستويات: تذكر ← تحليل ← ابتكار."]],"traps":[["Highest level: **evaluation** (original) vs **create** (revised) — read which version is meant.","انتبه للنسخة"],["In the 2001 document, 'classifying' and 'comparing' are listed under *understand*; most teacher verb lists put compare/contrast under analysis. Judge by the options given.","التصنيف والمقارنة يختلف موضعهما حسب القائمة"]],"src":"Anderson & Krathwohl, A Taxonomy for Learning, Teaching and Assessing; Bloom et al., Taxonomy of Educational Objectives"},"en.obj.verbs":{"k":"Objectives","t":["Bloom's Levels: Typical Verbs and Tasks","أفعال مستويات بلوم ومهامها"],"body":[["To place an objective or question on a level, look at the **thinking the task demands**, not only the verb. The lists below follow the revised taxonomy (Anderson & Krathwohl, 2001); the names in brackets are the 1956 terms.","المستوى يُحدد بنوع التفكير الذي تتطلبه المهمة لا بالفعل وحده."]],"pts":[["**Remember** (knowledge): list, name, define, recall, match — 'Name three manners of articulation.'","يتذكر: يعدّد، يسمّي، يعرّف"],["**Understand** (comprehension): explain, summarize, paraphrase, retell, give examples — 'Explain why the character felt sad.'","يفهم: يشرح، يلخّص، يعيد الصياغة"],["**Apply** (application): use, solve, complete, demonstrate — 'Use the past simple to describe your weekend.'","يطبّق: يستخدم، يحل، ينفّذ"],["**Analyze** (analysis): distinguish, compare, contrast, categorize, outline — 'Distinguish facts from opinions in the advert.'","يحلّل: يميّز، يقارن، يصنّف"],["**Evaluate** (evaluation): judge, justify, critique, assess, recommend — 'Which ending is better? Justify your choice.'","يقيّم: يحكم، يبرّر، ينقد"],["**Create** (synthesis): design, compose, plan, produce, invent — 'Design an advertisement for a new app.'","يبتكر: يصمم، يؤلف، يخطط"]],"traps":[["'Explain a character's feelings' = understand, not evaluate.","يشرح = فهم"],["'List' is lower-order; 'plan', 'assess' and 'design' are higher-order.","يعدّد أدنى، ويخطط ويقيّم ويصمم أعلى"],["Combining ideas into a new whole = synthesis (1956) / create (2001).","دمج الأفكار في كلّ جديد = تركيب أو ابتكار"]],"src":"Anderson & Krathwohl, A Taxonomy for Learning, Teaching and Assessing; Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.obj.domains":{"k":"Objectives","t":["Three Domains of Objectives","مجالات الأهداف الثلاثة"],"body":[["Each domain has its own ladder of levels, from simple to complex.","لكل مجال سلّم مستويات من البسيط إلى المعقد."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Cognitive","ar":"⁧المعرفي — Bloom⁩","items":[{"n":"Thinking","ar":"⁧من التذكر إلى الابتكار⁩","kw":["describe","analyze"]}]},{"n":"Affective","ar":"⁧الوجداني — Krathwohl⁩","items":[{"n":"Values","ar":"⁧استقبال، استجابة، تقدير القيمة، تنظيم، الاتّسام بالقيمة⁩","kw":["attitudes"]}]},{"n":"Psychomotor","ar":"⁧النفسحركي — Simpson / Dave⁩","items":[{"n":"Skills","ar":"⁧دايف: تقليد، معالجة، دقة، ترابط، تطبّع⁩","kw":["imitation","naturalisation"]}]}]},"pts":[["Simpson's levels: perception, set, guided response, mechanism, complex overt response, adaptation, origination.","مستويات سمبسون السبعة"],["An 'attitudinal' objective (develop a critical attitude) belongs to the affective domain.","هدف اتجاهات = وجداني"]],"traps":[["Affective domain = Krathwohl (not Bloom alone, not Dave).","الوجداني = كراثول"],["'Performs an experiment' = psychomotor; 'describes a topic' = cognitive.","يجري تجربة = نفسحركي"]],"src":"Krathwohl, Bloom & Masia, Taxonomy: Affective Domain; Anderson & Krathwohl; Simpson; Dave"},"en.obj.terminal":{"k":"Objectives","t":["Terminal and Enabling Objectives","الأهداف النهائية والتمكينية"],"body":[["Unit (module) objectives describe what learners will achieve by the end of a unit; lesson objectives break them into smaller steps for each lesson.","أهداف الوحدة تتفرع إلى أهداف دروس."],["A **terminal objective** describes the final performance expected at the end of a unit or course. **Enabling objectives** are the smaller, prerequisite skills and knowledge learners need to reach it, usually spread across lessons. Planning the enabling steps from the terminal objective is a form of backward design.","النهائي هو الأداء الختامي، والتمكينية خطوات لازمة للوصول إليه، وتخطيطها من النهائي تصميم عكسي."]],"ex":[["Terminal: 'write an 80-word email inviting a friend'. Enabling: use 'Would you like to…?'; write dates and times; open and close an email politely.","مثال الدعوة بالبريد"]],"traps":[["'By the end of the unit, students will…' usually signals a terminal (unit-level) objective.","بنهاية الوحدة = نهائي"]],"src":"Mager, Preparing Instructional Objectives; Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.obj.criteria":{"k":"Objectives","t":["Learning Intentions and Success Criteria","نوايا التعلم ومعايير النجاح"],"body":[["A **learning intention** tells students what they are learning; **success criteria** tell them how they and the teacher will know they have succeeded. Shared in simple language, they let learners monitor their own work, which is the heart of assessment for learning.","نية التعلم: ماذا نتعلم؟ ومعايير النجاح: كيف نعرف أننا نجحنا؟"]],"pts":[["WALT = We Are Learning To…; WILF = What I'm Looking For…","(WALT, WILF)"],["Can-do statements link lesson objectives to CEFR-style descriptors.","عبارات أستطيع"],["In CLIL/SIOP lessons: one **content** objective + one **language** objective.","هدف محتوى وهدف لغة"],["Share objectives on the board, explain them at the start, refer back during the lesson and check them at the end.","على السبورة وفي البداية والأثناء والنهاية"]],"ex":[["Success criterion: 'I can use *because* to give two reasons.'","معيار نجاح بصيغة «أستطيع»"]],"traps":[["Explaining objectives at the start of a course is **not** meant to show that the curriculum is fixed and suggestions are ignored.","ليس لإغلاق باب الاقتراحات"],["Objectives are written by the teacher and shared before **and** during teaching.","قبل التدريس وأثناءه"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Echevarría, Vogt & Short, Making Content Comprehensible (SIOP)"},"en.obj.solo":{"k":"Objectives","t":["The SOLO Taxonomy (Biggs & Collis)","تصنيف SOLO"],"body":[["SOLO — **Structure of the Observed Learning Outcome** — classifies answers by how well their parts are connected, not by the verb used.","يصنّف الإجابات حسب ترابط أجزائها لا حسب الفعل."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"1","ar":"⁧ما قبل البنية⁩","items":[{"n":"Prestructural","ar":"⁧لا فهم أو إجابة خارج الموضوع⁩"}]},{"n":"2–3","ar":"⁧كمي⁩","items":[{"n":"Unistructural","ar":"⁧فكرة واحدة⁩"},{"n":"Multistructural","ar":"⁧أفكار متعددة غير مترابطة⁩"}]},{"n":"4–5","ar":"⁧نوعي⁩","items":[{"n":"Relational","ar":"⁧أفكار مترابطة في كلّ⁩"},{"n":"Extended abstract","ar":"⁧تعميم ونقل لسياق جديد⁩"}]}]},"traps":[["SOLO has **five** levels.","خمسة مستويات"]],"src":"Biggs & Collis, Evaluating the Quality of Learning: The SOLO Taxonomy"},"en.plan.lessonplan":{"k":"Planning","t":["Levels of Planning and the Lesson Plan","مستويات التخطيط وخطة الدرس"],"body":[["Planning moves from broad to detailed: **curriculum** (goals, content, methods, evaluation) → **syllabus** (course content and sequence) → **scheme of work** (the syllabus spread over a term or year) → **unit plan** → **lesson plan**. A lesson plan is a flexible map, not a script: the teacher adapts it when learners' responses show a need.","من العام إلى التفصيلي: منهج ← مقرر ← خطة فصلية ← خطة وحدة ← خطة درس، والخطة مرنة لا نص يُتلى."]],"pts":[["Components: aims/objectives, materials, stages and procedures, timing, interaction patterns, anticipated problems and solutions, assessment of the objective.","مكونات الخطة"],["Uses: a guide while teaching, a record of what was taught, evidence for an observer, a basis for reflection.","دليل وسجل ودليل للمشرف وأساس للتأمل"],["A **skeleton plan** gives only the general outline; a detailed plan scripts each stage.","الخطة الهيكلية عامة"]],"traps":[["A written plan does **not** show students' actual motivation and performance — that comes from feedback.","الخطة لا تُظهر الدافعية"],["Before planning: learners' level, needs and educational and cultural background; first question: 'What will students achieve?'","خلفية الطلاب والهدف أولاً"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Scrivener, Learning Teaching; Richards, Curriculum Development in Language Teaching"},"en.plan.ppp":{"k":"Lesson shapes","t":["PPP and Other Lesson Shapes","PPP وأشكال الدرس الأخرى"],"body":[["**PPP** (Presentation → Practice → Production) moves from teacher-controlled input to learner-controlled use. It is clear and easy to plan, but critics note that it assumes language is learned in the same order it is presented.","PPP ينتقل من عرض يتحكم فيه المعلم إلى استخدام حر للطالب، وهو واضح لكنه يفترض أن اللغة تُتعلم بترتيب عرضها."],["Alternatives reorder the same elements: **TTT** (Test → Teach → Test) checks what learners know before teaching; Harmer's **ESA** (Engage, Study, Activate) allows flexible sequences; **TBL** puts the task first and the language focus last.","بدائل: اختبار ثم تعليم ثم اختبار، ونموذج ESA لهارمر، والتعلم بالمهام."]],"pts":[["Warm-up / lead-in: introduce the topic and activate prior knowledge.","التهيئة"],["Receptive-skills lessons: pre- → while- → post-.","قبل وأثناء وبعد"],["Wrap-up / closure: summarise and check objectives.","الختام"]],"traps":[["In an effective PPP lesson, presentation comes first and production last.","العرض أولاً"],["Correct order: lead-in → activity → feedback.","تهيئة ← نشاط ← تغذية راجعة"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Scrivener, Learning Teaching"},"en.plan.gradual":{"k":"Sequencing","t":["Gradual Release and Scaffolding","النقل التدريجي والسقالات"],"body":[["Pearson and Gallagher's (1983) **gradual release of responsibility** shifts the load from teacher to learner in steps; Fisher and Frey later set it out as four phases: focused instruction (I do), guided instruction (we do), collaborative work (you do together) and independent work (you do alone). **Scaffolding** (Wood, Bruner & Ross, 1976) is the temporary support used along the way — models, sentence frames, prompts — withdrawn as competence grows.","انتقال المسؤولية من المعلم إلى الطالب على خطوات، مع دعم مؤقت يُسحب تدريجياً."]],"pts":[["Scaffolding connects to Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development.","مرتبط بمنطقة النمو القريبة"],["Sentence starters and graphic organisers are typical scaffolds.","بدايات الجمل والمنظمات"]],"traps":[["Scaffolding is **temporary**; permanent help is not scaffolding.","السقالات مؤقتة"]],"src":"Pearson & Gallagher (1983); Fisher & Frey, Better Learning Through Structured Teaching; Wood, Bruner & Ross (1976)"},"en.plan.backward":{"k":"Planning","t":["Backward Design (Wiggins & McTighe)","التصميم العكسي"],"body":[["In *Understanding by Design*, Wiggins and McTighe argue that teachers often plan activities first and hope they lead somewhere. Backward design reverses this into three stages.","بدل أن نبدأ بالأنشطة، نبدأ بالنتائج المرغوبة."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Stage 1","ar":"⁧النتائج المرغوبة⁩","items":[{"n":"Desired results","ar":"⁧الأهداف والأسئلة الجوهرية⁩","kw":["essential questions"]}]},{"n":"Stage 2","ar":"⁧الأدلة المقبولة⁩","items":[{"n":"Evidence","ar":"⁧مهمة أداء واختبارات وروبرك⁩","kw":["performance task"]}]},{"n":"Stage 3","ar":"⁧خطة التعلم⁩","items":[{"n":"Learning plan","ar":"⁧الأنشطة والتدريس⁩","kw":["activities"]}]}]},"ex":[["A 'tourism' unit: the final task is a class brochure; the lessons recycle the language and skills it needs.","وحدة السياحة تنتهي بكتيّب يصممه الصف"]],"traps":[["Backward design starts with **desired results**, not with activities or materials.","يبدأ بالنتائج"],["Designing the rubric before choosing activities is a sign of backward design.","الروبرك قبل الأنشطة"]],"src":"Wiggins & McTighe, Understanding by Design"},"en.plan.syllabus":{"k":"Syllabus","t":["Synthetic vs Analytic Syllabuses","المنهج التركيبي والتحليلي"],"body":[["David Wilkins (1976) distinguished two broad strategies. In a **synthetic** syllabus, the parts of the language are taught separately and step by step, and the learner's job is to re-synthesise them for use. In an **analytic** syllabus, language is presented in whole, purposeful chunks organised by what learners need to do, and learners analyse the forms within them.","ويلكنز: التركيبي يعلّم الأجزاء منفصلة ليجمعها الطالب، والتحليلي يقدّم كتلاً هادفة ليحللها الطالب."]],"pts":[["**Structural (grammatical)**: grammar items, simple to complex. **Notional-functional**: notions (time, quantity) and functions (requesting, apologising).","قواعد / مفاهيم ووظائف"],["**Situational**: real-life situations (at the airport, at the bank). **Topic/theme-based**: topics (sport, health). **Content-based**: subject-matter content.","مواقف / موضوعات / محتوى"],["**Skills-based**: skills and sub-skills (skimming, listening for gist, writing a paragraph). **Task-based**: tasks learners perform (planning a trip). **Lexical** (Willis): high-frequency words, collocations and chunks.","مهارات / مهام / معجمي"],["Synthetic examples: structural, lexical. Analytic examples: task-based (Prabhu's procedural syllabus, Long), content-based. Wilkins himself proposed the **notional** syllabus as analytic, though later writers (Long & Crookes) argued it works synthetically in practice.","أمثلة التركيبي والتحليلي"],["Most modern coursebooks use a **multi-strand (integrated)** syllabus combining topics, grammar, vocabulary, functions and skills.","متعدد الخيوط"],["Curriculum (goals, content, methods, evaluation) is broader than syllabus (content + sequence).","المنهج أشمل من المقرر"]],"ex":[["A coursebook unit called 'At the airport' with dialogues for check-in and passport control follows a situational strand; its grammar box on 'can / can't' adds a structural strand — a multi-strand syllabus.","موقف المطار مع صندوق قواعد: منهج متعدد الخيوط."]],"traps":[["Grammar items one at a time, then combined = synthetic.","قواعد واحدة واحدة = تركيبي"],["Organised by situations like 'at the bank' = situational.","مواقف = موقفي"]],"src":"Wilkins, Notional Syllabuses; Nunan, Syllabus Design; Richards, Curriculum Development in Language Teaching"},"en.plan.needs":{"k":"Course design","t":["Needs Analysis","تحليل الاحتياجات"],"body":[["Needs analysis collects information about learners to decide what a course should contain. Hutchinson and Waters divide **target needs** into **necessities** (what the situation demands), **lacks** (the gap between what learners know and what they need) and **wants** (what learners themselves feel they need).","ما يتطلبه الموقف، وما ينقص الطالب، وما يريده هو."]],"pts":[["Tools: questionnaires, interviews, placement/diagnostic tests, observation.","أدوات: استبيانات ومقابلات واختبارات"],["Steps: design → conduct → analyse.","تصميم ← تنفيذ ← تحليل"],["Interest inventories are a small classroom version.","استبيان الاهتمامات"]],"traps":[["Surveys before a course = needs assessment, not achievement assessment.","الاستبيان قبل الدورة = احتياجات"]],"src":"Hutchinson & Waters, English for Specific Purposes; Richards, Curriculum Development in Language Teaching"},"en.plan.models":{"k":"Planning models","t":["Gagné, Hunter and 5E","نماذج جانييه وهنتر و5E"],"body":[["Each model answers 'what should happen, in what order?' from a different angle: Gagné from the psychology of learning, Hunter from direct instruction, and 5E from inquiry-based science teaching.","كل نموذج يرتب خطوات الدرس من زاوية مختلفة."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Gagné — 9 events","ar":"⁧أحداث جانييه⁩","items":[{"n":"Attention → transfer","ar":"⁧انتباه، هدف، استدعاء، عرض، توجيه، أداء، تغذية، تقويم، نقل⁩","kw":["nine events"]}]},{"n":"Madeline Hunter","ar":"⁧نموذج هنتر⁩","items":[{"n":"Anticipatory set","ar":"⁧تهيئة، هدف، مدخل ونمذجة، فحص الفهم، تدريب موجّه، إغلاق، تدريب مستقل⁩","kw":["guided practice"]}]},{"n":"5E","ar":"⁧النموذج الاستقصائي⁩","items":[{"n":"Engage → Evaluate","ar":"⁧انخراط، استكشاف، شرح، توسّع، تقويم⁩","kw":["inquiry"]}]}]},"pts":[["**Gagné's nine events**: gain attention, inform learners of the objective, stimulate recall of prior learning, present content, provide guidance, elicit performance, give feedback, assess performance, enhance retention and transfer.","أحداث جانييه التسعة"],["**Madeline Hunter** model: anticipatory set → objective and purpose → input and modelling → checking for understanding → guided practice → closure → independent practice.","نموذج مادلين هنتر"],["**5E model**: Engage → Explore → Explain → Elaborate → Evaluate (inquiry-based).","النموذج الخماسي"]],"traps":[["'Anticipatory set' is Hunter's opening step.","التهيئة الحافزة = هنتر"],["In 5E, students **explore** before the teacher **explains**.","الاستكشاف قبل الشرح"]],"src":"Gagné, The Conditions of Learning; Hunter, Mastery Teaching; Bybee (BSCS 5E)"},"en.plan.icq":{"k":"Classroom language","t":["ICQs and CCQs","أسئلة فحص التعليمات وفهم المعنى"],"body":[["'Do you understand?' almost always gets 'yes', whatever the truth. Scrivener and others recommend short, specific checking questions instead.","سؤال «فاهمين؟» جوابه دائماً نعم، لذلك نسأل أسئلة محددة."]],"pts":[["**ICQs** (instruction-checking questions) check the task: 'Alone or in pairs?', 'Write or speak?', 'How many minutes?'","أسئلة فحص التعليمات"],["**CCQs** (concept-checking questions) check meaning: for 'used to play' → 'Does he play now?' — 'No.'","أسئلة فحص المفهوم"],["Most reliable check before a complex task: ask a student to explain or demonstrate the steps.","اطلب من طالب أن يشرح الخطوات"]],"ex":[["Before a group task: 'Read the text, then agree on three rules for a safe trip. Five minutes.' ICQs: 'Alone or in groups?' — 'Groups.' 'How many rules?' — 'Three.' 'How long?' — 'Five minutes.' Only then are texts handed out.","تعليمات ثم أسئلة فحص التعليمات ثم توزيع الأوراق."]],"traps":[["ICQ = instructions; CCQ = meaning of new language.","التعليمات مقابل المعنى"]],"src":"Scrivener, Learning Teaching; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.plan.manage":{"k":"Classroom management","t":["Seating, Proximity and Rules","الجلوس والاقتراب والقواعد"],"body":[["Good management is mostly preventive: an arrangement that suits the task, routines that students know, and calm responses that keep the lesson moving. Kounin called a teacher's awareness of everything happening in the room **withitness**.","الإدارة الجيدة وقائية: ترتيب مناسب وروتين معروف واستجابة هادئة."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Rows","ar":"⁧صفوف⁩","items":[{"n":"Tests & input","ar":"⁧اختبار فردي أو شرح على السبورة⁩"}]},{"n":"Clusters","ar":"⁧مجموعات⁩","items":[{"n":"Group projects","ar":"⁧نقاش ومواد مشتركة⁩"}]},{"n":"Horseshoe / circle","ar":"⁧حدوة أو دائرة⁩","items":[{"n":"Discussion","ar":"⁧الكل يرى الكل: نقاش ومناظرة⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["Proximity control: move closer to off-task students without stopping the lesson.","الاقتراب دون إيقاف الدرس"],["Rules: few, clear, agreed with students, applied calmly and consistently.","قواعد قليلة واضحة متفق عليها"],["Routines are taught, modelled, practised and reinforced.","الروتين يُعلّم ويُنمذج ويُدرّب"],["**Wait time** (Rowe): pausing 3–5 seconds after a question raises the number and quality of answers.","وقت الانتظار 3–5 ثوانٍ يزيد الإجابات وجودتها"],["Harmer's teacher roles: controller, organiser, assessor, prompter, participant, resource, tutor, observer — chosen to suit the stage.","أدوار المعلم عند هارمر تتغير حسب المرحلة"]],"traps":[["Light, movable furniture suits classes that switch patterns often.","أثاث متحرك"],["Give instructions **before** students move into groups.","التعليمات قبل الحركة"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Scrivener, Classroom Management Techniques; Kounin, Discipline and Group Management in Classrooms"},"en.mat.authentic":{"k":"Materials","t":["Authentic vs Pedagogic Materials","المواد الأصيلة والتعليمية"],"body":[["**Authentic** texts are produced by and for proficient users for real purposes. **Pedagogic** (non-authentic) materials are written for learners: coursebook dialogues, graded readers, worksheets. Both have a place: pedagogic texts control difficulty, authentic texts show real language and culture.","الأصيلة للاستخدام الحقيقي، والتعليمية مكتوبة للمتعلم، ولكلٍّ دوره."]],"pts":[["Advantages of authentic: real language, culture, motivation, prepares for real use.","مزايا الأصيلة"],["Challenges: difficult vocabulary, cultural content, may date quickly.","تحدياتها"],["Grade the task, not the text, for lower levels.","بسّط المهمة لا النص"]],"traps":[["Newspapers and web pages = authentic; textbook dialogue = non-authentic.","الصحيفة أصيلة والكتاب لا"],["Cultural customs (greetings, clothing, housing) are best shown with authentic materials.","الثقافة بالمواد الأصيلة"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Nunan, Designing Tasks for the Communicative Classroom"},"en.mat.adapt":{"k":"Materials","t":["Adapting and Evaluating Materials","تكييف المواد وتقييمها"],"body":[["Adaptation keeps what is good in a coursebook and changes the rest to fit the learners and aims. McDonough and Shaw describe five main techniques; many teacher guides add **replacing**.","التكييف يحتفظ بالجيد ويغيّر الباقي."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Adding","ar":"⁧الإضافة⁩","items":[{"n":"Extending","ar":"⁧المزيد من نفس النوع⁩"},{"n":"Expanding","ar":"⁧عنصر جديد غير موجود⁩"}]},{"n":"Deleting","ar":"⁧الحذف⁩","items":[{"n":"Subtracting","ar":"⁧تقليل الكمية⁩"},{"n":"Abridging","ar":"⁧حذف جزء كامل⁩"}]},{"n":"Modifying","ar":"⁧التعديل⁩","items":[{"n":"Rewriting","ar":"⁧تغيير المحتوى⁩"},{"n":"Restructuring","ar":"⁧تغيير تنظيم الصف⁩"}]},{"n":"Simplify / Reorder","ar":"⁧التبسيط وإعادة الترتيب⁩","items":[{"n":"Simplifying","ar":"⁧لغة أسهل وجمل أقصر⁩"},{"n":"Reordering","ar":"⁧تغيير تسلسل الأنشطة⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["**Replacing**: substituting a text, picture or activity with a more suitable one (e.g. a culturally appropriate Saudi context instead of an unsuitable topic).","الاستبدال بما يناسب"],["Evaluation stages: **pre-use** (predictive, often a checklist such as Cunningsworth's), **in-use** (observation in class) and **post-use** (retrospective, to decide whether to keep the book).","تقييم قبل الاستخدام وأثناءه وبعده"],["**Piloting** = trying the book with a real class before adopting it; the main criterion is whether it serves the **learning goals**.","التجريب في الصف، والمعيار تحقيق الأهداف"]],"traps":[["Six more items of the same type = extending; a new pronunciation stage = expanding.","تمديد مقابل توسيع"],["Doing the vocabulary task before the reading = reordering.","تقديم نشاط = إعادة ترتيب"]],"ex":[["The book's reading text is about a winter festival in a cold country. The teacher uses a text about a family visit to AlUla instead (replacing) and adds a short stress-practice stage before the reading (expanding).","استبدال النص بآخر مناسب ثقافياً وإضافة مرحلة نطق جديدة."]],"src":"McDonough, Shaw & Masuhara, Materials and Methods in ELT; Cunningsworth, Choosing Your Coursebook; Tomlinson, Materials Development in Language Teaching"},"en.mat.organizers":{"k":"Visual tools","t":["Graphic Organizers","المنظمات المرئية"],"body":[["A graphic organizer is a visual frame that shows how ideas relate. Used before reading, it activates schema; during reading, it guides note-taking; before writing, it organises ideas.","إطار مرئي يُظهر العلاقات، يُستخدم قبل القراءة وأثناءها وقبل الكتابة."]],"pts":[["Venn diagram → compare and contrast.","فن: مقارنة"],["Flow chart → steps of a process.","تدفق: خطوات"],["Fishbone (Ishikawa) → cause and effect.","عظم السمكة: سبب ونتيجة"],["Concept map / mind map → central idea and related ideas.","خريطة مفاهيم: فكرة مركزية"],["KWL → Know / Want to know / Learned; T-chart → two columns (pros and cons).","KWL وجدول T"]],"traps":[["Infographic = a one-page visual presentation of information, not a planning frame.","الإنفوجرافيك عرض لا إطار تخطيط"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.mat.sync":{"k":"Technology","t":["Synchronous, Asynchronous, Blended","المتزامن وغير المتزامن والمدمج"],"body":[["Online learning is described by timing. **Synchronous** activities require everyone at the same time (live classes, video calls, chat). **Asynchronous** activities can be done at any time (recorded lessons, email, forums, blogs, wikis). **Blended learning** combines online and face-to-face teaching. A **VLE / LMS** (Blackboard, Moodle, Madrasati, Google Classroom) is the platform that organises content, tasks and communication.","المتزامن في الوقت نفسه، وغير المتزامن في أي وقت، والمدمج يجمع بينهما، والمنصة تنظّم كل ذلك."]],"pts":[["CALL = Computer-Assisted Language Learning; MALL = Mobile-Assisted.","التعلم بالحاسب (CALL) وبالجوال (MALL)"],["Computers are best used as **teacher aids**, not teacher replacements.","الحاسب مساعد للمعلم"],["Downside of web resources: technical problems.","عيبها: المشكلات التقنية"],["Self-learning software helps independent learners; technology boosts teachers' creativity.","برامج التعلم الذاتي وإبداع المعلم"],["Tools: Prezi (slide-based presentations), OCR (print → editable text, e.g. Braille for blind students), Rosetta Stone (language learning), OneNote (real-time collaboration).","أدوات وما تفعله"]],"traps":[["Blackboard = VLE, not a social network.","بلاك بورد بيئة افتراضية"],["Email homework = asynchronous; Skype / live Teams lesson = synchronous.","البريد غير متزامن والبث المباشر متزامن"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Dudeney & Hockly, How to Teach English with Technology"},"en.mat.autonomy":{"k":"Independent learning","t":["Learner Autonomy","استقلال المتعلم"],"body":[["Henri Holec defined autonomy as 'the ability to take charge of one's own learning': deciding objectives, content, methods and pace, monitoring progress and evaluating results. Teachers foster it gradually, by handing over decisions and teaching the skills needed to make them.","هوليك: القدرة على تولي مسؤولية التعلم، وتنمو بنقل القرارات تدريجياً وتعليم مهارات اتخاذها."]],"pts":[["**Learner training**: dictionaries, vocabulary notebooks, goal-setting, self-evaluation.","تدريب المتعلم"],["**Self-access centre** (graded readers, audio, software, answer keys); **learning contracts** (learner and teacher agree goals, tasks and deadlines); **learning logs/diaries** and **portfolios**.","مركز ذاتي وعقود وسجلات"],["Design tasks with **choice** (e.g. one of three reading texts or output formats) and provide **answer keys** for self-correction.","الاختيار ومفاتيح التصحيح الذاتي"],["Self-learning software supports independent learners.","برامج التعلم الذاتي"]],"traps":[["Autonomy ≠ learning without a teacher.","ليس تعلماً بلا معلم"],["Best single way to encourage autonomy: train students to use reference resources.","التدريب على المراجع"]],"src":"Holec, Autonomy and Foreign Language Learning; Benson, Teaching and Researching Autonomy; Harmer"},"en.lrn.styles":{"k":"Learner differences","t":["Learning Styles: VAK and Kolb","أنماط التعلم"],"body":[["Sensory models (VAK; Fleming's VARK adds read/write) classify how learners prefer to receive information. **Kolb**'s experiential model describes learning as a cycle — concrete experience, reflective observation, abstract conceptualisation, active experimentation — and four styles that combine two stages each.","النماذج الحسية تصنف طريقة استقبال المعلومة، وكولب يصف دورة خبرة بأربعة أنماط."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Diverging","ar":"⁧متشعب⁩","items":[{"n":"Feel + watch","ar":"⁧خبرة محسوسة وملاحظة تأملية⁩"}]},{"n":"Assimilating","ar":"⁧استيعابي⁩","items":[{"n":"Think + watch","ar":"⁧مفاهيم مجردة وملاحظة تأملية⁩"}]},{"n":"Converging","ar":"⁧تقاربي⁩","items":[{"n":"Think + do","ar":"⁧مفاهيم مجردة وتجريب نشط⁩"}]},{"n":"Accommodating","ar":"⁧تكيّفي⁩","items":[{"n":"Feel + do","ar":"⁧خبرة محسوسة وتجريب نشط⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["Visual: pictures, written words, charts. Auditory: listening, discussion. Kinesthetic: movement, hands-on.","بصري وسمعي وحركي"],["Logical/analytic learners make lists and look for relationships.","المنطقي يرتب ويبحث عن العلاقات"],["Research questions strict 'matching' of teaching to one style; the safe practice is varied, multi-sensory teaching.","التنويع أفضل من حصر الطالب في نمط"]],"traps":[["Arranging jumbled word cut-outs = kinesthetic.","ترتيب القصاصات = حركي"],["Remembers names by seeing them = visual.","يرى فيتذكر = بصري"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Kolb, Experiential Learning"},"en.lrn.mi":{"k":"Learner differences","t":["Gardner's Multiple Intelligences","الذكاءات المتعددة"],"body":[["In *Frames of Mind* (1983), **Howard Gardner** proposed that people have several distinct intelligences; a naturalist intelligence was added later. Each suggests classroom activities that open the same content to different learners.","هوارد غاردنر (1983): عدة ذكاءات مستقلة، ولكل منها أنشطة صفية."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Word & logic","ar":"⁧اللغة والمنطق⁩","items":[{"n":"Linguistic","ar":"⁧قصص، ألغاز كلمات، كلمات متقاطعة⁩"},{"n":"Logical-math.","ar":"⁧أنماط، تصنيف، حل مشكلات⁩"}]},{"n":"Senses & body","ar":"⁧الحواس والجسد⁩","items":[{"n":"Spatial","ar":"⁧صور، خرائط، رسم⁩"},{"n":"Musical","ar":"⁧أناشيد وإيقاع⁩"},{"n":"Bodily-kinesthetic","ar":"⁧حركة، تمثيل، TPR⁩"}]},{"n":"People & self","ar":"⁧الآخرون والذات⁩","items":[{"n":"Interpersonal","ar":"⁧عمل جماعي وأقران⁩"},{"n":"Intrapersonal","ar":"⁧تأمل، يوميات، أهداف ذاتية⁩"},{"n":"Naturalist","ar":"⁧تصنيف من الطبيعة⁩"}]}]},"traps":[["**Howard** Gardner = multiple intelligences; **Robert** Gardner (with Lambert) = integrative/instrumental motivation.","غاردنران مختلفان"],["Group work develops **interpersonal** (not intrapersonal) intelligence.","الجماعي = بين شخصي"]],"src":"Gardner, Frames of Mind; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.lrn.motivation":{"k":"Motivation","t":["Types of Motivation","أنواع الدافعية"],"body":[["Two classic distinctions are often confused. **Intrinsic vs extrinsic** (Deci & Ryan's self-determination theory) is about the **source** of the drive: the activity itself or an external reward. **Integrative vs instrumental** (Gardner & Lambert) is about the **purpose** of learning the language: connecting with its people and culture, or practical gain.","داخلية/خارجية = مصدر الدافع، وتكاملية/نفعية = غرض تعلم اللغة."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Source","ar":"⁧المصدر⁩","items":[{"n":"Intrinsic","who":"Deci & Ryan","ar":"⁧متعة ورضا ذاتي، فضول⁩"},{"n":"Extrinsic","who":"Deci & Ryan","ar":"⁧درجات، جوائز، ترقية⁩"}]},{"n":"Purpose","ar":"⁧الغرض⁩","items":[{"n":"Integrative","who":"Gardner & Lambert","ar":"⁧حب الثقافة والتواصل مع أهلها⁩"},{"n":"Instrumental","who":"Gardner & Lambert","ar":"⁧وظيفة، بعثة، نجاح في اختبار⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["Motivating practices: realistic high expectations, curiosity, active involvement, choice, useful feedback.","ما يرفع الدافعية"],["Demotivating: correcting every mistake, public criticism, tasks far too hard or too easy.","ما يخفضها"]],"traps":[["A job or scholarship = instrumental; liking the culture = integrative.","وظيفة = نفعية"],["Arousal of curiosity = an **internal** (intrinsic) motivating factor.","الفضول دافع داخلي"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned; Dörnyei, Teaching and Researching Motivation"},"en.lrn.dornyei":{"k":"Motivation","t":["Dörnyei's L2 Motivational Self System","نظام الذات الدافعي عند دورنيي"],"body":[["Zoltán Dörnyei proposed that L2 motivation comes from the gap between who learners are and who they imagine becoming. The system has three parts.","الدافعية تنشأ من الفجوة بين الذات الحالية والذات المتخيلة."]],"pts":[["**Ideal L2 self**: the English-speaking person the learner would like to become.","الذات المثالية"],["**Ought-to L2 self**: what the learner believes they should become to meet others' expectations or avoid failure.","الذات الواجبة"],["**L2 learning experience**: the motivating effect of the teacher, class, materials and success.","خبرة التعلم"]],"ex":[["A teacher asks students to imagine themselves five years from now using English at work and to describe that scene — strengthening the ideal L2 self.","تخيّل الذات المستقبلية يقوّي الذات المثالية"]],"traps":[["Ideal L2 self belongs to Dörnyei, not Gardner.","دورنيي لا غاردنر"]],"src":"Dörnyei, The Psychology of the Language Learner; Lightbown & Spada"},"en.lrn.affect":{"k":"Affective domain","t":["Anxiety and Willingness to Communicate","القلق والاستعداد للتواصل"],"body":[["**Foreign language anxiety** (Horwitz and colleagues) is tension specific to learning and using a new language, linked to communication apprehension, test anxiety and fear of negative evaluation. In Krashen's terms, it raises the **affective filter** and blocks input. Some mild tension can be facilitative, but debilitative anxiety lowers performance.","قلق اللغة الأجنبية يرفع المصفاة الوجدانية ويحجب المدخلات، وبعض التوتر الخفيف قد يكون مفيداً."],["**Willingness to communicate** (MacIntyre) is a learner's readiness to speak when given the chance; confidence and a supportive atmosphere increase it.","الاستعداد للتواصل يزيد بالثقة والجو الداعم."]],"pts":[["Other affective factors (Brown): self-esteem, inhibition, risk-taking, empathy, extroversion/introversion.","عوامل وجدانية أخرى"],["CLT tends to suit extroverted learners; introverts may prefer individual work first.","التواصلي يناسب المنفتحين"],["**Collectivist cultures** (group harmony, respect for the teacher's authority): learners may avoid asking questions or disagreeing in public — use small groups and anonymous question slips.","الثقافات الجماعية وتجنب السؤال العلني"],["Participation grows when teachers praise effort, give wait time and build on partly correct answers.","المديح على الجهد ووقت الانتظار والبناء على الإجابة الجزئية"]],"traps":[["Anxiety is an **affective** factor, not a cognitive style.","القلق وجداني"],["Modelling a performance first builds **confidence**.","النمذجة تبني الثقة"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.lrn.cogstyles":{"k":"Cognitive styles","t":["Field Independence and Reflectivity","الاستقلال عن المجال والتروي"],"body":[["Cognitive styles are consistent ways of perceiving and processing information. Witkin's **field independence** is the ability to pick out a detail from a confusing background; **field dependence** is perceiving the field as a whole. Brown notes that field independence helps with analytic, form-focused classroom learning, while field dependence helps with face-to-face communication.","المستقل عن المجال يلتقط التفاصيل، والمعتمد عليه يرى الكل ويتواصل اجتماعياً بسهولة."],["**Reflectivity vs impulsivity** (described by Jerome Kagan, 1965): reflective learners take longer and make fewer errors; impulsive learners answer quickly, guess and may be faster at fluent tasks.","المتروّي أبطأ وأدق، والمندفع أسرع ويخمّن أكثر."]],"traps":[["Detail-focused, analytic, likes grammar rules = field-independent.","تحليلي = مستقل عن المجال"],["A style is not a disability: dyslexia and ADHD are learning difficulties, not styles.","النمط ليس صعوبة تعلم"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.lrn.diff":{"k":"Differentiation","t":["Differentiated Instruction","التعليم المتمايز"],"body":[["Carol Ann Tomlinson describes differentiation as planning in response to learners' **readiness**, **interests** and **learning profiles**. The teacher can differentiate four elements: **content** (what is learned), **process** (how), **product** (how learning is shown) and **learning environment**.","توملينسون: الاستجابة لاستعداد الطلاب واهتماماتهم وأنماطهم بتكييف المحتوى والعملية والناتج والبيئة."]],"pts":[["Tiered tasks: same objective, different levels of support or challenge.","مهام متدرجة"],["Open-ended tasks and extension tasks for fast finishers.","مهام مفتوحة وإضافية"],["**Gifted / high-proficiency**: enrichment (deeper, broader tasks), acceleration (moving faster), curriculum compacting (skip what is mastered) (Renzulli), projects and leadership roles.","إثراء وتسريع وضغط المنهج"],["**Struggling / limited-proficiency**: pre-teach vocabulary, chunk tasks, visuals and graphic organisers, model answers and sentence frames, extra time, frequent checks, remedial lessons based on diagnostic results.","دعم المتعثر"],["**Flexible grouping**: similar-level groups for targeted help, mixed-level groups for peer support and **peer tutoring**.","تجميع مرن"],["Interest inventories collect learners' interests to personalise topics.","استبيان الاهتمامات"]],"traps":[["Differentiation keeps the same core objective; it does not give weaker students a different, easier curriculum.","نفس الهدف بطرق مختلفة"],["Giving gifted students 'more of the same' is not enrichment.","المزيد من نفس الشيء ليس إثراء"]],"src":"Tomlinson, How to Differentiate Instruction in Mixed-Ability Classrooms; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.lis.process":{"k":"Listening","t":["Top-down, bottom-up and interactive processing","المعالجة من الأعلى ومن الأسفل والتفاعلية"],"body":[["**Bottom-up** processing builds meaning from the signal upward: recognizing sounds, separating words in connected speech, noticing endings, word order and grammatical cues. It depends mainly on the listener's **linguistic (grammatical) competence**.","من الأسفل: من الأصوات إلى الكلمات إلى الجملة، ويعتمد على المعرفة اللغوية."],["**Top-down** processing starts from the listener's head: background knowledge (**schema**), knowledge of the situation and the topic, and expectations. The listener predicts, infers and gets the main idea even with gaps.","من الأعلى: الخلفية والسياق والتوقع، ويوصل للفكرة العامة رغم الثغرات."]],"pts":[["Skilled listening is **interactive**: both run together; learners with weak decoding over-rely on guessing, those with little background over-rely on words.","الماهر يجمعهما، والضعف في أحدهما يجعله يعتمد على الآخر"],["Top-down tasks: predicting from a title, listening for gist, inferring the speaker's attitude, summarizing.","مهام من الأعلى: توقع، فكرة عامة، استنتاج، تلخيص"],["Bottom-up tasks: dictation of short chunks, distinguishing minimal pairs, counting words in a sentence, spotting weak forms.","مهام من الأسفل: إملاء مقاطع، أزواج صغرى، عدّ الكلمات، الصيغ الضعيفة"]],"traps":[["'Listening for the main idea' is top-down; 'recognizing words and sounds' is bottom-up.","الفكرة الرئيسية من الأعلى، والكلمات والأصوات من الأسفل"],["Intensive listening develops **bottom-up** skills.","المكثف يطوّر من الأسفل"]],"ex":[["Before a weather forecast for Jeddah, students guess the words they will hear (*hot, humid, wind*) — **top-down**. Then they listen again and decide whether the speaker said *thirteen* or *thirty* degrees — **bottom-up**.","توقع كلمات النشرة الجوية قبل الاستماع = من الأعلى، والتمييز بين (thirteen, thirty) = من الأسفل."]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Richards, Teaching Listening and Speaking; Field, Listening in the Language Classroom"},"en.lis.scope":{"k":"Listening","t":["Intensive vs extensive listening","الاستماع المكثف والموسّع"],"body":[["**Intensive listening** uses short extracts and close attention to detail — exact words, sounds, grammatical forms — often replaying the same few seconds. It trains accurate perception (bottom-up).","المكثف: مقاطع قصيرة وانتباه للتفاصيل، ويدرّب الإدراك الدقيق."],["**Extensive listening** means large amounts of listening for general meaning or enjoyment, usually material learners choose: podcasts, stories, audiobooks, series. It builds fluency, stamina and familiarity with natural speech.","الموسّع: استماع كثير للفهم العام أو المتعة، ويبني الطلاقة والتعود على الكلام الطبيعي."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Intensive","ar":"⁧مكثف⁩","items":[{"n":"Short","ar":"⁧دقائق قليلة⁩","kw":["details","form"]},{"n":"Teacher-led","ar":"⁧في الفصل غالباً⁩","kw":["replay"]}]},{"n":"Extensive","ar":"⁧موسّع⁩","items":[{"n":"Long","ar":"⁧كمية كبيرة⁩","kw":["gist","pleasure"]},{"n":"Learner-chosen","ar":"⁧يختاره الطالب⁩","kw":["podcasts"]}]}]},"traps":[["A long passage for general understanding and pleasure = extensive, not intensive.","نص طويل للمتعة = موسّع"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Field, Listening in the Language Classroom"},"en.lis.stages":{"k":"Listening","t":["Pre-, while- and post-listening","مراحل درس الاستماع"],"body":[["The three-stage lesson gives listening a purpose and a structure. Each stage answers a question: *Why am I listening?* (pre), *What do I need to get?* (while), *What do I do with it?* (post).","كل مرحلة تجيب سؤالاً: لماذا أستمع؟ ماذا آخذ؟ ماذا أفعل به؟"]],"pts":[["**Pre**: set the listening objective, activate schema, predict from pictures or a title, pre-teach only the essential words, generate questions.","قبل: هدف، خلفية، توقع، كلمات أساسية فقط، توليد أسئلة"],["**While**: first listen for **gist**, second listen for **specific information**; note-taking; checking predictions.","أثناء: الفكرة العامة ثم التفاصيل، تدوين، تحقق من التوقعات"],["**Post**: discussion, role play, writing, reflection on strategies, short bottom-up work on difficult parts.","بعد: نقاش أو كتابة أو تأمل أو تدريب على الأجزاء الصعبة"]],"traps":[["'Verify predictions' happens **while** listening, not before.","التحقق من التوقعات أثناء الاستماع"],["The first listening is usually for gist, not details.","الاستماع الأول للفكرة العامة"]],"ex":[["Topic: a guide talking about AlUla. **Pre**: photos on the board, students predict what the guide will mention, the teacher pre-teaches *heritage site*. **First listen**: choose the best title. **Second listen**: complete a table of dates and opening times. **Post**: pairs plan a one-day visit.","صور العُلا وتوقع ← اختيار العنوان ← جدول التواريخ ← تخطيط زيارة في أزواج."]],"src":"Richards, Teaching Listening and Speaking; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.lis.types":{"k":"Listening","t":["Types of listening by purpose","أنواع الاستماع حسب الغرض"],"body":[["Listening types are named after the listener's **purpose**: find the purpose in the situation and the type follows.","اسم النوع يأتي من الغرض: ابحث عن الغرض في الموقف."]],"pts":[["**Appreciative** — for enjoyment (stories, music, a funny clip).","تقديري: للمتعة"],["**Comprehensive** — to understand and remember a message (lectures, directions).","استيعابي: لفهم الرسالة"],["**Critical** — to analyse and judge a message before accepting or rejecting it.","نقدي: تحليل وحكم"],["**Empathic / therapeutic** — to support the speaker emotionally.","تعاطفي: لدعم المتحدث"],["**Discriminative** — to distinguish sounds (minimal pairs, tone of voice).","تمييزي: للتفريق بين الأصوات"],["**Selective** — only for certain information, ignoring the rest (often with note-taking).","انتقائي: معلومة محددة فقط"]],"traps":[["'Listen and do' (respond physically to commands) = **Total Physical Response**, not a listening type.","اسمع ونفّذ = TPR"],["Radio, TV and films = **non-interactive**; phone calls = interactive.","الراديو غير تفاعلي"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Wolvin & Coakley, Listening"},"en.lis.brown":{"k":"Listening","t":["What makes listening difficult (Brown)","ما يجعل الاستماع صعباً عند براون"],"body":[["Brown lists characteristics of spoken language that make listening difficult for learners. Teachers use the list to choose texts and to plan bottom-up practice.","براون يعدد خصائص الكلام المنطوق التي تصعّب الاستماع، ونستفيد منها في اختيار النص والتدريب."]],"pts":[["**Clustering** — speech comes in phrases, not single words.","التجمّع: الكلام عبارات لا كلمات"],["**Redundancy** — repetitions, rephrasing, 'I mean' (can help or confuse).","الحشو والتكرار"],["**Reduced forms** — *gonna, wanna, whatcha*, contractions, elision.","الصيغ المختصرة"],["**Performance variables** — hesitations, false starts, fillers (*uh, you know*).","متغيرات الأداء: التردد والبدايات الخاطئة"],["**Colloquial language** — idioms, slang, cultural references.","العامية"],["**Rate of delivery** — the speed of speech.","سرعة الكلام"],["**Stress, rhythm and intonation** — they carry meaning.","النبر والإيقاع والتنغيم"],["**Interaction** — negotiating meaning in conversation.","التفاعل والتفاوض على المعنى"]],"traps":[["Fillers and false starts = performance variables, not reduced forms.","التردد = متغيرات الأداء"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.lis.metacog":{"k":"Listening","t":["Metacognitive listening instruction","تعليم الاستماع بما وراء المعرفة"],"body":[["Field argued that the familiar **comprehension approach** (listen, answer questions, check) **tests** listening rather than teaching it, because it shows the result without training the process.","فيلد: «استمع وأجب» يختبر الاستماع ولا يعلّمه."],["Vandergrift and Goh proposed a **metacognitive** sequence: learners **plan** and predict before listening, **monitor** and verify predictions over several listens, and **evaluate** and reflect afterwards on which strategies helped. The aim is listeners who know *how* they listen.","فاندرغريفت وغوه: تخطيط وتوقع ← مراقبة وتحقق عبر عدة استماعات ← تقويم وتأمل."]],"pts":[["Planning: setting a goal, predicting content and vocabulary.","التخطيط: هدف وتوقع"],["Monitoring: checking comprehension while listening.","المراقبة: فحص الفهم أثناء الاستماع"],["Evaluating: judging what worked after the task.","التقويم: ماذا نجح بعد المهمة"],["A **process approach** trains the sub-skills behind comprehension, such as recognising word boundaries in connected speech, instead of only checking answers.","المدخل العملي: تدريب العمليات الفرعية للفهم"]],"traps":[["'Checking whether my prediction was correct' is a **metacognitive** (monitoring) strategy, not a cognitive one.","التحقق من التوقع = ما وراء معرفي"]],"src":"Field, Listening in the Language Classroom; Vandergrift & Goh, Teaching and Learning Second Language Listening"},"en.spk.drills":{"k":"Speaking","t":["Types of oral drills","أنواع التدريبات الشفهية"],"body":[["Drills come from the Audio-Lingual Method, which saw learning as habit formation. Today they are used briefly to improve **pronunciation and accuracy** of a new form before freer practice.","من الطريقة السمعية الشفهية، وتُستخدم اليوم باختصار للنطق والدقة."]],"pts":[["**Repetition / choral** — learners repeat the model, individually or together.","تكرار وترديد جماعي"],["**Substitution** — replace one word using a cue: *I like apples → (dates) → I like dates.*","استبدال كلمة بإشارة"],["**Transformation** — change the form: positive → negative, active ↔ passive, statement → question.","تحويل الصيغة"],["**Back-chaining** — build a long sentence from the end: *…yesterday → …to the mall yesterday → I went to the mall yesterday.*","بناء عكسي من آخر الجملة"],["**Chain drill** — each learner asks the next one in turn.","سلسلة بين الطلاب"],["**Imperative drills** — commands that elicit physical responses (*Stand up, open your book*).","أوامر وحركة"]],"traps":[["Passive → active is **transformation**, not substitution.","المجهول ← المعلوم = تحويل"],["Choral repetition is linked to the **Audio-Lingual Method**.","الترديد الجماعي من السمعية الشفهية"]],"ex":[["Teacher: *I like apples.* — cue: *dates* — class: *I like dates.* — cue: *mangoes* … This is a **substitution** drill. Then: *The window was broken by Ali.* → *Ali broke the window.* — a **transformation** drill.","تغيير كلمة واحدة = استبدال. تحويل المجهول إلى معلوم = تحويل."]],"src":"Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods in Language Teaching; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.spk.questions":{"k":"Speaking","t":["Display vs referential questions","أسئلة العرض والأسئلة المرجعية"],"body":[["**Display questions** ask for information the teacher already has; they check knowledge and recall but usually produce one-word answers. **Referential questions** seek information or opinions the teacher does not know; they lead to longer and more natural answers and real communication.","سؤال العرض يعرف المعلم جوابه وجوابه قصير. المرجعي حقيقي ويولّد كلاماً أطول وتواصلاً."],["**Eliciting** draws language from learners with clues, examples, pictures or mime. It keeps learners active and shows the teacher what they already know.","الاستنباط: نستخرج اللغة من الطلاب بالتلميح والأمثلة."]],"ex":[["Display: *What's the capital of Saudi Arabia?* — Referential: *If you could live in any Saudi city, which would you choose and why?*","عرض مقابل مرجعي"]],"traps":[["Closed-ended questions are designed for **objectivity**.","المغلق للموضوعية"],["A question with a known answer is never referential, however long it is.","ما يعرف المعلم جوابه ليس مرجعياً"]],"pts":[["Eliciting example: *Who drives a vehicle carrying twenty people?* → *a bus driver*.","مثال الاستنباط بالتلميح"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Richards & Lockhart, Reflective Teaching in Second Language Classrooms"},"en.spk.errors":{"k":"Errors","t":["Corder: errors vs mistakes","كوردر: الخطأ والزلّة"],"body":[["Corder made a lasting distinction. An **error** is systematic: it reflects the learner's current knowledge (competence), so the learner cannot correct it alone. A **mistake** is a performance slip — tiredness, haste, nerves — which the learner can self-correct when it is pointed out.","الخطأ نظامي من نقص المعرفة ولا يصححه الطالب وحده. الزلّة هفوة أداء يصححها إذا نُبّه."],["Corder also argued that errors are **significant**: they show the teacher what the learner has learned, give researchers evidence of how language is acquired, and are a device the learner uses to test hypotheses.","والأخطاء مهمة: تكشف ما تعلّمه الطالب وكيف يكتسب اللغة."]],"pts":[["Practical test: can the learner fix it when prompted? Yes → mistake; No → error.","هل يصححه إذا نُبّه؟ نعم = زلّة، لا = خطأ"]],"traps":[["A mistake reflects **performance**, not competence.","الزلّة أداء لا كفاية"]],"src":"Corder, The Significance of Learners' Errors; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.spk.feedback":{"k":"Errors","t":["Lyster & Ranta: six types of oral corrective feedback","لايستر ورانتا: ستة أنواع للتغذية التصحيحية"],"body":[["Lyster and Ranta observed classrooms and identified six feedback moves. They found **recasts** were the most common but least often led learners to repair their own error, while moves that make learners do the correcting (elicitation, metalinguistic clues, clarification requests, repetition) led to more learner repair.","إعادة الصياغة الأكثر استخداماً لكنها الأقل دفعاً للطالب لتصحيح نفسه، والأنواع التي تجعله يصحح تثمر أكثر."],["In **fluency** work, the teacher notes errors and gives **delayed**, **selective** feedback on one or two errors linked to the aim; in accuracy work, correction can be immediate.","في الطلاقة: تصحيح مؤجل وانتقائي، وفي الدقة فوري."]],"pts":[["**Recast** — the teacher reformulates correctly without saying it is wrong: *S: He go. T: Oh, he goes?*","إعادة صياغة ضمنية"],["**Explicit correction** — *No, not 'go' — you should say 'goes'.*","تصحيح صريح"],["**Clarification request** — *Pardon? / Sorry?*","طلب توضيح"],["**Metalinguistic feedback** — a comment about the rule: *We use the past with 'yesterday'.*","تعليق على القاعدة"],["**Elicitation** — *She…?* (pause), or *How do we say that?*","استنباط بوقفة"],["**Repetition** — the teacher repeats the error with rising intonation: *He go?*","تكرار الخطأ بنغمة صاعدة"]],"traps":[["A recast has **no** 'that's wrong'; explicit correction does.","الإعادة الضمنية بلا «خطأ»"],["Repeating the error with rising intonation = repetition, not recast.","تكرار الخطأ ≠ إعادة صياغة"]],"ex":[["Student: *Yesterday I go to the mall.* Teacher: *Oh, you went to the mall? Which one?* — a **recast**: the correct form is given implicitly and the conversation continues.","المعلم يعيد الجملة صحيحة ضمناً ويكمل الحديث: إعادة صياغة."]],"src":"Lyster & Ranta (1997); Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.spk.arab":{"k":"Pronunciation","t":["Pronunciation problems of Arab learners","صعوبات النطق عند المتعلم العربي"],"body":[["Most difficulties come from differences between the Arabic and English sound systems. Arabic has no /p/, so learners merge /p/ and /b/; /v/ is often replaced by /f/; /ŋ/ may become /ŋg/ or /n/; and long consonant clusters are broken by an added vowel.","معظم الصعوبات من اختلاف النظامين: لا /p/ في العربية، والصوت /v/ يتحول إلى /f/، والتجمعات الصامتة يُقحم بينها صائت."],["When two separate L2 sounds are merged because the L1 does not distinguish them, Weinreich called it **under-differentiation**. **Minimal pairs** (*pen/Ben, fan/van*) are the classic tool: first hear the difference, then produce it.","دمج صوتين منفصلين في الإنجليزية لأن العربية لا تفرّق بينهما = under-differentiation، وعلاجه الأزواج الصغرى."]],"pts":[["Segmental = individual sounds; suprasegmental = stress, rhythm, intonation.","مفردة / فوق مقطعية"],["Teenagers' resistance to new sounds is often **self-consciousness**, not inability.","المراهق: الحرج"]],"traps":[["*lamb/lamp* is not a minimal pair (the b in lamb is silent, so the words differ by an added sound).","lamb/lamp ليس زوجاً صغيراً"],["In Prator's hierarchy of difficulty (cited by Brown) one L1 sound becoming two L2 sounds is called a **split** — read the options carefully.","في هرم براتور يسمى هذا split"]],"src":"Swan & Smith, Learner English; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Kelly, How to Teach Pronunciation"},"en.spk.intonation":{"k":"Pronunciation","t":["Stress and intonation in sentences","نبر الجملة والتنغيم"],"body":[["English is stress-timed: stressed **content words** come at fairly regular intervals and unstressed **function words** are squeezed into weak forms (*can* → /kən/). The **tonic syllable** carries the main pitch movement, normally on the last content word, and moves to show contrast or new information.","كلمات المحتوى تُنبر وكلمات الوظيفة تضعف، والمقطع النووي يتحرك للتقابل."],["Intonation carries attitude: the same words can sound sure or unsure, polite or rude. Teach it through meaning, with arrows, hand gestures, back-chaining and short dialogues.","التنغيم يحمل الموقف، فيُعلَّم بالمعنى والحوارات."]],"pts":[["Falling ↘: statements, wh-questions, commands.","هابط: إخبار وأسئلة wh وأوامر"],["Rising ↗: many yes/no questions, surprise, unfinished lists.","صاعد: نعم/لا والتعجب والقوائم غير المكتملة"],["Falling tag: the speaker is fairly sure and expects agreement. Rising tag: a real question.","الذيل الهابط تأكيد، والصاعد سؤال حقيقي"]],"traps":[["*I didn't take YOUR pen* = contrastive stress (I took someone else's).","نبر تقابلي"]],"ex":[["The teacher says *You're from Taif, aren't you?* twice — once falling, once rising — and students show a thumbs-up for *the speaker is sure* or a question mark for *the speaker is asking*.","نفس السؤال الذيلي بنغمتين، والطلاب يحددون: متأكد أم يسأل فعلاً."]],"src":"Kelly, How to Teach Pronunciation; Roach, English Phonetics and Phonology"},"en.spk.lfc":{"k":"Pronunciation","t":["Intelligibility and the Lingua Franca Core","الوضوح ونواة الإنجليزية المشتركة"],"body":[["Because English is now used mostly between non-native speakers, Jenkins studied which pronunciation features actually cause breakdowns. Her **Lingua Franca Core** lists the features that matter most for **intelligibility**; the rest can be left to learners' own accents.","جنكنز درست ما يسبب انقطاع الفهم بين غير الناطقين، وحددت النواة الضرورية للوضوح."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Core","ar":"⁧أساسي⁩","items":[{"n":"Consonants","ar":"⁧معظم الصوامت⁩","kw":["/p/–/b/"]},{"n":"Clusters","ar":"⁧تجمعات أول الكلمة⁩","kw":["street"]},{"n":"Vowel length","ar":"⁧طول الصائت⁩","kw":["ship/sheep"]},{"n":"Nuclear stress","ar":"⁧النبر النووي⁩","kw":["contrast"]}]},{"n":"Non-core","ar":"⁧غير أساسي⁩","items":[{"n":"/θ/ /ð/","ar":"think / this","kw":["substitutes OK"]},{"n":"Weak forms","ar":"⁧الصيغ الضعيفة⁩","kw":["/ə/"]}]}]},"traps":[["/θ/ and /ð/ are among the **least** essential features in the LFC.","الصوتان (/θ/, /ð/) غير أساسيين"],["The modern goal is intelligibility, not a native-like accent.","الهدف الوضوح لا اللكنة الأصلية"]],"src":"Jenkins, The Phonology of English as an International Language; Walker, Teaching the Pronunciation of English as a Lingua Franca"},"en.rd.skimscan":{"k":"Reading","t":["Skimming vs scanning","القراءة السريعة والانتقائية"],"body":[["Both are fast, selective reading — the difference is the **target**. Skimming looks for the general picture: topic, main idea, writer's purpose. Scanning looks for a single item and ignores the rest.","كلاهما سريع، والفرق في الهدف: الصورة العامة أم معلومة واحدة."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Skimming","ar":"⁧للفكرة العامة⁩","items":[{"n":"Choose a title","ar":"⁧اختيار العنوان⁩","kw":["gist"]},{"n":"Match headlines","ar":"⁧مطابقة العناوين⁩","kw":["main idea"]},{"n":"Overview a chapter","ar":"⁧استعراض فصل⁩","kw":["survey"]}]},{"n":"Scanning","ar":"⁧لمعلومة محددة⁩","items":[{"n":"Find a date","ar":"⁧تاريخ أو رقم⁩","kw":["specific"]},{"n":"Dictionary","ar":"⁧البحث عن كلمة⁩","kw":["look up"]},{"n":"Timetable","ar":"⁧جدول مواعيد⁩","kw":["detail"]}]}]},"traps":[["Finding a country's population figure = scanning, even if the text is about the whole country.","رقم محدد = انتقائية"]],"src":"Grellet, Developing Reading Skills; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.rd.er":{"k":"Reading","t":["Extensive reading — Day & Bamford","القراءة الموسّعة: داي وبامفورد"],"body":[["Extensive reading means reading **a lot** of **easy** material for general understanding and enjoyment. Day and Bamford set out ten principles that describe a successful programme; the core idea is that fluency comes from quantity, and quantity comes only when reading is easy and enjoyable.","الفكرة: الطلاقة تأتي من الكمية، والكمية لا تأتي إلا إذا كانت القراءة سهلة وممتعة."]],"pts":[["The material is **easy** — at or below the learner's level.","سهلة: في مستواه أو أقل"],["A **variety** of material on many topics is available, and learners **choose** what to read.","متنوعة ويختارها الطالب"],["Learners read **as much as possible**, for pleasure and general understanding.","أكبر قدر ممكن للمتعة"],["Reading is its own reward — few or no comprehension tests.","القراءة مكافأة نفسها"],["Reading is individual, silent and usually faster.","فردية وصامتة وأسرع"],["The teacher orients and guides, and **models** being a reader.","المعلم يوجّه ويكون قدوة قارئة"]],"traps":[["'Texts should be above learners' level to challenge them' is NOT a principle of extensive reading.","النص الأعلى من المستوى ليس من مبادئها"]],"src":"Day & Bamford, Extensive Reading in the Second Language Classroom; Nation, Teaching ESL/EFL Reading and Writing"},"en.rd.models":{"k":"Reading","t":["Models of reading","نماذج القراءة"],"body":[["**Bottom-up** models see reading as decoding in sequence: letters → sounds → words → sentences → meaning. The text drives understanding, so word recognition is central.","من الأسفل: فك الترميز بالترتيب، والنص هو المحرك."],["**Top-down** models put the reader first. Goodman described reading as a *psycholinguistic guessing game*: readers sample the text, predict, and confirm or revise predictions using their knowledge.","من الأعلى: القارئ أولاً، يتوقع ويؤكد (لعبة التخمين النفسية اللغوية عند غودمان)."]],"pts":[["Names: **Gough** (1972) described a classic bottom-up model; **Goodman** (1967) the top-down 'psycholinguistic guessing game'; **Stanovich** (1980) the interactive-compensatory model.","غوف من الأسفل، وغودمان من الأعلى، وستانوفيتش التفاعلي التعويضي"],["Stanovich's **interactive-compensatory** model combines both: processes at all levels work together, and a deficit in one is compensated by greater reliance on the other.","التفاعلي التعويضي لستانوفيتش: العمليتان معاً، والضعف في واحدة تعوّضه الأخرى."]],"traps":[["Reading word by word, looking up every unknown word, or using prefix + root + suffix to decode = bottom-up.","كلمة كلمة أو تفكيك الكلمة = من الأسفل"],["Starting from what learners know and the overall meaning = top-down.","البدء من الخلفية والمعنى العام = من الأعلى"]],"ex":[["A student reading about camel racing does not know *jockey* but understands it from the picture and topic (**top-down** compensating). Another student works out *unbelievable* from *un- + believe + -able* (**bottom-up**).","فهم كلمة من الصورة والموضوع = من الأعلى، وتفكيكها إلى سابقة وجذر ولاحقة = من الأسفل."]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Grabe & Stoller, Teaching and Researching Reading"},"en.rd.strategies":{"k":"Reading","t":["Comprehension strategies — the confusing pairs","استراتيجيات الفهم: الأزواج المتشابهة"],"body":[["Several strategies sound alike. The key is **when** they happen and **what** they use.","المفتاح: متى تحدث الاستراتيجية، وعلى ماذا تعتمد."]],"pts":[["**Predicting** — *before* reading: prior knowledge + title, headings, pictures → what the text will say.","التوقع: قبل القراءة من العنوان والصور"],["**Inferring** — *during/after*: background knowledge + clues in the text → meaning not stated.","الاستنتاج: أدلة النص + الخلفية ← معنى غير مذكور"],["**Summarizing** — main ideas only, shorter, own words.","التلخيص: الأفكار الرئيسية باختصار"],["**Paraphrasing** — same idea, similar length, different words.","إعادة الصياغة: نفس الفكرة بكلمات أخرى"],["**Monitoring** — noticing a breakdown in understanding and fixing it (re-read, slow down, ask).","المراقبة: اكتشاف الخلل وإصلاحه"],["**Visualizing** — forming mental images; **text-to-text** connections — linking texts by the same author or theme.","التصوّر، والربط بين النصوص"]],"traps":[["Prior knowledge + title BEFORE reading = predicting; background knowledge + text clues = inferring.","قبل القراءة توقع، ومن أدلة النص استنتاج"]],"src":"Grabe & Stoller, Teaching and Researching Reading; Anderson, Exploring Second Language Reading"},"en.rd.cueing":{"k":"Early reading","t":["Cueing systems and phonological awareness","أنظمة التلميح والوعي الصوتي"],"body":[["Beginning readers use three kinds of clues to identify words. Strong readers use all three together and check one against the others.","القارئ المبتدئ يستخدم ثلاثة أنواع من التلميحات، والقوي يجمعها."]],"pts":[["**Semantic** — meaning: context, pictures, background knowledge (*Does it make sense?*).","دلالي: المعنى والسياق والصورة"],["**Syntactic** — grammar: what kind of word fits here (*Does it sound right?*).","نحوي: ما الذي يناسب تركيب الجملة"],["**Graphophonic** — letter–sound relationships (*Does it look right?*).","صوتي كتابي: علاقة الحرف بالصوت"],["**Phonological awareness** — hearing and manipulating sound units: rhyme, syllables, onset–rime, phonemes. Developed through rhyming, syllabifying, **segmenting** (word → sounds) and **blending** (sounds → word).","الوعي الصوتي: قافية ومقاطع وتجزئة ودمج"]],"traps":[["Guessing a word from the picture and context = **semantic** cueing.","من الصورة والسياق = دلالي"],["Segmenting = breaking apart; blending = joining together.","التجزئة تفكيك والدمج تركيب"]],"src":"Cameron, Teaching Languages to Young Learners; Pinter, Teaching Young Language Learners"},"en.rd.phonics":{"k":"Early reading","t":["Synthetic vs analytic phonics","الصوتيات التركيبية والتحليلية"],"body":[["**Synthetic phonics** teaches letter–sound correspondences one by one and then trains learners to **blend** them to read words: /s/ /æ/ /t/ → *sat*. It works from the parts to the whole.","التركيبية: صوت كل حرف ثم الدمج لقراءة الكلمة، من الجزء إلى الكل."],["**Analytic phonics** starts from whole words learners already know and analyses the shared sound patterns: *cat, cap, can* all begin with /kæ/. It works from the whole to the parts.","التحليلية: من كلمات معروفة نحلل الأنماط المشتركة، من الكل إلى الجزء."]],"traps":[["Teaching s, a, t and then blending them = **synthetic**.","أصوات ثم دمج = تركيبية"],["Decoding is the first stage in learning to read.","فك الترميز أول مراحل القراءة"]],"src":"Cameron, Teaching Languages to Young Learners; Nation, Teaching ESL/EFL Reading and Writing"},"en.rd.tools":{"k":"Reading","t":["KWL, SQ3R and reciprocal teaching","جدول KWL وطريقة SQ3R والتدريس التبادلي"],"body":[["These are structured routines that make reading strategies visible and repeatable.","روتينات منظمة تجعل استراتيجيات القراءة ظاهرة ومتكررة."]],"pts":[["**KWL** (Ogle): *Know* — *Want to know* — *Learned*; connects prior knowledge with new learning and gives a purpose.","KWL: أعرف، أريد أن أعرف، تعلمت"],["**SQ3R** (Robinson): Survey, Question, Read, Recite, Review — a study-reading method.","SQ3R: استعرض، اسأل، اقرأ، سمّع، راجع"],["**Reciprocal teaching** (Palincsar & Brown): learners take turns leading four strategies — **predict, question, clarify, summarize**.","التدريس التبادلي: توقع، اسأل، وضّح، لخّص"]],"traps":[["KWL's main benefit = connecting prior knowledge with new learning.","KWL يربط السابق بالجديد"]],"src":"Grabe & Stoller, Teaching and Researching Reading; Anderson, Exploring Second Language Reading"},"en.voc.knowing":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["What it means to know a word (Nation)","ماذا تعني معرفة الكلمة عند نيشن"],"body":[["Nation describes word knowledge in three areas — **form, meaning and use** — each with a receptive and a productive side. Knowing a word is therefore a matter of degree, not yes or no.","معرفة الكلمة ثلاثة مجالات: شكل ومعنى واستخدام، ولكل منها جانب استقبالي وإنتاجي، فهي درجات."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Form","ar":"⁧الشكل⁩","items":[{"n":"Spoken","ar":"⁧النطق⁩","kw":["stress"]},{"n":"Written","ar":"⁧الإملاء⁩","kw":["spelling"]},{"n":"Word parts","ar":"⁧أجزاء الكلمة⁩","kw":["affixes"]}]},{"n":"Meaning","ar":"⁧المعنى⁩","items":[{"n":"Form-meaning","ar":"⁧ربط الشكل بالمعنى⁩","kw":["concept"]},{"n":"Associations","ar":"⁧الترابطات⁩","kw":["synonyms"]}]},{"n":"Use","ar":"⁧الاستخدام⁩","items":[{"n":"Grammar","ar":"⁧الوظيفة النحوية⁩","kw":["part of speech"]},{"n":"Collocations","ar":"⁧التلازم⁩","kw":["make a decision"]},{"n":"Constraints","ar":"⁧الرسمية والتكرار⁩","kw":["register"]}]}]},"pts":[["**Receptive** vocabulary is larger than **productive** vocabulary.","الاستقبالية أكبر من الإنتاجية"],["**Breadth** = how many words; **depth** = how well each word is known.","الاتساع كم، والعمق كيف"]],"traps":[["Knowing a word's meaning but not its collocations or derived forms = lack of **depth**.","يعرف المعنى فقط = نقص العمق"]],"src":"Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language; Thornbury, How to Teach Vocabulary"},"en.voc.present":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["Presenting and checking meaning","عرض المعنى والتحقق منه"],"body":[["Teachers choose the clearest, quickest way to convey meaning, then check it. Visual techniques suit concrete words; context, examples and definitions suit abstract words; translation can be efficient for a quick check.","نختار الأوضح والأسرع: المرئي للمحسوس، والسياق والتعريف للمجرد، والترجمة للتحقق السريع."]],"pts":[["**Realia** — real objects (a thermos, a ruler, dates).","Realia: الأشياء الحقيقية نفسها"],["**Visuals** — pictures, flashcards, quick drawings; mime and gesture.","صور وبطاقات ورسم وتمثيل"],["**Context and examples** — a sentence or short situation that makes meaning clear.","السياق والأمثلة"],["**CCQs** — short questions that test the core meaning: *starving → a little hungry or extremely hungry?*","أسئلة فحص المفهوم"]],"traps":[["Pictures and drawings are not realia.","الصور والرسوم ليست realia"],["*Do you understand?* is not a good check — learners usually say yes.","«فاهمين؟» ليس تحققاً"]],"src":"Thornbury, How to Teach Vocabulary; Gairns & Redman, Working with Words"},"en.voc.memory":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["How words are remembered","كيف تُحفظ الكلمات"],"body":[["Vocabulary is learned gradually through **repeated encounters**. Each meeting adds a little knowledge, and meetings in different contexts make the word richer and easier to retrieve. Forgetting is fastest soon after learning, so early reviews matter most.","الكلمة تُتعلم تدريجياً بتكرار اللقاء في سياقات مختلفة، والنسيان أسرع بعد التعلم مباشرة فالمراجعة المبكرة أهم."]],"pts":[["**Spaced repetition** — review at increasing intervals (1 day, 3 days, 1 week…).","مراجعة على فترات متزايدة"],["**Retrieval** — trying to recall the meaning (word cards, quizzes) strengthens memory more than re-reading.","الاسترجاع أقوى من إعادة القراءة"],["**Incidental** learning — while focusing on meaning (reading, listening). **Intentional** learning — deliberate study.","عرضي ومقصود"],["**Recycling** — teachers reuse words in later lessons and activities.","إعادة التدوير في دروس لاحقة"]],"traps":[["The factor that helps memory most = meeting the word **many times** in different contexts.","التكرار في سياقات مختلفة"]],"src":"Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language; Schmitt, Vocabulary in Language Teaching"},"en.voc.lexical":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["Collocations, chunks and the Lexical Approach","التلازم والكتل والمدخل المعجمي"],"body":[["Michael Lewis argued that language is largely made of **lexical chunks** — prefabricated multi-word units — rather than single words slotted into grammar rules. Fluent speakers retrieve whole chunks, which is why they speak quickly and naturally.","لويس: اللغة كتل جاهزة متعددة الكلمات، والمتحدث الطليق يستدعيها كاملة."]],"pts":[["**Collocations** — words that habitually co-occur: *make a decision, heavy rain, strong coffee*.","التلازم: كلمات تأتي معاً"],["**Fixed and semi-fixed expressions** — *by the way; Would you mind…?; If I were you, I'd…*","عبارات ثابتة وشبه ثابتة"],["**Idioms** — meaning not deducible from the parts: *break the ice*; closely tied to culture.","التعابير الاصطلاحية مرتبطة بالثقافة"],["Teaching implication: help learners **notice** and record chunks, not just single words.","علّم الطالب ملاحظة الكتل وتدوينها"]],"traps":[["The Lexical Approach is Michael **Lewis**, not Nation.","المدخل المعجمي لمايكل لويس"]],"ex":[["*make a decision*, *heavy rain*, *do homework* are collocations; learners who translate word by word produce odd combinations like *do a mistake*.","الترجمة كلمة بكلمة تنتج تراكيب غريبة مثل do a mistake."]],"src":"Lewis, The Lexical Approach; Thornbury, How to Teach Vocabulary"},"en.voc.strands":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["Nation's four strands","خيوط نيشن الأربعة"],"body":[["Nation proposes that a well-balanced course gives roughly **equal time** to four strands. If one strand is missing, learning becomes unbalanced — for example, lots of explicit study but no fluency work.","المقرر المتوازن يعطي الخيوط الأربعة وقتاً متساوياً تقريباً."]],"pts":[["**Meaning-focused input** — learning through listening and reading (e.g. extensive reading).","مدخلات تركز على المعنى"],["**Meaning-focused output** — learning through speaking and writing.","مخرجات تركز على المعنى"],["**Language-focused learning** — deliberate study of words and features (word cards, affixes).","تعلم يركز على اللغة"],["**Fluency development** — using what is already known faster (4/3/2, speed reading).","تطوير الطلاقة بالمعروف مسبقاً"]],"traps":[["Fluency work uses **easy, familiar** material — no new language.","الطلاقة بمادة سهلة مألوفة"]],"src":"Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language; Nation, What Should Every EFL Teacher Know?"},"en.voc.coverage":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["Lexical coverage and word lists","التغطية المعجمية وقوائم الكلمات"],"body":[["**Lexical coverage** is the percentage of running words in a text that a reader knows. Research suggests about **95%** for adequate comprehension and about **98%** for comfortable, unassisted reading — that is, roughly one unknown word in fifty.","التغطية: نسبة الكلمات المعروفة في النص. نحو 95% للفهم المقبول، و98% للقراءة المريحة."],["Because a small number of words cover most of any text, teachers prioritise **high-frequency** vocabulary: West's **General Service List** (about 2,000 headwords) and, for academic study, Coxhead's **Academic Word List** (570 word families).","عدد قليل من الكلمات يغطي معظم النصوص، فنبدأ بقائمة ويست ثم قائمة كوكسهيد الأكاديمية."]],"pts":[["A **word family** = base word + inflections + derivations: *use, uses, used, useful, useless*.","عائلة الكلمة: الأصل ومشتقاته"]],"traps":[["98% (not 95%) is the figure for comfortable unassisted reading.","98% للقراءة المريحة"],["AWL = 570 word families, by Coxhead.","570 عائلة لكوكسهيد"]],"src":"Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language; Schmitt, Vocabulary in Language Teaching"},"en.voc.corpus":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["Corpora, concordances and data-driven learning","المدونات اللغوية والتعلم من البيانات"],"body":[["A **corpus** is a large collection of real texts stored electronically. A **concordance** shows every line containing a search word, centred on the word (key word in context), so patterns of collocation and grammar become visible at a glance.","المدونة نصوص حقيقية ضخمة، والكونكوردانس يعرض الكلمة في سطورها فيظهر نمطها فوراً."],["In **data-driven learning** (Tim Johns), learners act like researchers: they examine concordance lines and discover rules and patterns themselves — an inductive approach to vocabulary and grammar.","التعلم من البيانات (جونز): الطالب كالباحث يكتشف النمط بنفسه، أسلوب استقرائي."]],"traps":[["Learners discovering *make/do* patterns from corpus lines = data-driven learning.","اكتشاف النمط من سطور المدونة = DDL"]],"pts":[["Learner's (monolingual) dictionaries use a controlled defining vocabulary and example sentences.","قواميس المتعلم الأحادية: مفردات تعريف محدودة وأمثلة"]],"ex":[["The teacher shows twenty corpus lines with *depend* and asks: *Which word always comes after it?* Students discover *depend on* themselves — **data-driven learning**.","عشرون سطراً من مدونة لغوية، والطلاب يكتشفون أن depend يتبعها on: تعلم من البيانات."]],"src":"Johns (1991); O'Keeffe, McCarthy & Carter, From Corpus to Classroom"},"en.wr.approaches":{"k":"Writing","t":["Product, process and genre approaches","مداخل المنتج والعمليات والنوع"],"body":[["The **product approach** is model-based: learners study a model text, practise its features in controlled exercises, then produce a similar text. The focus is the accuracy of the final product.","المنتج: نموذج ← تدريب مضبوط ← نص مشابه، والتركيز على دقة النتيجة."],["The **process approach** focuses on the writer's activities — planning, drafting, feedback, revising — and treats writing as **recursive**. The **genre approach** focuses on text types and their social purposes, making the conventions of each genre explicit.","العمليات: مراحل متكررة. النوع: أعراف كل نوع نصي وغرضه الاجتماعي."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Product","ar":"⁧المنتج⁩","items":[{"n":"Imitate a model","ar":"⁧تقليد نموذج⁩","kw":["accuracy"]}]},{"n":"Process","ar":"⁧العمليات⁩","items":[{"n":"Drafts & feedback","ar":"⁧مسودات وتغذية راجعة⁩","kw":["recursive"]}]},{"n":"Genre","ar":"⁧النوع⁩","items":[{"n":"Text purpose","ar":"⁧غرض النص وأعرافه⁩","kw":["social context"]}]},{"n":"Process-genre","ar":"⁧العملي النوعي⁩","items":[{"n":"Both","who":"Badger & White","ar":"⁧النوع + المراحل⁩"}]}]},"traps":[["Imitating a model text = product approach.","تقليد النموذج = منتج"],["Analysing several real job-application letters and then writing one = genre approach.","تحليل رسائل حقيقية = نوع"]],"ex":[["Product: students copy the structure of a model email to a pen-friend. Process: they brainstorm, draft, exchange drafts and rewrite. Genre: they compare three real job-application letters, list the shared features, then write their own.","تقليد نموذج = منتج. عصف ومسودات وتبادل = عمليات. تحليل رسائل حقيقية ثم الكتابة = نوع."]],"src":"Hyland, Second Language Writing; Badger & White (2000); Harmer, How to Teach Writing"},"en.wr.stages":{"k":"Writing","t":["The writing process","مراحل عملية الكتابة"],"body":[["The stages are usually listed in order, but real writers move back and forth between them — the process is **recursive**.","المراحل تُذكر بالترتيب لكن الكاتب يتنقل بينها ذهاباً وإياباً."]],"pts":[["**Pre-writing** — brainstorming, freewriting, clustering/mind maps, outlining.","قبل الكتابة: عصف وكتابة حرة وخرائط ومخطط"],["**Drafting** — getting ideas down; fluency over accuracy.","المسودة: الأفكار قبل الدقة"],["**Revising** — improving content and organisation using peer and teacher feedback.","المراجعة: المحتوى والتنظيم بملاحظات الآخرين"],["**Editing / proofreading** — grammar, spelling, punctuation.","التدقيق: القواعد والإملاء والترقيم"],["**Publishing** — sharing the final version.","النشر"]],"traps":[["Incorporating peers' and teacher's suggestions = **revising**; correcting spelling and punctuation = **editing/proofreading**.","اقتراحات الآخرين = مراجعة، والإملاء = تدقيق"],["Freewriting is a **pre-writing** technique.","الكتابة الحرة من قبل الكتابة"]],"src":"Harmer, How to Teach Writing; Hedge, Writing"},"en.wr.control":{"k":"Writing","t":["Controlled, guided and free writing","الكتابة المضبوطة والموجهة والحرة"],"body":[["The three types sit on a continuum of support. As learners progress, the teacher gradually removes control over content and form.","الأنواع الثلاثة متدرجة في الدعم، والمعلم يسحب التحكم تدريجياً."]],"pts":[["**Controlled** — content and form fixed: copying, dictation, substitution tables, rewriting a passage in another tense.","مضبوطة: نسخ وإملاء وتغيير الزمن"],["**Guided** — support through an outline, key words, a model, sentence starters or pictures.","موجّهة: مخطط وكلمات ونموذج"],["**Free** — learners choose content and organisation.","حرة: الطالب يختار"],["**Modeled writing** — the teacher writes in front of learners and thinks aloud; the first step with young learners.","الكتابة النموذجية: أول خطوة مع الصغار"],["Give beginners words to build on, and do not comment on every error.","أعطِ المبتدئ كلمات ولا تعلّق على كل خطأ"]],"traps":[["*Rewrite the passage in the past simple* = controlled.","إعادة الكتابة بزمن آخر = مضبوطة"]],"src":"Raimes, Techniques in Teaching Writing; Hedge, Writing"},"en.wr.paragraph":{"k":"Writing","t":["Paragraph and essay structure; coherence vs cohesion","بناء الفقرة والمقال والترابط والتماسك"],"body":[["A paragraph develops **one** main idea: topic sentence → supporting sentences (explanations, examples, details) → concluding sentence. An essay develops one **thesis** through several paragraphs, so the thesis statement is broader than any single topic sentence.","الفقرة فكرة واحدة، والمقال أطروحة واحدة عبر فقرات، فالأطروحة أعمّ من الجملة الموضوعية."],["**Coherence** is about ideas: logical order and clear relevance to the main point. **Cohesion** is about language: the grammatical and lexical links between sentences — transitions, pronouns, repetition, synonyms.","الترابط في الأفكار ومنطقها، والتماسك في أدوات الربط اللغوية."]],"traps":[["In an argumentative essay the thesis usually comes at the **end of the introduction**.","الأطروحة آخر المقدمة"],["Transitional expressions improve coherence (and are cohesive devices).","أدوات الانتقال تحسّن الترابط"]],"src":"Oshima & Hogue, Writing Academic English; Halliday & Hasan, Cohesion in English"},"en.wr.types":{"k":"Writing","t":["Types of writing","أنواع الكتابة"],"body":[["Each type is defined by its **purpose**, so identify the purpose first. Narrative writing is often read for pleasure; expository writing is **subject-oriented**.","حدّد الغرض أولاً ثم النوع. السردي للمتعة غالباً، والتفسيري يدور حول الموضوع."]],"pts":[["**Narrative** — tells a story; chronological order (*first, then, finally*).","سردي: قصة بترتيب زمني"],["**Descriptive** — creates a picture; sensory details, metaphors.","وصفي: تفاصيل حسية واستعارات"],["**Expository** — explains or informs; process (*how to make a milkshake*), cause–effect, comparison.","تفسيري: يشرح ويخبر"],["**Persuasive / argumentative** — takes a position; facts, statistics, expert opinion.","إقناعي: موقف وأدلة"],["**Reflective** — responds to personal experience and feelings (journals).","تأملي: تجربة ومشاعر"],["**Informative** genres — summaries, letters, reports.","إخباري: ملخصات ورسائل وتقارير"]],"traps":[["A 'how to' process paragraph is expository.","كيف تصنع… = تفسيري"]],"src":"Oshima & Hogue, Writing Academic English; Harmer, How to Teach Writing"},"en.wr.wcf":{"k":"Writing","t":["Written corrective feedback","التغذية التصحيحية الكتابية"],"body":[["**Truscott (1996)** claimed grammar correction in L2 writing does not work and may harm, because it draws attention from content and creates anxiety. **Ferris (1999)** answered that the evidence was not enough to abandon it, and that feedback which is clear, selective and consistent can help.","تروسكوت: التصحيح لا يفيد وقد يضر. فيريس: الأدلة لا تكفي للتخلي عنه، والواضح الانتقائي يفيد."]],"pts":[["**Direct** — the teacher writes the correct form.","مباشر: يكتب الصحيح"],["**Indirect** — the error is shown (underline, circle, code) and the learner corrects it.","غير مباشر: يشير فقط"],["**Focused** — one or a few error types; **unfocused** — all errors.","مركّز وشامل"],["**Metalinguistic** — codes or brief rule explanations.","تعليق على القاعدة أو رموز"],["Respond to **content and organisation** on early drafts and to language accuracy on later drafts; require learners to **act on** the feedback (rewrite).","المحتوى أولاً ثم اللغة، والطالب يعيد الكتابة"]],"traps":[["Underlining + codes (*sp, vt*) without the correct form = **indirect**.","خط ورمز بلا تصحيح = غير مباشر"],["Correcting only article errors this week = **focused**.","أدوات التعريف فقط = مركّز"]],"ex":[["This week the teacher only underlines article errors (*a/an/the*) and writes *art* in the margin; students must correct them before resubmitting — **indirect, focused** feedback.","يضع خطاً تحت أخطاء أدوات التعريف فقط ويكتب رمزاً، والطالب يصحّح بنفسه: غير مباشر ومركّز."]],"src":"Truscott (1996); Ferris (1999); Ellis, A typology of written corrective feedback types (2009)"},"en.wr.tlc":{"k":"Writing","t":["Genre teaching and the teaching-learning cycle","مدخل النوع ودورة التعليم والتعلم"],"body":[["Genre-based teaching (from the Sydney School) makes the structure and language of each text type explicit. Its **teaching-learning cycle** gradually hands responsibility from teacher to learner.","مدخل النوع يكشف بنية كل نوع نصي ولغته، ودورته تنقل المسؤولية تدريجياً للطالب."],["Badger and White's **process-genre** approach combines this genre knowledge with the drafting and revising stages of process writing.","المدخل العملي النوعي يدمج معرفة النوع بمراحل المسودة والمراجعة."]],"pts":[["1 **Building the context** — the purpose and situation of the genre.","بناء السياق"],["2 **Modelling and deconstruction** — analysing a model text's stages and language.","النمذجة وتحليل النص"],["3 **Joint construction** — teacher and class write a text together.","الكتابة المشتركة"],["4 **Independent construction** — learners write alone.","الكتابة المستقلة"],["**Peer review** works best with training and a checklist; a **writing conference** is a short one-to-one talk with the teacher about a draft.","مراجعة الأقران بقائمة، ومناقشة فردية قصيرة"],["**Automated writing evaluation (AWE)** and AI tools give instant language feedback; teachers still guide content, check the suggestions and protect **academic integrity**.","الأدوات الآلية مساعدة لا بديل"]],"traps":[["Teacher and class composing a text together on the board = **joint construction**.","الكتابة معاً على السبورة = مشتركة"]],"src":"Hyland, Genre and Second Language Writing; Badger & White (2000); Feez, Text-Based Syllabus Design"},"en.wr.kaplan":{"k":"Writing","t":["Contrastive rhetoric and Arab writers","البلاغة التقابلية والكاتب العربي"],"body":[["Kaplan compared essays by writers from different language backgrounds and suggested that each culture has preferred patterns of organising ideas. He described Arabic-influenced writing as relying on **parallelism** and **coordination**, while English academic writing tends to be linear and to state links explicitly.","كابلان: لكل ثقافة أنماط في تنظيم الأفكار؛ العربية تميل للتوازي والعطف، والإنجليزية خطية بروابط صريحة."],["The idea has been criticised as too simple, but it still helps teachers anticipate common difficulties: long chains joined by *and*, restatement, few full stops, and general statements without specific support.","الفكرة انتُقدت لتبسيطها، لكنها تساعد على توقع الصعوبات: سلاسل بالواو وقلة النقاط والتعميم."]],"pts":[["Remedies: teach sentence boundaries and punctuation, a range of linking words, topic sentences with specific support.","العلاج: حدود الجملة والترقيم والروابط والتفاصيل الداعمة"]],"traps":[["The difficulty is mainly **cohesion and text organisation**, not vocabulary.","المشكلة في التماسك والتنظيم"]],"src":"Kaplan, Cultural Thought Patterns in Intercultural Education (1966); Connor, Contrastive Rhetoric"},"en.gr.dedind":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Deductive vs inductive grammar teaching","التدريس الاستنتاجي والاستقرائي"],"body":[["**Deductive** (rule-driven): the teacher states the rule, gives examples, and learners apply it. It is efficient and respects adult learners' analytical skills, but can be dry and teacher-centred.","الاستنتاجي: القاعدة ثم الأمثلة ثم التطبيق؛ سريع لكنه قد يكون جافاً."],["**Inductive** (discovery): learners meet examples and work out the rule. The mental effort aids memory and independence, but it takes longer and learners may reach a wrong rule — so teachers often use **guided discovery**, leading with focused questions.","الاستقرائي: الأمثلة أولاً والطالب يستخرج القاعدة؛ أرسخ لكنه أبطأ، فنستخدم الاكتشاف الموجّه."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Deductive","ar":"⁧استنتاجي⁩","items":[{"n":"Rule → examples","ar":"⁧القاعدة أولاً⁩","kw":["explicit"]},{"n":"Fast","ar":"⁧سريع وواضح⁩","kw":["adults"]}]},{"n":"Inductive","ar":"⁧استقرائي⁩","items":[{"n":"Examples → rule","ar":"⁧الأمثلة أولاً⁩","kw":["discovery"]},{"n":"Memorable","ar":"⁧أرسخ في الذاكرة⁩","kw":["autonomy"]}]}]},"traps":[["Rule first, examples after = **deductive**.","القاعدة أولاً = استنتاجي"],["Students studying sentences and working out the rule = **inductive**.","يستخرجون القاعدة = استقرائي"]],"src":"Thornbury, How to Teach Grammar; Ur, Grammar Practice Activities"},"en.gr.fmu":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Larsen-Freeman: form, meaning and use","لارسن-فريمان: الشكل والمعنى والاستخدام"],"body":[["Larsen-Freeman pictures grammar as a pie with three slices. **Form** is how a structure is built (spelling, pronunciation, word order). **Meaning** is what it expresses. **Use** is when and why a speaker chooses it rather than an alternative.","القاعدة ثلاثة أبعاد: كيف تُبنى، وماذا تعني، ومتى ولماذا نختارها."],["The challenge for learners is often **use**: they can form the passive or the present perfect correctly but choose it in the wrong situation.","التحدي غالباً في الاستخدام: يعرف التركيب ويستعمله في غير موضعه."]],"ex":[["Present perfect — **form**: have/has + past participle; **meaning**: past action with present relevance; **use**: news (*I've passed!*), experience (*Have you ever…?*), not with *yesterday*.","مثال المضارع التام بأبعاده الثلاثة"]],"traps":[["The three dimensions are Larsen-Freeman's, not Nation's (Nation's form-meaning-use is about words).","أبعاد القاعدة للارسن-فريمان، وأبعاد الكلمة لنيشن"]],"src":"Larsen-Freeman, Teaching Language: From Grammar to Grammaring; Celce-Murcia & Larsen-Freeman, The Grammar Book"},"en.gr.fonf":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Focus on form, focus on forms, consciousness-raising","التركيز على الشكل وعلى الأشكال ورفع الوعي"],"body":[["Long distinguished **focus on forms** — the traditional syllabus that teaches discrete structures one by one — from **focus on form**, brief attention to linguistic form that arises during a meaning-focused, communicative activity.","لونغ: التركيز على الأشكال منهج قواعد منفصلة، والتركيز على الشكل التفات عابر أثناء نشاط تواصلي."],["**Consciousness-raising** (Rutherford, Sharwood Smith, Ellis) helps learners notice and understand a feature, often by analysing data, without requiring immediate production.","رفع الوعي: مساعدة الطالب على ملاحظة الظاهرة وفهمها دون مطالبته بإنتاجها فوراً."]],"pts":[["**Structure-based instruction** — teaching that focuses on the language itself (grammar, vocabulary) rather than on communication.","التعليم القائم على البنية"],["Good practice: grammar in context; non-contextualised drills are weak practice.","القواعد في سياق"]],"traps":[["A lesson that focuses mainly on structures = focus on **forms** (with an s).","تركيز على البنى = forms"],["Brief attention during a communicative task = focus on **form**.","التفات عابر = form"]],"ex":[["While pairs plan a class trip to the Red Sea, many students say *We will going*. The teacher pauses for one minute, writes *We are going to…* / *We will go…* on the board, then lets the planning continue — **focus on form**.","أثناء تخطيط رحلة يتوقف المعلم دقيقة لتصحيح الصيغة ثم يكمل النشاط: تركيز على الشكل."]],"src":"Long (1991); Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned; Ellis, Instructed Second Language Acquisition"},"en.gr.frames":{"k":"Grammar","t":["PPP, TTT and ESA","أطر الدرس: (PPP, TTT, ESA)"],"body":[["**PPP** — Presentation (teacher introduces the form in context, most teacher control) → Practice (controlled activities for accuracy) → Production (free use for fluency). Clear and easy to plan; criticised for assuming learners acquire forms in the order they are taught.","PPP: عرض ← ممارسة مضبوطة ← إنتاج حر. واضح، ويُنتقد بافتراض أن الطالب يكتسب بنفس ترتيب التدريس."],["**TTT** — Test → Teach → Test: a first task reveals what learners already know, the teacher teaches the gaps, a second task checks progress. **ESA** (Harmer) — Engage, Study, Activate: three elements every lesson needs; they can come in a *straight arrow*, *boomerang* or *patchwork* order.","إطار TTT يبدأ بتشخيص ما يعرفه الطلاب، وإطار ESA عند هارمر: شدّ ودراسة وتفعيل، بترتيب مستقيم أو مرتد أو متداخل."]],"traps":[["PPP order: Presentation first, Production last; the teacher has most control in **Presentation**.","العرض أولاً وفيه أعلى تحكم، والإنتاج آخراً"],["The first stage of TTT aims to **find out what students already know**.","أول TTT للتشخيص"]],"ex":[["TTT: students first describe their last holiday in pairs while the teacher listens (**Test**), she teaches the irregular past forms most of them got wrong (**Teach**), then pairs describe a different trip (**Test**).","يصفون عطلة ← يشرح الأفعال الشاذة التي أخطأوا فيها ← يصفون رحلة أخرى: TTT."]],"pts":[["Inside PPP — **Practice**: controlled and semi-controlled activities (drills, gap-fills, matching) with close monitoring and correction; **Production**: role plays and discussions, minimal intervention, delayed feedback.","الممارسة بمراقبة وتصحيح، والإنتاج بأقل تدخل وتصحيح مؤجل"]],"src":"Harmer, How to Teach English; Scrivener, Learning Teaching; Thornbury, How to Teach Grammar"},"en.gr.ccq":{"k":"Grammar","t":["CCQs and ICQs","أسئلة فحص المفهوم والتعليمات"],"body":[["**Concept-checking questions (CCQs)** check that learners understand the **meaning** of new language. They are short, simple, use easier language than the target item, and avoid repeating the target item itself.","أسئلة فحص المفهوم: قصيرة وبسيطة ولا تكرر التركيب المستهدف."],["**Instruction-checking questions (ICQs)** check that learners know **what to do** in a task: *Are you writing or speaking? Alone or in pairs? How many minutes?*","أسئلة فحص التعليمات: هل يعرف الطالب ماذا يفعل؟"]],"pts":[["*If I had a million riyals…* → *Do I have a million riyals now?* (No.)","الشرط الثاني: هل عندي المال الآن؟"],["*He used to smoke* → *Does he smoke now?* (No.) *Did he smoke in the past?* (Yes.)","used to: هل يدخن الآن؟"],["*You mustn't use your phone* → *Is it allowed?* (No.)","mustn't: هل هو مسموح؟"],["**Timelines** make tense meaning visible.","الخط الزمني للأزمنة"],["*He must wear a uniform* → *Does he have a choice?* (No.)","must: هل له خيار؟"],["*He was driving when it rained* → *Which started first?* (Driving.)","أيهما بدأ أولاً؟"]],"traps":[["*Do you understand?* is not a CCQ.","«فاهمين؟» ليس سؤال فحص"]],"src":"Scrivener, Learning Teaching; Thornbury, How to Teach Grammar"},"en.gr.pi":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Noticing, Processing Instruction and input enhancement","الملاحظة ومعالجة المدخلات وإبراز الشكل"],"body":[["Schmidt's **Noticing Hypothesis** holds that learners must consciously notice a form in the input before it can become intake. Several techniques aim to make noticing more likely.","فرضية الملاحظة لشميت: لا يُكتسب الشكل قبل أن يُلاحظ بوعي."],["**Processing Instruction** (VanPatten) gives brief explicit information, then **structured input** activities built so that learners can only interpret meaning correctly by processing the target form — for example, deciding *now or yesterday* from a verb ending. Production comes later.","معالجة المدخلات لفان باتن: شرح موجز ثم مدخلات مُهيكلة لا يُفهم معناها إلا بالانتباه للشكل، والإنتاج لاحقاً."]],"pts":[["**Input enhancement** (Sharwood Smith) — making forms salient: bold, colour, underlining (textual enhancement), or stress in speech.","إبراز الشكل: عريض ولون وخط"],["**Input flood** — a text with many examples of the target form.","الإغراق بالأمثلة"],["**Dictogloss** — hear, note, reconstruct in groups, compare with the original.","إعادة بناء النص جماعياً"]],"traps":[["Printing target verbs in bold = **input enhancement**, not input flood.","الخط العريض = إبراز"],["Processing Instruction is input-based: learners interpret before they produce.","معالجة المدخلات: فهم قبل الإنتاج"]],"ex":[["Structured input: students hear sentences and tick *now* or *yesterday*: *He walked to school* / *He walks to school*. The only clue is the ending **-ed**, so they must notice the form to get the meaning.","يسمعون جملاً ويحددون: الآن أم أمس؟ والدليل الوحيد نهاية ed: مدخلات مُهيكلة."]],"src":"VanPatten, Input Processing and Grammar Instruction; Schmidt (1990); Sharwood Smith (1993)"},"en.gr.cond":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Teaching the conditionals","تدريس الجمل الشرطية"],"body":[["Conditionals combine form and hidden meaning: the second and third conditionals say the opposite of what their words seem to say. That is why teachers rely on situations, timelines and CCQs rather than formulas alone.","الشرط الثاني والثالث يقولان عكس ظاهر الكلمات، فنعتمد على المواقف والأسئلة لا الصيغ وحدها."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Zero","ar":"⁧الصفري⁩","items":[{"n":"If + present, present","ar":"⁧حقائق عامة⁩","kw":["If you heat ice, it melts"]}]},{"n":"First","ar":"⁧الأول⁩","items":[{"n":"If + present, will","ar":"⁧مستقبل محتمل⁩","kw":["If it rains, we'll stay"]}]},{"n":"Second","ar":"⁧الثاني⁩","items":[{"n":"If + past, would","ar":"⁧خيال في الحاضر⁩","kw":["If I had…, I would…"]}]},{"n":"Third","ar":"⁧الثالث⁩","items":[{"n":"If + had done, would have","ar":"⁧خيال في الماضي وندم⁩","kw":["If she had studied…"]}]}]},"pts":[["**Mixed** conditional: past condition, present result — *If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now.*","المختلط: شرط ماضٍ ونتيجة حاضرة"],["Practice ideas: chain stories (*If I go to Abha, I'll… If I…*), *What would you do if…?* discussions, regret stories for the third conditional.","قصص متسلسلة وأسئلة «ماذا لو» وقصص ندم"]],"traps":[["No *will* in the if-clause: *If it rains tomorrow* (not *will rain*).","لا will بعد if"],["*If she had studied, she would have passed* = she did NOT study and did NOT pass.","الثالث: لم تذاكر ولم تنجح"]],"src":"Swan, Practical English Usage; Thornbury, How to Teach Grammar"},"en.gr.transfer":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Typical grammar transfer errors of Arab learners","أخطاء النقل النحوي عند المتعلم العربي"],"body":[["Many systematic errors of Arab learners are **negative transfer** (interference) from Arabic. Recognising the source lets the teacher explain briefly through contrast instead of repeating the rule.","كثير من الأخطاء نقل سلبي من العربية، ومعرفة المصدر تتيح شرحاً سريعاً بالمقارنة."]],"pts":[["**Generic *the*** — *The honesty is important* (Arabic *al-* with abstract/generic nouns).","The مع المجرد والعام"],["**Resumptive pronoun** — *the book that I read it* (Arabic relative clauses keep a returning pronoun).","الضمير العائد في جمل الصلة"],["**Missing copula** — *He a teacher* (Arabic nominal sentences have no verb 'be' in the present).","حذف فعل الكينونة"],["**Present perfect** — no direct equivalent, so learners use the past simple or present instead.","المضارع التام بلا مقابل مباشر"],["**Uncountable nouns** — *informations, advices* (countable in Arabic).","جمع ما لا يُجمع"],["**Run-ons** — clauses joined with *and* (Arabic *wa*).","الجمل المتصلة بالواو"]],"traps":[["These are **interlingual** (transfer) errors, not developmental (intralingual) ones like *goed*.","أخطاء نقل بين لغتين، لا أخطاء نمائية"]],"src":"Swan & Smith, Learner English (Arabic speakers chapter); Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.sla-krashen":{"k":"SLA Theories","t":["Krashen's Monitor Model","نموذج المراقب عند كراشن"],"body":[["Stephen Krashen built the Monitor Model from five linked hypotheses. Its starting point is that **acquisition**, a subconscious process driven by understanding messages, is what produces fluent speech, while **learning**, conscious knowledge of rules, plays a limited role. The model shaped communicative teaching and Krashen and Terrell's **Natural Approach**, which puts comprehension first and lets speech emerge.","خمس فرضيات مترابطة: الاكتساب اللاواعي يصنع الطلاقة، والتعلم الواعي دوره محدود. وأثّر النموذج في التدريس التواصلي وفي المدخل الطبيعي الذي يقدّم الفهم على الكلام."],["Critics such as McLaughlin argued that the line between acquisition and learning cannot be tested, and that learned knowledge can become automatic through practice. Skill Acquisition Theory later developed that second point.","انتُقد لأن الفصل بين الاكتساب والتعلم يصعب اختباره، ولأن المعرفة المتعلَّمة قد تصير آلية بالتدريب."]],"pts":[["**Acquisition–Learning**: subconscious picking up versus conscious study.","الاكتساب والتعلم"],["**Monitor**: learned rules only edit output.","المراقب يحرر الكلام"],["**Natural Order**: structures are acquired in a predictable sequence.","ترتيب متوقع"],["**Input**: acquisition comes from comprehensible input at i+1.","مدخل مفهوم"],["**Affective Filter**: emotions block or admit input.","المصفاة الوجدانية"]],"traps":[["'Picking up an L2 resembles how children develop their L1' points to **Acquisition–Learning**, not the Input Hypothesis.","تشبيه الطفل يعني فرضية الاكتساب والتعلم"],["Acquisition is **subconscious**; learning is **conscious**. Do not swap them.","الاكتساب لا واعٍ والتعلم واعٍ"]],"src":"Krashen, Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.sla-monitor":{"k":"Krashen","t":["The Monitor Hypothesis","فرضية المراقب"],"body":[["The Monitor is the learner's internal **editor**. Krashen argued that it works only when three conditions are met: the speaker has enough **time**, is **focused on form** (correctness) and actually **knows the rule**. In fast conversation these conditions rarely hold together, so the Monitor shows most in writing and in careful, planned speech.","المراقب محرر داخلي لا يعمل إلا بثلاثة شروط: وقت كافٍ، وتركيز على الصحة، ومعرفة القاعدة. لذلك يظهر في الكتابة والكلام المتأني أكثر من المحادثة السريعة."]],"pts":[["**Over-users** check everything, so they speak hesitantly and lack fluency.","المفرطون: كلام متردد بطيء"],["**Under-users** rely on feel and rarely self-correct, even when they know the rule.","المقلّون: نادراً ما يصححون"],["**Optimal users** apply the Monitor when it helps, such as when editing writing, without blocking communication.","المثاليون: يستعملونه عند الحاجة"]],"ex":[["A student rereads an email to her teacher and changes 'He go' to 'He goes'. She has time and is focused on form, so the Monitor can work.","مراجعة الرسالة قبل إرسالها مثال واضح على عمل المراقب."]],"traps":[["'Consciously correcting speech' and 'the device that edits output' both mean the **Monitor**.","التصحيح الواعي يعني المراقب"],["Hesitant, over-careful speakers are **over-users**, not under-users.","المتردد مفرط في المراقبة"]],"src":"Krashen, Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.sla-input":{"k":"Krashen","t":["The Input Hypothesis (i+1)","فرضية المدخل المفهوم"],"body":[["Krashen claims that we acquire language in one way: by understanding messages. Input must be **comprehensible** and pitched slightly beyond the learner's current competence (**i**), which gives **i+1**. Learners understand the extra step thanks to context, pictures and what they already know. Speaking is a result of acquisition, not its cause, so learners may go through a **silent period** before they speak.","نكتسب اللغة بفهم الرسائل. المدخل يجب أن يكون مفهوماً وأعلى قليلاً من الكفاية الحالية، والسياق والصور تساعد على فهم الزيادة. والكلام نتيجة للاكتساب لا سبب له، لذلك قد يمر المتعلم بفترة صمت."]],"pts":[["Good input is **comprehensible**: understood with help from context.","مفهوم"],["Good input is **interesting and relevant**, so learners attend to meaning.","ممتع ومناسب"],["Good input is **not grammatically sequenced**: rich natural input already contains i+1.","غير مرتب نحوياً"],["Good input comes in **sufficient quantity**.","كافٍ في كميته"]],"ex":[["A Grade 8 class reads a graded reader just above their level. Pictures and the familiar story let them understand new phrases from context, which is i+1 in practice.","قصة مبسطة أعلى قليلاً من المستوى، والصور والسياق يكملان الفهم."]],"traps":[["In i+1, **i** is the learner's current **competence**, not output or performance.","i هي الكفاية الحالية"],["'Ambiguous' is not one of Krashen's features of good input; **comprehensible** is.","الغموض ليس من صفات المدخل الجيد"],["The Input Hypothesis concerns **acquisition**, not instruction or training.","تخص الاكتساب"]],"src":"Krashen, The Input Hypothesis; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.sla-filter":{"k":"Krashen","t":["The Affective Filter Hypothesis","فرضية المصفاة الوجدانية"],"body":[["The Affective Filter is an imaginary emotional screen between input and the part of the mind that acquires language. When learners are **anxious**, **shy**, **unmotivated** or low in **self-confidence**, the filter is **up** and even comprehensible input is blocked. When they feel relaxed, motivated and confident, the filter is **down** and input gets through. The idea was first proposed by Dulay and Burt, and Krashen made it one of his five hypotheses.","حاجز عاطفي بين المدخل والاكتساب: يرتفع مع القلق والخجل وضعف الدافعية والثقة فيحجب المدخل، وينخفض مع الراحة والدافعية والثقة فيمر المدخل."]],"pts":[["**Raises** the filter: anxiety, shyness, fear of mistakes, low motivation, peer pressure.","ترفعه: القلق والخجل والخوف من الخطأ"],["**Lowers** the filter: self-confidence, motivation, a relaxed, low-stakes atmosphere.","تخفضه: الثقة والدافعية والجو الآمن"]],"ex":[["A relaxed, personalised warm-up lowers the filter; a surprise graded quiz at the start of the lesson raises it.","النشاط التمهيدي الشخصي يخفضها، والاختبار المفاجئ يرفعها."],["Before a listening task, a Grade 10 teacher in Jeddah chats about the weekend, accepts short answers and does not correct every slip — keeping the **affective filter** low.","حديث خفيف بلا تصحيح متكرر يخفض المصفاة قبل الاستماع."]],"traps":[["Anxiety **raises** the filter. It does not lower it.","القلق يرفع المصفاة"],["Self-confidence is the variable that **helps** acquisition.","الثقة بالنفس تساعد الاكتساب"],["The filter blocks **input** from being acquired; it is not mainly about speaking.","تحجب المدخل"]],"src":"Krashen, Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.sla-interaction":{"k":"After Krashen","t":["Interaction, Output and Noticing","التفاعل والمخرج والملاحظة"],"body":[["**Long's Interaction Hypothesis** keeps Krashen's idea that comprehensible input matters, but adds that conversation is what makes input comprehensible. When a listener does not understand, both speakers adjust through **negotiation of meaning**, and these modifications push acquisition forward.","التفاعل يجعل المدخل مفهوماً: عند سوء الفهم يعدّل المتحدثان كلامهما، وهذا التفاوض يدفع الاكتساب."],["**Swain's Output Hypothesis** came from immersion students in Canada who understood French well but spoke it inaccurately. Producing language forces learners to process grammar, test hypotheses and **notice the gap** between what they want to say and what they can say. **Schmidt's Noticing Hypothesis** adds that input becomes **intake** only when learners consciously notice a feature.","الإنتاج يجبر المتعلم على معالجة القواعد واختبار فرضياته وملاحظة الفجوة. وعند شميت: المدخل لا يصبح مأخوذاً إلا بالملاحظة الواعية."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Long","ar":"⁧فرضية التفاعل⁩","items":[{"n":"Negotiation","ar":"⁧التفاوض على المعنى⁩","kw":["negotiation of meaning"]},{"n":"Modified input","ar":"⁧تعديل الكلام ليُفهم⁩"}]},{"n":"Swain","ar":"⁧فرضية المخرج⁩","items":[{"n":"Notice the gap","ar":"⁧ملاحظة الفجوة⁩","kw":["pushed output"]},{"n":"Hypothesis testing","ar":"⁧تجربة القاعدة في الكلام⁩"}]},{"n":"Schmidt","ar":"⁧فرضية الملاحظة⁩","items":[{"n":"Input → intake","ar":"⁧الملاحظة شرط للأخذ⁩"},{"n":"Attention","ar":"⁧الانتباه الواعي للشكل⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["**Clarification request**: 'Sorry, what do you mean?'","طلب توضيح"],["**Confirmation check**: 'You mean the blue one?'","التحقق من الفهم"],["**Comprehension check**: 'Do you understand?'","التأكد من فهم المستمع"]],"traps":[["Negotiation of meaning = **Long**; noticing gaps through producing language = **Swain**; input must be noticed = **Schmidt**.","التفاوض لونغ، والمخرج سوين، والملاحظة شميت"],["Swain: gaps are noticed most when learners **talk to others**, not when they only listen.","الفجوة تظهر أكثر عند التحدث مع الآخرين"]],"src":"Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.sla-compperf":{"k":"Learner errors","t":["Competence vs Performance","الكفاية والأداء"],"body":[["Chomsky distinguished **competence**, a speaker's underlying knowledge of the language system, from **performance**, the actual use of that knowledge in real time. Performance is affected by memory limits, tiredness, nerves and distraction, so it contains slips even when competence is complete.","الكفاية معرفة المتكلم الداخلية بنظام اللغة، والأداء استعمالها الفعلي الذي تؤثر فيه الذاكرة والتعب والتوتر."],["Corder applied the same idea to learners. An **error** reflects a gap in competence: the learner cannot correct it because he does not know the rule. A **mistake** is a performance slip: the learner knows the rule and can usually correct it himself.","وطبّقها كوردر على المتعلمين: الخطأ المنهجي نقص في الكفاية لا يستطيع المتعلم تصحيحه، والزلة خطأ أداء يصححه بنفسه."]],"pts":[["Knows the 3rd-person rule but says 'He run fast!' → has competence, **lacks performance**.","يعرف القاعدة ويخطئ: أداء"],["Says 'We swimmed' and does not know it is wrong → a **competence** problem.","لا يعرف خطأه: كفاية"],["Slips of the tongue, such as 'I will see you yesterday' → **performance** errors.","زلات اللسان: أداء"]],"traps":[["Self-repair while speaking shows **metalinguistic awareness**; the slip itself is performance, not lack of competence.","التصحيح الذاتي وعي ما وراء لغوي"],["Communicative competence (Hymes) is broader than Chomsky's competence.","الكفاية التواصلية أوسع من كفاية تشومسكي"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Yule, The Study of Language; Corder, The Significance of Learners' Errors"},"en.sla-bics":{"k":"Cummins","t":["BICS and CALP","مهارات التواصل والكفاءة الأكاديمية"],"body":[["Jim Cummins separated two kinds of proficiency. **BICS** (Basic Interpersonal Communicative Skills) is everyday social language: face-to-face, supported by context and cognitively light. It usually develops in about **2 years**. **CALP** (Cognitive Academic Language Proficiency) is the language of school subjects: abstract, less supported by context and cognitively demanding. It takes about **5–7 years**.","لغة التواصل اليومي مدعومة بالسياق وسهلة ذهنياً وتتكون في نحو سنتين. واللغة الأكاديمية مجردة وصعبة ذهنياً وتحتاج من 5 إلى 7 سنوات."]],"pts":[["Fluent chat is not proof of readiness for academic content.","الطلاقة في الحديث لا تعني الجاهزية الأكاديمية"],["Academic language grows through content-based and cross-curricular tasks.","تنمو بالمحتوى والروابط بين المواد"]],"traps":[["CALP takes **5–7 years**; BICS takes about **2**.","الأكاديمية 5–7 سنوات، والتواصلية نحو سنتين"],["Advanced fluency in the SLA stages is the level linked to **CALP**.","الطلاقة المتقدمة ترتبط بالكفاءة الأكاديمية"]],"src":"Cummins, Language, Power and Pedagogy; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.sla-cc":{"k":"Communicative Competence","t":["From Hymes to Canale & Swain","من هايمز إلى كنال وسوين"],"body":[["Dell Hymes introduced **communicative competence** in reaction to Chomsky's narrow notion of competence. For Hymes, a speaker who produced perfect grammar at the wrong moment, or in the wrong tone, would not be competent. Knowing a language includes knowing what is **appropriate** in a given social situation.","هايمز أدخل المصطلح رداً على تشومسكي: من يتكلم بقواعد سليمة في موقف غير مناسب ليس كفئاً، فمعرفة اللغة تشمل معرفة المناسب للموقف."],["Canale and Swain (1980) built the first teaching model with grammatical, sociolinguistic and strategic competence, and Canale (1983) added **discourse** competence, giving the four-part model used today. Later, Bachman (1990) made **pragmatic** competence, understanding the function and intention of utterances, one of the two main branches of language competence, beside organizational competence.","نموذج كنال وسوين بدأ بثلاثة مكونات، ثم أضاف كنال الكفاية الخطابية فصارت أربعة. ولاحقاً جعل باكمان الكفاية التداولية أحد فرعين رئيسيين في نموذجه."]],"pts":[["**Grammatical**: vocabulary, grammar, pronunciation.","قواعدية"],["**Sociolinguistic**: appropriateness to setting, status and purpose.","اجتماعية لغوية"],["**Discourse**: cohesion and coherence across sentences.","خطابية"],["**Strategic**: compensating for gaps and repairing breakdowns.","استراتيجية"],["Hymes: knowing a language also means knowing **when, where and to whom** to say what.","متى وأين ولمن"]],"traps":[["The term was introduced by **Hymes**, not Chomsky or Krashen.","المصطلح لهايمز"],["Understanding the function of an utterance, such as a request, = **pragmatic** competence.","فهم وظيفة العبارة تداولية"],["'An animal that lives in water' for 'fish' = **strategic**, not discourse.","الوصف بدل الكلمة استراتيجية"]],"src":"Hymes, On Communicative Competence; Canale & Swain (1980); Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Richards & Rodgers, Approaches and Methods in Language Teaching","ex":[["A student forgets 'umbrella' and says 'the thing you open when it rains' (**strategic**). Saying 'Give me the paper' to the principal shows a gap in **sociolinguistic** competence.","وصف الكلمة المنسية استراتيجية، والطلب الجاف من المدير ضعف اجتماعي لغوي."]]},"en.sla-order":{"k":"Krashen","t":["The Natural Order Hypothesis","فرضية الترتيب الطبيعي"],"body":[["The hypothesis grew out of the **morpheme order studies**. Roger Brown (1973) found that children acquiring English as an L1 master grammatical morphemes in a similar order. Dulay and Burt (1974) then found a shared order among child L2 learners from different L1 backgrounds, and similar results were reported for adults. Krashen concluded that structures are acquired in a **predictable sequence** that teaching does not change.","جاءت من دراسات ترتيب اكتساب الصرفيات: الأطفال في اللغة الأولى، ثم متعلمو اللغة الثانية من لغات أم مختلفة، يكتسبون التراكيب بترتيب متقارب لا يغيّره التدريس."]],"pts":[["Early: **-ing**, **plural -s**, **copula be**.","مبكرة: -ing والجمع وفعل الكون"],["Middle: **auxiliary be**, **articles**, then **irregular past**.","وسطى: الفعل المساعد وأدوات التعريف والماضي الشاذ"],["Late: **regular past -ed**, **third-person -s**, **possessive 's**.","متأخرة: -ed و-s الغائب والملكية"]],"traps":[["Krashen did **not** recommend teaching in this order; the order is acquired whatever the syllabus.","لا يعني ترتيب التدريس"],["Third-person -s is easy to state but **late** to acquire.","-s الغائب سهلة القاعدة متأخرة الاكتساب"]],"src":"Krashen, Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.sla-skill":{"k":"Cognitive SLA","t":["Skill Acquisition Theory (DeKeyser)","نظرية اكتساب المهارة"],"body":[["Robert DeKeyser, building on Anderson's cognitive psychology, treats language like any complex skill. Learning starts with **declarative** knowledge (knowing *that*: the rule can be stated), becomes **procedural** knowledge (knowing *how*: the rule can be used) through practice, and with long, meaningful practice becomes **automatic**: fast, accurate and needing little attention.","تبدأ بمعرفة تقريرية (أعرف القاعدة)، ثم إجرائية بالتدريب (أستعملها)، ثم آلية بالتدريب الطويل ذي المعنى (أستعملها بسرعة وبلا جهد)."]],"pts":[["Practice is **skill-specific**: practising comprehension improves comprehension more than production, and vice versa.","التدريب خاص بالمهارة"],["Unlike Krashen, the theory says learned knowledge **can** become fluent, automatic use.","بخلاف كراشن: المتعلَّم يمكن أن يصير آلياً"]],"traps":[["The direction is declarative → procedural → automatic, never the reverse.","الاتجاه من التقريرية إلى الإجرائية"],["Automaticity = performing without conscious effort; it is not fossilization.","الآلية ليست تحجراً"]],"src":"DeKeyser, Skill Acquisition Theory (in VanPatten & Williams, Theories in Second Language Acquisition); Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.sla-usage":{"k":"Cognitive SLA","t":["Usage-based Views of Learning","النظرة القائمة على الاستعمال"],"body":[["Usage-based researchers (for example Nick Ellis and Michael Tomasello) argue that no special innate grammar is needed. Learners meet language in use, notice **frequent patterns** and chunks ('Can I have…?', 'I don't know'), and gradually build more general rules from them. **Frequency** of exposure is therefore a key factor.","لا حاجة لقواعد فطرية خاصة: المتعلم يلتقط الأنماط والعبارات الجاهزة المتكررة في الاستعمال ثم يستخلص منها قواعد أعم، فكثرة التعرض عامل أساسي."]],"pts":[["Classroom link: rich input, repeated exposure to useful chunks, and extensive reading and listening.","مدخل غزير وتكرار العبارات المفيدة"]],"traps":[["Usage-based views stand **against** the nativist idea of an innate Universal Grammar.","عكس الفطرية"],["Frequency here means statistical learning from input, not behaviorist habit formation by reinforcement.","ليست السلوكية"]],"src":"Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned; Ellis, The Study of Second Language Acquisition"},"en.sla-schumann":{"k":"Social SLA","t":["Schumann's Acculturation Model","نموذج التثاقف عند شومان"],"body":[["John Schumann (1978) studied Alberto, an adult Costa Rican immigrant in the US whose English stayed simple and pidgin-like. He explained it by **distance** from the English-speaking community: the more a learner stays apart from the target-language group, the less he acquires. Acculturation, adapting to the new culture, is therefore a major factor.","درس شومان مهاجراً بقيت إنجليزيته بسيطة كالهجين، وفسّر ذلك ببعده عن مجتمع اللغة: كلما زاد البعد قلّ الاكتساب."]],"pts":[["**Social distance**: the relation between the two groups, such as dominance, wish to assimilate, cohesiveness, size and intended length of stay.","البعد الاجتماعي بين الجماعتين"],["**Psychological distance**: the individual's language shock, culture shock and motivation.","البعد النفسي عند الفرد"]],"traps":[["Acculturation Model = social and psychological **distance**; investment and identity = Norton.","البعد لشومان، والاستثمار لنورتن"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Ellis, The Study of Second Language Acquisition"},"en.sla-norton":{"k":"Social SLA","t":["Norton — Investment and Identity","نورتن والاستثمار والهوية"],"body":[["Bonny Norton studied immigrant women in Canada who were motivated to learn English but often stayed silent in situations where they felt powerless. She proposed **investment**: learners commit to a language when they expect it to increase their symbolic and material resources (education, jobs, friendships, status), and this is tied to their changing **identity**. A learner can be highly motivated yet not invested in a particular classroom whose practices exclude her.","درست نورتن مهاجرات متحمسات للتعلم لكنهن يصمتن في مواقف يشعرن فيها بالضعف. الاستثمار: يلتزم المتعلم باللغة إذا رأى أنها تزيد موارده كالتعليم والعمل والمكانة، وهذا مرتبط بهويته المتغيرة."]],"pts":[["Investment is a **sociological** complement to the psychological idea of motivation.","مكمل اجتماعي لمفهوم الدافعية"],["**Imagined communities**: groups learners hope to join in future, such as a profession, shape their investment.","المجتمعات المتخيلة"]],"traps":[["Investment is about identity and power, not only instrumental motivation.","الاستثمار هوية وسلطة لا دافعية نفعية فقط"]],"src":"Norton, Identity and Language Learning; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.sla-oxford":{"k":"Learning Strategies","t":["Oxford's Language Learning Strategies (1990)","استراتيجيات التعلم عند أكسفورد"],"body":[["Rebecca Oxford defined learning strategies as specific actions learners take to make learning easier, faster, more enjoyable, more self-directed and more effective. In *Language Learning Strategies: What Every Teacher Should Know* (1990) she grouped them into **direct** strategies, which work with the language itself, and **indirect** strategies, which support and manage learning. Her **SILL** (Strategy Inventory for Language Learning) is a questionnaire that measures how often learners use them.","الاستراتيجيات أفعال يقوم بها المتعلم ليجعل تعلمه أسهل وأسرع وأمتع وأكثر استقلالاً. قسمتها أكسفورد إلى مباشرة تتعامل مع اللغة، وغير مباشرة تدير التعلم، واستبانتها SILL تقيس كثرة استعمالها."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Direct","ar":"⁧المباشرة⁩","items":[{"n":"Memory","ar":"⁧التصنيف والصور والمراجعة⁩"},{"n":"Cognitive","ar":"⁧التدريب والتحليل والتلخيص⁩"},{"n":"Compensation","ar":"⁧التخمين والإيماء والشرح بكلمات أخرى⁩"}]},{"n":"Indirect","ar":"⁧غير المباشرة⁩","items":[{"n":"Metacognitive","ar":"⁧التخطيط والمراقبة والتقويم⁩"},{"n":"Affective","ar":"⁧خفض القلق وتشجيع النفس⁩"},{"n":"Social","ar":"⁧طلب التوضيح والتعاون⁩"}]}]},"pts":[["O'Malley and Chamot (1990) use three groups instead: metacognitive, cognitive and social/affective.","تصنيف آخر بثلاث فئات"],["Direct: **memory** (grouping, associating, images and rhymes, reviewing), **cognitive** (practising, analysing, note-taking, summarising), **compensation** (guessing from context, gestures, synonyms, circumlocution).","ذاكرة، معرفية، تعويضية"],["Indirect: **metacognitive** (planning, organising, self-monitoring, self-evaluating), **affective** (lowering anxiety, self-encouragement, rewarding oneself), **social** (asking for clarification, cooperating with peers, cultural empathy).","ما وراء معرفية، وجدانية، اجتماعية"]],"traps":[["Compensation strategies are **direct**; metacognitive strategies are **indirect**.","التعويضية مباشرة، وما وراء المعرفية غير مباشرة"],["Planning and self-evaluation = **metacognitive**; asking for clarification = **social**.","التخطيط ما وراء معرفي، وطلب التوضيح اجتماعي"]],"src":"Oxford, Language Learning Strategies: What Every Teacher Should Know; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.l17-ca":{"k":"Learner Language","t":["Contrastive Analysis and Transfer","التحليل التقابلي والنقل"],"body":[["Robert Lado's *Linguistics Across Cultures* (1957) proposed that L2 difficulty can be predicted by comparing the learner's L1 with the target language: where they are similar, learning is easy (positive transfer); where they differ, errors appear (negative transfer or interference). The idea fitted **behaviorism**, which saw language as habits and L1 habits as getting in the way of new ones.","لادو: المقارنة بين اللغتين تتنبأ بالصعوبة؛ التشابه يسهّل والاختلاف يسبب التداخل. والفكرة منسجمة مع السلوكية التي ترى اللغة عادات."],["The **strong version** (predict all errors in advance; Wardhaugh's term, 1970) failed: many predicted errors never appeared, and many real errors had nothing to do with the L1. A **weak version** survives: comparing languages helps explain errors after they occur, especially in pronunciation.","الصيغة القوية التي تتنبأ بكل الأخطاء فشلت، وبقيت الصيغة الضعيفة: المقارنة تفسر الأخطاء بعد وقوعها، خاصة في النطق."]],"pts":[["Adjective order: 'a pen blue', 'a smile beautiful'.","ترتيب الصفة بعد الاسم"],["Resumptive pronoun: 'the man whom I met him'.","ضمير زائد في جملة الصلة"],["Time expression: 'five years before' for 'five years ago'.","قبل بمعنى ago"],["Missing verb 'to be': 'He a teacher'.","حذف فعل الكينونة"],["Sound confusion: /p/ and /b/ ('park' / 'bark').","الخلط بين p و b"]],"traps":[["Contrastive Analysis is linked to **behaviorism**, not cognitivism or nativism.","مرتبط بالسلوكية"],["L1 influence is usually **most noticeable in pronunciation** (foreign accent).","أثر اللغة الأم أوضح في النطق"],["Similar structures produced correctly = **positive** transfer.","التشابه نقل إيجابي"]],"src":"Lado, Linguistics Across Cultures; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Swan & Smith, Learner English","ex":[["Positive transfer: an Arabic speaker easily produces 'The cat jumped over the fence'. Negative transfer: 'pen blue' follows Arabic noun–adjective order.","التشابه يسهّل، والاختلاف يسبب الخطأ."]]},"en.l17-ea":{"k":"Learner Language","t":["Error Analysis","تحليل الأخطاء"],"body":[["Pit Corder's 1967 paper on the significance of learners' errors changed how teachers see mistakes. Errors are useful three times over: they show the **teacher** how far the learner has come, show the **researcher** how languages are learned, and are the **learner's** own way of testing hypotheses about the new language.","كوردر: الأخطاء مفيدة للمعلم ليعرف تقدم الطالب، وللباحث ليفهم التعلم، وللمتعلم نفسه ليختبر فرضياته."],["Error Analysis classifies errors by source. **Interlingual** errors come from the L1. **Intralingual** errors come from the target language itself: **overgeneralization** ('goed', 'childs'), simplification and incomplete application of rules. Developmental errors follow similar paths for learners of any L1, and learners who share an L1 add the same transfer errors, so a class of Arab learners tends to show very similar errors.","الأخطاء نوعان: من اللغة الأم، ومن داخل اللغة الهدف كالتعميم الزائد. وأخطاء النمو متشابهة عند كل المتعلمين، ومن يشتركون في اللغة الأم يضيفون أخطاء النقل نفسها، فتتشابه أخطاء الطلاب العرب كثيراً."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Interlingual","ar":"⁧من اللغة الأم⁩","items":[{"n":"Transfer","ar":"⁧نقل سلبي من العربية⁩","kw":["pen blue","five years before"]}]},{"n":"Intralingual","ar":"⁧من اللغة الهدف⁩","items":[{"n":"Overgeneralization","ar":"⁧تعميم القاعدة⁩","kw":["goed","tooths","childs"]},{"n":"Simplification","ar":"⁧تبسيط التركيب⁩","kw":["He go"]}]}]},"pts":[["Steps: collect samples → identify errors → describe → explain the source → evaluate seriousness.","اجمع، حدد، صِف، فسّر، قيّم"]],"traps":[["Errors like 'rided' and 'goed' **mean the student is learning**; they are not failure.","أخطاء النمو علامة تعلم"],["Teachers should analyse **errors** to find inappropriate L1 transfer.","حلل الأخطاء لتكتشف النقل"],["Error caused by the target language itself = **intralingual**, not interlingual.","من داخل اللغة الهدف"]],"src":"Corder, The Significance of Learners' Errors; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Ellis, The Study of Second Language Acquisition","ex":[["'Yesterday we swimmed and eated kabsa': the learner has found the -ed rule and applies it too widely — **intralingual** overgeneralization that shows learning.","تعميم قاعدة -ed خطأ من داخل اللغة يدل على التعلم."]]},"en.l17-il":{"k":"Learner Language","t":["Interlanguage, Fossilization and Avoidance","اللغة البينية والتحجر والتجنب"],"body":[["Larry Selinker (1972) used **interlanguage** for the system a learner builds on the way to the target language. It is **systematic**, because it has its own rules, and **dynamic**, because it keeps changing as new input arrives. Selinker linked it to processes such as L1 transfer, transfer of training, learning and communication strategies, and overgeneralization.","اللغة البينية نظام يبنيه المتعلم في طريقه إلى اللغة الهدف: منتظم له قواعده، ومتغير مع كل مدخل جديد."],["**Fossilization** happens when some interlanguage forms stop developing and resist correction, often in pronunciation and especially in adult learners. Lack of feedback and the fact that the message still gets across both help errors settle. **Avoidance** is harder to spot: learners simply do not use structures they find difficult, as Schachter showed with relative clauses, so the gap appears as something missing rather than as an error.","التحجر توقف بعض الأخطاء عن التطور ومقاومتها للتصحيح، ويساعد عليه غياب التغذية الراجعة ونجاح التواصل رغم الخطأ. والتجنب ترك التراكيب الصعبة، فيظهر غياباً لا خطأً."]],"pts":[["Interlanguage sits **between** L1 and L2; it is not a pidgin or a creole.","بين اللغتين وليست هجيناً"],["Negative transfer repeated without noticing or feedback → **fossilization**.","النقل السلبي المتكرر قد ينتهي بالتحجر"],["Loss of a language through disuse or the influence of another = **attrition**, not fossilization.","الفقد بقلة الاستعمال تآكل"]],"traps":[["Errors that become permanent and stop developing = **fossilization**.","الخطأ الدائم تحجر"],["A learner who never uses structures unlike Arabic to avoid being laughed at = **avoidance**.","ترك التركيب خوفاً من السخرية تجنب"]],"src":"Selinker, Interlanguage; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Lightbown & Spada, How Languages are Learned"},"en.l17-we":{"k":"TESOL Trends","t":["World Englishes, ELF and Kachru's Circles","الإنجليزيات العالمية والدوائر الثلاث"],"body":[["Braj Kachru described the spread of English as three concentric circles. The **inner circle** (UK, US, Australia) uses English as a first language and provides norms. The **outer circle** (India, Nigeria, Singapore) uses English as an official or second language with its own developing norms. The **expanding circle** (Saudi Arabia, China, Japan) learns English as a foreign language. From this view, Indian or Nigerian English are legitimate varieties, not failed British English.","كاتشرو: الدائرة الداخلية لغتها الأم الإنجليزية، والخارجية تستعملها لغة رسمية أو ثانية، والمتسعة تتعلمها لغة أجنبية. والتنوعات الهندية والنيجيرية مشروعة لا أخطاء."],["**English as a Lingua Franca** research (Jenkins, Seidlhofer) starts from the fact that most English conversations today happen between non-native speakers. The goal is **mutual intelligibility**, so teaching focuses on the pronunciation features that most affect understanding, and on accommodating to the listener.","أبحاث الإنجليزية المشتركة: أغلب المحادثات بين غير الناطقين، فالهدف الفهم المتبادل والتركيز على ما يؤثر في الوضوح."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Inner circle","ar":"⁧الدائرة الداخلية⁩","items":[{"n":"UK · US · Australia","ar":"⁧لغة أولى تضع المعايير⁩","kw":["ENL"]}]},{"n":"Outer circle","ar":"⁧الدائرة الخارجية⁩","items":[{"n":"India · Nigeria","ar":"⁧لغة رسمية أو ثانية⁩","kw":["ESL"]}]},{"n":"Expanding circle","ar":"⁧الدائرة المتسعة⁩","items":[{"n":"Saudi Arabia · China","ar":"⁧لغة أجنبية⁩","kw":["EFL"]}]}]},"traps":[["Saudi Arabia is in the **expanding** circle.","السعودية في الدائرة المتسعة"],["ELF aims at **intelligibility**, not a native-like accent or perfect grammar.","الوضوح لا اللكنة الأصلية"],["Competent teachers make students **aware of varieties** of English rather than hiding them.","التوعية بالتنوعات"]],"src":"Kachru, The Other Tongue; Jenkins, The Phonology of English as an International Language; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.l17-icc":{"k":"TESOL Trends","t":["Byram's Intercultural Communicative Competence","كفاءة التواصل بين الثقافات عند بايرام"],"body":[["Michael Byram argued that the aim of language teaching is not to turn learners into imitation native speakers but into **intercultural speakers**: people who can see relationships between cultures, mediate between them and reflect on their own culture as well as others'. His model of intercultural communicative competence has five components.","بايرام: هدف تعليم اللغة متحدث بين الثقافات يفهم العلاقة بينها ويتوسط ويتأمل ثقافته وثقافة غيره، لا ناطق أصلي مقلَّد."],["The five components are often called Byram's **savoirs** (French for 'kinds of knowing').","وتُسمّى المكونات الخمسة savoirs."]],"pts":[["**Attitudes**: curiosity and openness, readiness to suspend judgement.","الفضول والانفتاح"],["**Knowledge**: of social groups and practices in one's own and other cultures.","معرفة المجتمعات"],["**Skills of interpreting and relating**: explaining a document or event from another culture.","التفسير والربط"],["**Skills of discovery and interaction**: learning new cultural knowledge in real time.","الاكتشاف والتفاعل"],["**Critical cultural awareness**: evaluating practices in one's own and other cultures on clear criteria.","الوعي الثقافي النقدي"]],"ex":[["Students compare wedding customs in Saudi Arabia and Japan, explain the reasons behind each and discuss what a visitor might misunderstand.","مقارنة عادات الزواج وتفسيرها وتوقع سوء الفهم تبني هذه الكفاءة."]],"traps":[["The ideal outcome is the **intercultural speaker**, not the native speaker.","المتحدث بين الثقافات"],["ICC adds culture to communicative competence; it does not replace language skills.","تضيف الثقافة ولا تلغي اللغة"]],"src":"Byram, Teaching and Assessing Intercultural Communicative Competence; Kramsch, Language and Culture"},"en.l17-postmethod":{"k":"TESOL Trends","t":["Post-method Pedagogy and the Native-speaker Debate","ما بعد الطريقة وقضية المعلم الناطق"],"body":[["B. Kumaravadivelu argued that the search for one best method had failed, because no method fits every context. Teachers should instead build their own practice from three parameters, **particularity** (the local context), **practicality** (teacher-generated theory) and **possibility** (learners' social and political realities), guided by ten **macrostrategies**. Concrete classroom techniques, the **microstrategies**, are derived from these principles.","كوماراڤاديفيلو: لا توجد طريقة مثلى لكل سياق. المعلم يبني ممارسته من خصوصية سياقه وتجربته وواقع طلابه، بإرشاد عشر استراتيجيات كبرى تُشتق منها الأساليب الصفية."],["Adrian Holliday named **native-speakerism**, the ideology that native speakers are the best models and teachers. Péter Medgyes showed that non-native teachers bring their own strengths and are not second-best.","هوليداي سمّى الانحياز للناطق الأصلي، وميدجيز بيّن مزايا المعلم غير الناطق."]],"pts":[["Macrostrategies: maximise learning opportunities; minimise perceptual mismatches; facilitate negotiated interaction; promote learner autonomy; foster language awareness.","استراتيجيات كبرى 1–5"],["Macrostrategies: activate intuitive heuristics; contextualise linguistic input; integrate language skills; ensure social relevance; raise cultural consciousness.","استراتيجيات كبرى 6–10"],["Medgyes: non-native teachers are models of successful learners, teach learning strategies, give information about the language, **anticipate difficulties**, show empathy and can use the **shared L1**.","مزايا المعلم غير الناطق"]],"traps":[["General principles like 'promote learner autonomy' = **macrostrategies**.","المبادئ العامة استراتيجيات كبرى"],["Native-speakerism is **Holliday's** term.","المصطلح لهوليداي"],["Post-method is principled, not 'anything goes': techniques must follow from the macrostrategies and the context.","ليس عشوائياً"]],"src":"Kumaravadivelu, Beyond Methods; Medgyes, The Non-native Teacher; Holliday, Native-speakerism"},"en.l17-reflect":{"k":"Teacher Development","t":["Reflective Teaching and Action Research","التدريس التأملي والبحث الإجرائي"],"body":[["Donald Schön described professionals as people who think while they work. **Reflection-in-action** is the quick thinking that changes a lesson as it happens. **Reflection-on-action** is the slower look back afterwards, often in a journal or a discussion with a colleague. Tools for reflection include teaching journals, audio or video recordings, peer observation, student feedback and portfolios.","شون: التأمل أثناء الفعل يغيّر الحصة وهي تجري، والتأمل بعد الفعل مراجعة لاحقة. وأدواته اليوميات والتسجيل وملاحظة الزملاء وآراء الطلاب وملف الإنجاز."],["**Action research** turns reflection into a cycle. Kemmis and McTaggart describe it as **plan → act → observe → reflect**, then a new plan. It is done by the teacher, in her own classroom, on a real local problem, and the results feed straight back into practice.","البحث الإجرائي دورة: خطط، نفّذ، لاحظ، تأمّل، ثم خطة جديدة. ينفذه المعلم في صفه على مشكلة حقيقية، ونتائجه تعود مباشرة إلى تدريسه."]],"pts":[["5 R's: **Reporting** (what happened) → **Responding** (feelings) → **Relating** (own experience) → **Reasoning** (theory) → **Reconstructing** (new plan).","المراحل الخمس للتأمل"],["Peer observation is for learning and feedback, not for evaluation.","ملاحظة الزميل للتعلم لا للتقييم"]],"traps":[["Changing an activity mid-lesson = reflection-**in**-action.","التعديل وسط الحصة أثناء الفعل"],["Explaining an event through a theory such as the ZPD = **Reasoning**.","التفسير بالنظرية Reasoning"],["Action research is small-scale and classroom-based, not a large experimental study.","بحث صغير داخل الصف"]],"src":"Schön, The Reflective Practitioner; Richards & Lockhart, Reflective Teaching in Second Language Classrooms; Burns, Doing Action Research in English Language Teaching","ex":[["Mr. Khalid's Grade 9 students rarely speak in groups. He introduces role cards (plan, act), counts who speaks for two weeks (observe), finds one leader still dominates (reflect) and adds a turn-taking rule: **action research**.","مشكلة في صفه، تجربة حل، بيانات، تأمل وتعديل."],["5 R's journal: 'Only three students spoke (Reporting). I felt frustrated (Responding). The same happened in my first year (Relating). The topic needed vocabulary beyond their ZPD (Reasoning). Next time I will pre-teach key phrases (Reconstructing).'","التفسير بالنظرية هو Reasoning."]]},"en.l17-culture":{"k":"TESOL and Culture","t":["Culture in the English Classroom","الثقافة في درس الإنجليزية"],"body":[["Language and culture are tied together, so every coursebook carries cultural content. Cortazzi and Jin described three choices: **source culture** (the learners' own), **target culture** (countries where English is a first language) and **international culture** (the many settings where English is used). Starting from source culture makes new content familiar before learners compare it with others.","كل كتاب يحمل محتوى ثقافياً: ثقافة المتعلم، أو ثقافة اللغة الهدف، أو ثقافات دولية. والبدء بثقافة المتعلم يسهّل المقارنة."],["The **Sapir–Whorf hypothesis** (linguistic relativity) holds that language shapes thought. Its strong version, that language **determines** thought, is largely rejected; its weak version, that language **influences** perception and memory, has some support. Robert Phillipson's **linguistic imperialism** argues that the dominance of English can maintain structural and cultural inequality.","النسبية اللغوية: الصيغة القوية (اللغة تحدد التفكير) مرفوضة غالباً، والضعيفة (اللغة تؤثر في الإدراك) لها دعم. والإمبريالية اللغوية ترى أن هيمنة الإنجليزية قد تكرّس عدم المساواة."]],"pts":[["**Big C**: literature, art, history, institutions.","الثقافة الكبرى"],["**Little c**: greetings, food, routines, gestures, values.","الثقافة الصغرى"],["Culture shock stages: honeymoon → frustration → adjustment → adaptation.","مراحل الصدمة الثقافية"],["A **stereotype** is an oversimplified belief about a group; teachers should challenge it.","الصورة النمطية"]],"traps":[["Shakespeare = **Big C**; how people greet = **little c**.","شكسبير كبرى والتحية صغرى"],["A unit on Jeddah's historic district = **source** culture.","جدة التاريخية ثقافة المصدر"],["Start from **familiar local** culture, then move to the unfamiliar.","ابدأ بالمألوف"]],"src":"Cortazzi & Jin, Cultural Mirrors; Kramsch, Language and Culture; Phillipson, Linguistic Imperialism; Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.l17-bilingual":{"k":"Bilingualism","t":["Bilingualism, Code-switching and Diglossia","الثنائية والتناوب والازدواجية"],"body":[["A **bilingual** person can use two languages. **Code-switching** is moving between them within one conversation or even one sentence ('Okay, yalla, let's start'). Research shows that it is normal and rule-governed: speakers switch to fill a gap, add emphasis, quote someone or show shared identity. It is not in itself a sign of weak proficiency.","ثنائي اللغة يستعمل لغتين، والتناوب الانتقال بينهما في الحديث نفسه. وهو سلوك طبيعي له قواعد: لسد فجوة أو للتأكيد أو لإظهار الانتماء، ولا يدل بذاته على ضعف."],["**Diglossia** (Ferguson, 1959) is different: one community uses two varieties of the **same** language for different functions, a 'high' variety for formal uses and a 'low' one for daily talk, as with Fusha and colloquial Arabic.","الازدواجية: صيغتان للغة نفسها لكل منهما وظيفة، رفيعة للرسمي ودارجة للحياة اليومية، كالفصحى والعامية."]],"pts":[["**EFL**: English learned where it is not the community language (Saudi Arabia); **ESL**: English needed in daily life around the learner.","لغة أجنبية / لغة ثانية"]],"traps":[["Switching between Arabic and English in one conversation = **code-switching**, not diglossia.","التناوب بين لغتين غير الازدواجية"],["Fusha and colloquial Arabic = **diglossia**, not bilingualism.","الفصحى والعامية ازدواجية"]],"src":"Ferguson, Diglossia; Wardhaugh, An Introduction to Sociolinguistics; Yule, The Study of Language"},"en.l17-translang":{"k":"TESOL Trends","t":["Translanguaging","توظيف المخزون اللغوي كاملاً"],"body":[["The term began in Welsh bilingual classrooms (Cen Williams, 1994), where input came in one language and output in the other. Ofelia García and Li Wei developed it into a view of bilinguals as having **one integrated linguistic repertoire**, not two separate languages. In class, learners use all their languages on purpose, for example reading or discussing in Arabic and then presenting or writing in English.","بدأ المصطلح في الصفوف الثنائية في ويلز، ثم طوّرته أوفيليا غارسيا ولي وي: للمتعلم مخزون لغوي واحد متكامل، ويوظفه كله عن قصد، كأن يناقش بالعربية ثم يعرض بالإنجليزية."]],"pts":[["Code-switching describes moving between two languages; translanguaging is also a **teaching approach** that plans for using the whole repertoire.","التناوب وصف، والتوظيف الكامل أسلوب تدريس مخطط"],["Aim: deeper understanding and confidence, with the English output as the goal.","الهدف فهم أعمق والناتج بالإنجليزية"]],"traps":[["Translanguaging is a planned use of L1 as a resource, not **negative transfer** and not Grammar-Translation.","ليس نقلاً سلبياً ولا ترجمة"]],"src":"García & Li Wei, Translanguaging: Language, Bilingualism and Education; Baker, Foundations of Bilingual Education and Bilingualism"},"en.l18-hockett":{"k":"Language as a System","t":["Hockett's Design Features","خصائص هوكيت للغة البشرية"],"body":[["Charles Hockett compared human language with animal systems such as bee dances, bird song and primate calls, and listed the features that a full human language has. Some features are shared with animals, such as the vocal-auditory channel, rapid fading and semanticity. Others appear fully only in human language: duality, prevarication and reflexiveness, together with unlimited productivity, displacement and cultural transmission. Animals show these last three only in a very limited form: bees can signal where food is, but cannot talk about yesterday's flowers.","قارن هوكيت لغة الإنسان بتواصل الحيوان. بعض الخصائص مشتركة كالقناة الصوتية والزوال السريع، وبعضها لا يكتمل إلا في لغة الإنسان كالازدواجية والكذب والانعكاسية، والإبداعية والإزاحة والانتقال الثقافي لا تظهر عند الحيوان إلا محدودة جداً، فالنحل مثلاً إزاحته محدودة."]],"pts":[["**Arbitrariness**: no natural link between form and meaning.","الاعتباطية"],["**Displacement**: talking about things absent in time or space.","الإزاحة"],["**Productivity / creativity**: endless new sentences from finite means.","الإبداعية"],["**Duality of patterning**: sounds (no meaning) → morphemes and words (meaning).","الازدواجية"],["**Cultural transmission**: learned from the community, not inherited.","الانتقال الثقافي"],["**Discreteness**: sounds are separate units; changing one changes the word (pin / bin).","التمايز"]],"traps":[["Generalization, receptiveness, imitation and translation are **not** design features.","ليست من الخصائص"],["Displacement = absent things; productivity = new messages. Do not swap them.","الإزاحة غير الإبداعية"],["Duality = two levels of structure; discreteness = distinct units.","الازدواجية غير التمايز"]],"src":"Hockett, The Origin of Speech; Yule, The Study of Language","ex":[["Students talk about a dream trip to Japan next summer — a place and time that are not here and now. Birds and cats cannot do this: **displacement**. ('tree' and 'shajara' name the same thing: **arbitrariness**.)","الحديث عن رحلة لم تحدث بعد: إزاحة."]]},"en.l18-functions":{"k":"Language as a System","t":["Transactional and Interactional Language","الوظيفة التبادلية والتفاعلية"],"body":[["Brown and Yule separated two broad functions. **Transactional** language is message-oriented: its purpose is to pass on information, so clarity and accuracy of content matter most. **Interactional** language is listener-oriented: its purpose is to establish and maintain social relationships, so friendliness and agreement matter most.","التبادلية موجهة للرسالة وغرضها نقل المعلومة بوضوح، والتفاعلية موجهة للمستمع وغرضها بناء العلاقة والود."],["Malinowski called talk whose main purpose is social contact **phatic communion**: greetings, weather talk and polite small talk. It carries little information, but it keeps relationships running.","سمّى مالينوفسكي كلام المجاملة الاجتماعية التواصل الاتصالي: تحيات وحديث عن الطقس، قليل المعلومة كثير الأثر في العلاقات."]],"pts":[["Giving directions, explaining a process, a news report → transactional.","أمثلة تبادلية"],["Greetings, compliments, small talk → interactional / phatic.","أمثلة تفاعلية"]],"traps":[["Passing on information = **transactional**, not interactional, international or translational.","نقل المعلومة تبادلية"],["'Nice weather, isn't it?' = **phatic** communication.","المجاملة اتصالية"]],"src":"Brown & Yule, Discourse Analysis; Yule, The Study of Language"},"en.l18-saussure":{"k":"Structural Linguistics","t":["Ferdinand de Saussure","فرديناند دي سوسير"],"body":[["Saussure moved linguistics from the history of words to the study of language as a **system** in which each unit gets its value from its relations with other units. His lectures were published by his students as the *Course in General Linguistics* (1916), after his death.","نقل سوسير اللسانيات من تاريخ الكلمات إلى دراسة اللغة نظاماً تأخذ فيه كل وحدة قيمتها من علاقتها بغيرها. ونشر طلابه محاضراته بعد وفاته."],["He described four pairs that are still basic to linguistics. Saussure himself called the paradigmatic relation 'associative'; the term 'paradigmatic' came later.","وضع أربع ثنائيات أساسية، وكان يسمي العلاقة الرأسية «ترابطية» ثم شاع اسم «استبدالية»."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Linguistic sign","ar":"⁧العلامة اللغوية⁩","items":[{"n":"Signifier","ar":"⁧الصورة الصوتية أو الشكل⁩","kw":["/triː/"]},{"n":"Signified","ar":"⁧المفهوم⁩","kw":["the idea of a tree"]}]},{"n":"Langue / Parole","ar":"⁧اللسان والكلام⁩","items":[{"n":"Langue","ar":"⁧النظام المشترك في المجتمع⁩"},{"n":"Parole","ar":"⁧الكلام الفعلي للأفراد⁩"}]},{"n":"Time","ar":"⁧الزمن⁩","items":[{"n":"Synchronic","ar":"⁧اللغة في لحظة واحدة⁩"},{"n":"Diachronic","ar":"⁧تطور اللغة عبر الزمن⁩"}]},{"n":"Relations","ar":"⁧العلاقات⁩","items":[{"n":"Syntagmatic","ar":"⁧أفقية: تتابع الكلمات⁩","kw":["the + cat + sat"]},{"n":"Paradigmatic","ar":"⁧رأسية: الاستبدال في الموقع⁩","kw":["cat / dog / bird"]}]}]},"traps":[["The sound-image /triː/ = **signifier**; the concept 'tree' = **signified**.","الصورة الصوتية دال"],["Studying English as used today, without its history = **synchronic**.","دراسة اللغة اليوم تزامنية"],["Words that can replace each other in one slot = **paradigmatic**.","الاستبدال علاقة رأسية"],["Langue is social and shared; Chomsky's competence is individual knowledge. They are similar but not identical.","اللسان اجتماعي والكفاية فردية"]],"src":"Saussure, Course in General Linguistics; Yule, The Study of Language; Crystal, A Dictionary of Linguistics and Phonetics","pts":[["**Linguistic sign** = signifier (sound-image, /triː/) + signified (concept 'tree'), joined arbitrarily.","الدال + المدلول"],["**Synchronic** = a language at one point in time; **diachronic** = its change over time.","تزامني / تعاقبي"],["**Syntagmatic** = horizontal relations in sequence (the + cat + sat); **paradigmatic** = vertical relations of substitution in one slot (cat / dog / bird).","أفقية / رأسية"]],"ex":[["Substitution table (I / You / We + like / love / hate + tea / juice / dates): each column holds **paradigmatic** choices; each row read left to right is a **syntagmatic** chain.","الأعمدة رأسية، والصف أفقي."]]},"en.l18-origins":{"k":"Language Origins","t":["Theories of the Origin of Language","نظريات نشأة اللغة"],"body":[["Speech leaves no fossils, so the beginnings of language cannot be observed. Each theory picks one plausible source and stretches it; none can be proved. The **divine source** was even tested with isolation experiments on infants, reported since ancient times, but children who grow up without hearing language simply do not develop one.","الكلام لا يترك أثراً مادياً، فكل نظرية تخمين. وحتى تجارب عزل الأطفال لاختبار المصدر الإلهي لم تثبت شيئاً، فالطفل الذي لا يسمع لغة لا يتكلم."],["The **physical adaptation** view looks for evidence in the body. Human teeth are upright, lips are muscular and flexible, the mouth is small and the tongue agile, and the larynx sits lower than in other primates, creating a longer pharynx that works as a resonator. The human brain is **lateralised**, with language functions mainly in the left hemisphere, and the **tool-making** view links language to the same brain functions used for manipulating objects.","التكيف الجسدي: أسنان قائمة، وشفاه مرنة، وفم صغير، ولسان مرن، وحنجرة منخفضة تطيل البلعوم. والدماغ متخصص الجانبين، واللغة في الأيسر غالباً، وترتبط بالوظائف نفسها التي تدير صنع الأدوات."]],"pts":[["**Bow-wow** (natural sound): imitation of natural sounds; problem: few onomatopoeic words, and soundless or abstract things cannot be named this way.","محاكاة الطبيعة"],["**Pooh-pooh**: instinctive emotional cries; problem: such cries use sounds unlike ordinary speech.","صيحات الانفعال"],["**Yo-heave-ho** (social interaction): sounds of group work; explains the social setting, not the sounds.","العمل الجماعي"],["**Oral-gesture** (Paget): mouth and tongue movements copy hand gestures.","الإيماءة الفموية"],["**Genetic source** (innateness): humans are born with a capacity for language.","المصدر الوراثي"]],"traps":[["The main problem with all origin theories: they **lack scientific evidence**.","لا دليل علمي"],["'cuckoo', 'splash' = **bow-wow**; 'Ouch!' = **pooh-pooh**.","المحاكاة والانفعال"],["Teeth, lips, larynx and a lateralised brain = **physiological adaptation**, not the genetic source.","الأعضاء تكيف جسدي"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language","ex":[["The teacher drops a book (bang!), pretends to touch a hot cup (Ouch!) and pulls a desk with two students ('one, two, heave!'): bow-wow, pooh-pooh and yo-heave-ho.","ثلاثة مواقف لثلاث نظريات."]]},"en.l18-history":{"k":"History of English","t":["A Short History of English","تاريخ الإنجليزية باختصار"],"body":[["English began with Germanic tribes, the Angles, Saxons and Jutes, who settled in Britain from the 5th century. Viking settlers later added Old Norse words such as sky, egg and they. After the **Norman Conquest of 1066**, French became the language of the court, law and government for about three centuries, and English absorbed thousands of French words. The printing press and Shakespeare mark **Early Modern English**. The **Great Vowel Shift** (about 1400–1700), which ran from late Middle English into this period, changed the pronunciation of long vowels after spelling had begun to settle, so English spelling no longer matches sound.","بدأت الإنجليزية مع قبائل جرمانية في القرن الخامس، ثم أضاف الفايكنغ كلمات، ثم جاء الغزو النورماندي 1066 بآلاف الكلمات الفرنسية. والطباعة وشكسبير علامة الحديثة المبكرة. وتغيّر نطق الصوائت الطويلة بين 1400 و1700 تقريباً بعد أن بدأت التهجئة تستقر، فابتعدت التهجئة عن النطق."]],"map":{"type":"cards","groups":[{"n":"Old English","ar":"⁧الإنجليزية القديمة⁩","idea":"⁧450–1100 تقريباً⁩","items":[{"n":"Anglo-Saxon","ar":"⁧قبائل جرمانية⁩","kw":["Beowulf"]},{"n":"-en plural","ar":"⁧بقيت في oxen و children⁩"}]},{"n":"Middle English","ar":"⁧الإنجليزية الوسطى⁩","idea":"⁧1100–1500 تقريباً⁩","items":[{"n":"1066","ar":"⁧الغزو النورماندي⁩","kw":["French words"]},{"n":"Chaucer","ar":"⁧أشهر كتّابها⁩"}]},{"n":"Early Modern","ar":"⁧الحديثة المبكرة⁩","idea":"⁧1500–1700 تقريباً⁩","items":[{"n":"Shakespeare","ar":"⁧الطباعة وتوحيد الكتابة⁩","kw":["printing"]},{"n":"Vowel Shift","ar":"⁧تغيّر نطق الصوائت الطويلة⁩","kw":["Great Vowel Shift"]}]},{"n":"Modern","ar":"⁧الحديثة⁩","idea":"⁧من 1700 تقريباً⁩","items":[{"n":"Dictionaries","ar":"⁧المعاجم وتوحيد المعيار⁩"},{"n":"Global","ar":"⁧انتشار عالمي وتنوعات⁩","kw":["World Englishes"]}]}]},"pts":[["French: café, croissant, restaurant, government.","من الفرنسية"],["Greek and Latin: most scientific and biological terms.","من اليونانية واللاتينية"],["Arabic: algebra, alcohol, sugar, coffee, cotton.","من العربية"]],"traps":[["English is **Germanic** despite its many Latin and French words.","جرمانية رغم الكلمات اللاتينية"],["Shakespeare = **Early Modern** English; Chaucer = Middle English.","شكسبير حديثة مبكرة"],["The most frequent everyday words (the, be, have, of) are **Germanic**.","الكلمات الأكثر استعمالاً جرمانية"]],"src":"Crystal, The Cambridge Encyclopedia of the English Language; Baugh & Cable, A History of the English Language; Yule, The Study of Language"},"en.l18-varieties":{"k":"Language Variation","t":["Dialect, Accent and Contact Languages","اللهجة واللكنة ولغات التماس"],"body":[["Variation is a normal, built-in part of every language, not a sign of decay. A **dialect** is a variety marked by grammar, vocabulary and pronunciation, linked to region or social group; an **accent** concerns pronunciation only. Everyone speaks a dialect and has an accent, including speakers of the standard.","التنوع جزء أصيل من كل لغة. اللهجة تختلف في القواعد والمفردات والنطق، واللكنة في النطق فقط، وكل متكلم له لهجة ولكنة."],["When speakers with no common language must communicate, for trade for example, they create a **pidgin**: a reduced contact language with limited vocabulary and simple grammar and no native speakers. When a new generation grows up speaking it as a first language, it expands into a full **creole**. **Diglossia** (Ferguson) describes two varieties of one language with separate functions, as with Fusha and colloquial Arabic.","الهجين لغة تواصل مبسطة بلا متحدثين أصليين، فإذا نشأ عليها جيل صارت كريول كاملة. والازدواجية وجود صيغتين للغة الواحدة لكل منهما وظيفة، كالفصحى والعامية."]],"pts":[["**Register**: variety by situation and formality.","السجل"],["**Idiolect**: one person's own way of speaking.","اللهجة الفردية"],["**Isogloss**: a map line marking where a language feature changes.","خط تساوي الظاهرة"],["**Lingua franca**: a shared language between speakers of different L1s.","اللغة المشتركة"]],"traps":[["Pronunciation only = **accent**; grammar + vocabulary + pronunciation = **dialect**.","اللكنة نطق فقط"],["A pidgin that became a mother tongue = **creole**.","الهجين الذي صار لغة أم كريول"],["Language variation is **inherent**, not found only in uneducated speech.","التنوع أصيل"]],"src":"Yule, The Study of Language; Trudgill, Sociolinguistics; Wardhaugh, An Introduction to Sociolinguistics","ex":[["The same sentence read by a Scottish and an American speaker: same words and grammar, different sounds — **accent**, not dialect.","الكلمات والقواعد نفسها والنطق مختلف: لكنة."]]},"en.l18-rpga":{"k":"Language Variation","t":["RP and General American","النطق البريطاني المعياري والأمريكي العام"],"body":[["**Received Pronunciation** is the traditional prestige accent of British English. It is non-regional, but only a small minority of British people actually speak it. **General American** is the broad accent heard in much of the US media. Dictionaries and coursebooks usually model one of the two.","النطق البريطاني المعياري لكنة مرموقة غير مرتبطة بمنطقة ولا يتكلمها إلا قلة، والأمريكي العام هو الشائع في الإعلام الأمريكي، والمعاجم والكتب تعتمد أحدهما."]],"pts":[["**Rhoticity**: GA pronounces /r/ after vowels (car, hard); RP does not.","نطق الراء بعد الصائت"],["**Flapped t**: GA often says 'water' and 'better' with a quick d-like sound.","التاء المرنة في الأمريكية"],["**bath, dance, can't**: RP /ɑː/, GA /æ/.","صائت bath"],["**hot, stop**: RP /ɒ/, GA /ɑ/.","صائت hot"]],"traps":[["An accent that pronounces /r/ in 'car' = **rhotic**; RP is **non-rhotic**.","الراوي ينطق الراء"],["Standard English is about grammar and vocabulary, **not** a particular accent.","المعيارية ليست لكنة"]],"src":"Roach, English Phonetics and Phonology; Trudgill & Hannah, International English"},"en.assess.intl":{"k":"Assessment","t":["International Standardized Proficiency Tests","الاختبارات الدولية المقننة"],"body":[["A **standardized test** is administered and scored under uniform conditions with fixed procedures, so results can be compared across test-takers and administrations. International tests are **proficiency** tests (not tied to a course) and **high-stakes**, so they create strong **washback** on teaching.","تطبيق وتصحيح موحّد يسمح بالمقارنة، وهي اختبارات كفاءة عالية التأثير."]],"pts":[["**IELTS** (owned by the British Council, IDP and Cambridge): **Academic** and **General Training** versions; listening, reading, writing, speaking; **band scale of 0–9**; speaking is a **face-to-face interview**.","آيلتس: نسختان، 0–9، مقابلة وجهاً لوجه"],["**TOEFL iBT** (ETS): internet-based test of **academic** English in four skills; speaking responses (including a simulated 'Take an Interview' task) are **recorded** and rated — no live examiner.","توفل: أكاديمي والمحادثة مسجّلة"],["Since **21 January 2026** TOEFL scores are reported on a **1–6 band scale** aligned with the CEFR, with a comparable 0–120 score during a two-year transition.","من يناير 2026 مقياس 1–6 مع 0–120 مرحلياً"],["Cambridge English Qualifications: **A2 Key (KET)**, **B1 Preliminary (PET)**, B2 First, C1 Advanced, C2 Proficiency; Key, Preliminary and First have **'for Schools'** versions.","كامبريدج: اختبار لكل مستوى ونسخ للمدارس"]],"traps":[["IELTS speaking = live interview; TOEFL speaking = recorded responses.","مقابلة حية مقابل تسجيل"],["KET = A2 and PET = B1 — not the other way round.","اختبار KET للمستوى A2، واختبار PET للمستوى B1"]],"src":"IELTS, ETS (TOEFL iBT) and Cambridge English official test information; Council of Europe, CEFR"},"en.assess.tos":{"k":"Test quality","t":["Table of Specifications","جدول المواصفات"],"body":[["A table of specifications is the test's blueprint: before writing items, the teacher lists the objectives and content taught and decides how many items, and which skills, will sample each one. It protects **content validity** because the test then represents the course fairly.","مخطط الاختبار قبل كتابة الأسئلة: يوزّع الأسئلة على الأهداف والمحتوى فيحمي صدق المحتوى."]],"pts":[["Careful consideration of objectives and content determines a course test's validity.","النظر في الأهداف والمحتوى يحدد الصدق"],["Weight each area by its importance and teaching time.","الوزن حسب الأهمية وزمن التدريس"]],"ex":[["A unit covered reading, vocabulary and writing equally, but the test is 80% grammar — a table of specifications would have shown the imbalance.","اختبار لا يمثّل الوحدة: خلل في صدق المحتوى"]],"traps":[["Table of specifications → content validity (not reliability).","جدول المواصفات = صدق المحتوى"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers"},"en.assess.ctest":{"k":"Testing skills","t":["Cloze and C-test","اختبار الإكمال واختبار C"],"body":[["Both are integrative gap tests. A classic **cloze** deletes words at a fixed interval (every nth word, e.g. every 7th — a **fixed-ratio** cloze) or selected words. A **C-test** deletes the **second half of every second word**; learners must read the damaged text to restore it.","الإكمال يحذف كلمات بانتظام، واختبار C يحذف النصف الثاني من كل كلمة ثانية."]],"pts":[["A C-test draws mainly on **reading** and overall proficiency.","يعتمد على القراءة والكفاءة العامة"],["Both are scored objectively although they are integrative.","تكاملي وموضوعي التصحيح"]],"ex":[["A Grade 11 cloze on Saudi coffee deletes every seventh word; students use grammar, vocabulary and reading together to restore it.","نص بفراغات يحتاج القواعد والمفردات والقراءة معاً"]],"traps":[["Deleting every nth word = fixed-ratio cloze; deleting half of every second word = C-test.","كل كلمة رقم n = إكمال، ونصف كل كلمة ثانية = C"]],"src":"Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers; Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment"},"en.assess.stats":{"k":"Test analysis","t":["Describing and Using Test Results","وصف نتائج الاختبار واستخدامها"],"body":[["Descriptive statistics summarise a set of scores: where the centre is and how spread out the scores are. Item analysis then shows which questions, and which objectives, need attention.","الإحصاء الوصفي يلخّص الدرجات: مركزها وتشتتها."]],"pts":[["**Mean** = average; **median** = middle score; **mode** = most frequent score.","المتوسط والوسيط والمنوال"],["**Range** and **standard deviation** = how spread out the scores are.","المدى والانحراف المعياري للتشتت"],["If most of the class fails items on one objective, **reteach** that objective.","إعادة تدريس الهدف الضعيف"],["Keep records of each learner's results to track progress and plan remediation.","سجل النتائج للمتابعة والعلاج"]],"traps":[["Most frequent score = mode, not median.","الأكثر تكراراً = المنوال"]],"src":"Brown & Abeywickrama, Language Assessment; Hughes, Testing for Language Teachers"},"en.plan.routines":{"k":"Classroom management","t":["Instructions, Transitions and Routines","التعليمات والانتقالات والروتين"],"body":[["Most lost time comes from unclear instructions and messy changes between activities. Short instructions given at the right moment, practised routines and planned transitions keep the lesson moving and raise student talking time.","التعليمات الواضحة والروتين والانتقالات المخططة تحفظ وقت الدرس."]],"pts":[["Instructions: short, demonstrated, given **before** students move and before handing out materials; then check (ICQs or a student explains the steps).","قصيرة ومع نمذجة وقبل الحركة"],["Keep **TTT** (teacher talking time) low to raise **STT**: gestures, demonstration and model examples instead of long explanations.","قلّل كلام المعلم"],["Rules: few, clear, ideally agreed with students; calm, consistent consequences; aim: a safe, orderly, supportive environment.","قواعد قليلة متفق عليها"],["Routines are taught, modelled, practised and reinforced.","الروتين يُعلّم ويُدرّب"],["Smooth transitions: materials ready, **signposting** ('Now that we've read…'), time warnings ('one minute left'), **extension tasks** for fast finishers.","انتقال سلس"],["Give groups clear **roles**: leader, note-taker, timekeeper, reporter.","أدوار المجموعة"]],"traps":[["Instructions come before students move into groups, not after.","التعليمات قبل الحركة"]],"src":"Scrivener, Learning Teaching; Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching"},"en.mat.tools":{"k":"Technology","t":["Madrasati and Classroom Digital Tools","مدرستي والأدوات الرقمية الصفية"],"body":[["**Madrasati** (مدرستي) is the Saudi Ministry of Education's national e-learning platform (launched 2020) for virtual classes, digital lessons, assignments, tests and communication with students and parents.","منصة وزارة التعليم الوطنية للفصول الافتراضية والدروس والواجبات والتواصل."]],"pts":[["**Kahoot** (game-based quizzes), **Quizlet** (vocabulary sets and flashcards), **Padlet** (collaborative digital wall).","اختبارات ألعاب وبطاقات وجدار رقمي"],["**Edpuzzle** (video with embedded questions), **Nearpod** (interactive lessons), **Google Classroom / Microsoft Teams** (class management).","فيديو بأسئلة ودروس تفاعلية وإدارة الصف"],["**MALL** (mobile-assisted language learning): phones and tablets for vocabulary apps, recording speaking and podcasts.","التعلم بالجوال"]],"traps":[["MALL = mobile; CALL = computer.","التعلم بالجوال (MALL) وبالحاسب (CALL)"]],"src":"Saudi Ministry of Education; Dudeney & Hockly, How to Teach English with Technology"},"en.mat.digcit":{"k":"Technology","t":["Generative AI and Digital Citizenship","الذكاء الاصطناعي التوليدي والمواطنة الرقمية"],"body":[["**Generative AI** can produce texts, dialogues and practice items, but the teacher must check accuracy, level and cultural fit, and teach responsible use.","يولّد نصوصاً وتمارين، والمعلم يتحقق من الدقة والمستوى والملاءمة."]],"pts":[["**Digital citizenship**: online safety, privacy and respect for **copyright**.","السلامة والخصوصية وحقوق النشر"],["Prefer openly licensed (e.g. **Creative Commons**) materials and cite sources.","مواد مرخصة مفتوحاً مع ذكر المصدر"],["Evaluate any app or website by **learning objectives first**, then usability, accuracy, cost, privacy and accessibility.","الهدف أولاً ثم الجوانب الأخرى"]],"traps":[["The first criterion for choosing a tool is the learning objective, not popularity or cost.","الهدف قبل الشهرة والتكلفة"]],"src":"Dudeney & Hockly, How to Teach English with Technology"},"en.mat.writetools":{"k":"Technology","t":["Digital Tools for Writing and Skills Practice","أدوات رقمية للكتابة وممارسة المهارات"],"body":[["Match the tool to the skill: text-based tools practise writing, video sites mainly listening, live calls speaking.","لكل مهارة أداتها."]],"pts":[["**Google Docs**: collaborative writing — several students edit the same document online at the same time.","كتابة تعاونية متزامنة"],["**Grammarly** checks grammar, spelling and style (a **writing** tool); in Microsoft Word, the **spelling checker** marks misspellings, the **thesaurus** suggests synonyms, **word count** counts words.","أدوات التدقيق في الكتابة"],["Text-based social networks and blogs (posts and comment threads) → writing; video sites → listening.","المنشورات للكتابة والفيديو للاستماع"],["**Online tutoring platforms** (e.g. Cambly) → live speaking with native or proficient speakers.","منصات المحادثة الحية"],["**Vlog** = video blog; **vodcast** = video podcast — recorded, one-way input.","مدونة مرئية وبودكاست مرئي"]],"traps":[["The thesaurus suggests synonyms; it does not check spelling.","المكنز للمرادفات"]],"src":"Dudeney & Hockly, How to Teach English with Technology"},"en.lrn.sen":{"k":"Learner differences","t":["Dyslexia and ADHD","عسر القراءة وفرط الحركة وتشتت الانتباه"],"body":[["Learning difficulties are not learning styles: they persist despite adequate teaching and need planned support.","صعوبات التعلم ليست أنماطاً، وتحتاج دعماً مخططاً."]],"pts":[["**Dyslexia**: persistent difficulty with decoding and spelling despite adequate teaching — use **multisensory** techniques, clear fonts, extra time and oral alternatives.","عسر القراءة: طرق متعددة الحواس ووقت إضافي"],["**ADHD**: difficulty sustaining attention and controlling impulses — short varied tasks, clear routines, movement breaks, seating near the teacher.","فرط الحركة: مهام قصيرة وروتين واستراحات حركة"]],"traps":[["Dyslexia and ADHD are learning difficulties, not cognitive styles.","صعوبات لا أساليب"]],"src":"Brown, Principles of Language Learning and Teaching; Kormos & Smith, Teaching Languages to Students with Specific Learning Differences"},"en.lis.skills":{"k":"Listening","t":["Receptive skills: hearing vs listening","مهارات الاستقبال: السماع والاستماع"],"body":[["Listening and reading are **receptive** skills (input); speaking and writing are **productive** (output). Receptive does not mean passive: **hearing** is only receiving sound, while **listening** means attending, decoding, linking to what we know and interpreting.","الاستقبال ليس سلبية: السماع التقاط صوت، والاستماع انتباه وفهم وتفسير."],["Listening is often called the **least understood** (most neglected) skill: unlike a reader, the listener cannot control the speed or go back. So input must be **meaningful** and comprehensible — roughly at the learners' level.","لا يتحكم المستمع في السرعة ولا يرجع، فيكون المدخل ذا معنى ومفهوماً."]],"ex":[["A class hears a short airport announcement and only needs the **flight number and the gate** — understanding a real message, not just hearing sounds.","المطلوب رقم الرحلة والبوابة فقط: فهم رسالة لا مجرد سماع."]],"traps":[["Hearing ≠ listening: hearing is physical; listening is active understanding.","السماع غير الاستماع"]],"src":"Brown, Teaching by Principles; Rost, Teaching and Researching Listening"},"en.lis.strat":{"k":"Listening","t":["Listener strategies","استراتيجيات المستمع"],"body":[["Strategies are what a listener does when understanding is incomplete. Questions usually describe a behaviour and ask for its name.","الاستراتيجية ما يفعله المستمع حين لا يكتمل الفهم، والسؤال يصف سلوكاً ويطلب اسمه."]],"pts":[["**Tolerance of ambiguity** — accepting that some information is unclear and continuing.","تحمّل الغموض: يكمل رغم عدم وضوح بعض المعلومات"],["**Compensation** — inferring missing information from context.","التعويض: استنتاج الناقص من السياق"],["**Self-monitoring** — checking one's own understanding while listening.","المراقبة الذاتية للفهم"],["**Cornell method** — a note-taking technique (notes, cue words, summary).","طريقة كورنيل لتدوين الملاحظات"],["**Shadowing** — repeating after the speaker at the same time.","المحاكاة المتزامنة للمتحدث"],["**Dictogloss** — hear a short text, note key words, reconstruct it in groups.","إعادة بناء النص بعد سماعه"]],"ex":[["A student misses one word in a dialogue about a hospital but continues, then guesses from *doctor* and *appointment* that it was *clinic* — **compensation** plus **tolerance of ambiguity**.","فاتته كلمة فأكمل وخمّنها من السياق: تعويض وتحمّل غموض."]],"traps":[["Going on despite unclear words = tolerance of ambiguity; guessing the missing word from context = compensation.","الاستمرار = تحمّل غموض، والتخمين = تعويض"]],"src":"Field, Listening in the Language Classroom; Vandergrift & Goh, Teaching and Learning Second Language Listening"},"en.lis.texts":{"k":"Listening","t":["Choosing listening texts and tasks","اختيار نص الاستماع ومهامه"],"body":[["Learner **interest** in the topic is the factor most linked to how engaging and followable a listening text is. Speed, length and accent matter too, but an interesting topic keeps attention.","الموضوع الممتع أهم عامل، والسرعة والطول واللهجة مهمة أيضاً."]],"pts":[["**Pre**: predicting from a title or picture, **generating questions** the text might answer, pre-teaching key words.","قبل: توقع وتوليد أسئلة وكلمات أساسية"],["**While**: note-taking, checking predictions.","أثناء: تدوين وتحقق من التوقعات"],["**Post**: summarizing, discussing.","بعد: تلخيص ونقاش"],["Following spoken classroom instructions is itself a listening task; 'listen and do' activities belong to [[TPR|en.meth.tpr]].","اتباع التعليمات استماع، و«اسمع ونفّذ» من TPR"]],"traps":[["Checking predictions happens **while** listening, not before.","التحقق من التوقعات أثناء الاستماع"]],"src":"Richards, Teaching Listening and Speaking; Field, Listening in the Language Classroom"},"en.spk.activities":{"k":"Speaking","t":["Speaking activities","أنشطة الكلام"],"body":[["Good speaking activities create a real **need** to talk: one learner has what another needs, or a role demands a certain way of speaking.","النشاط الجيد يخلق حاجة حقيقية للكلام."]],"pts":[["**Information gap** — a learner needs a partner's information to complete a task.","فجوة المعلومات: ما ينقصك عند زميلك"],["**Jigsaw** — each learner holds one part; the group shares to complete the whole.","كل طالب جزء والمجموعة تكمل"],["**Role play** — acting out characters with suitable dialogue; changing the partner (friend → minister) practises **register**.","لعب الأدوار ومستويات الخطاب"],["Shy learners: **group-based** activities; **games** add competition.","الخجول: جماعي، والألعاب منافسة"],["One-on-one oral interview: focus primarily on **correct content**.","المقابلة: المحتوى أولاً"]],"traps":[["Information gap is a speaking activity, not reading.","فجوة المعلومات كلام"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Brown, Teaching by Principles"},"en.spk.stress":{"k":"Pronunciation","t":["Word stress: tendencies and techniques","نبر الكلمة: قواعد تقريبية وتقنيات"],"body":[["Every English word of two or more syllables has one **primary stress**. A stressed syllable is louder, longer and higher in pitch, and its vowel is full; unstressed vowels often reduce to /ə/.","المقطع المنبور أعلى وأطول وصائته كامل، وغير المنبور يضعف إلى /ə/."]],"pts":[["Many two-syllable **nouns/adjectives** stress the first syllable (**TA**ble, **HAP**py); many two-syllable **verbs** the second (de**CIDE**, be**GIN**) — tendencies, not absolute rules.","ميول لا قواعد مطلقة"],["Word class: **RE**cord (noun) vs re**CORD** (verb), **PRE**sent vs pre**SENT**.","النبر يفرّق الاسم عن الفعل"],["**-tion, -sion, -ic, -ity**: stress just before the suffix — infor**MA**tion, eco**NOM**ic, a**BIL**ity.","قبل اللاحقة مباشرة"],["Compound nouns: **GREEN**house (a building for plants) vs green **HOUSE** (a house painted green).","المركّب على الجزء الأول"],["Techniques: circles (oOo) or underlining, clapping or tapping, sorting words into stress-pattern columns, humming the rhythm.","دوائر وتصفيق وتصنيف ودندنة"]],"traps":[["Teach stress **with every new word**, not as a separate rule list.","علّم النبر مع كل كلمة جديدة"]],"src":"Kelly, How to Teach Pronunciation; Roach, English Phonetics and Phonology"},"en.spk.fluency":{"k":"Speaking","t":["Building speaking fluency","بناء طلاقة الكلام"],"body":[["Fluency research gives simple classroom techniques. Repeating a task and planning before it free the learner's attention, so speech becomes faster, smoother and more complex.","تكرار المهمة والتخطيط لها يحرران الانتباه فيصبح الكلام أسرع وأسلس."]],"pts":[["**4/3/2** — the same talk to three different partners in 4, then 3, then 2 minutes: repetition under time pressure.","نفس الحديث بوقت يقصر"],["**Task repetition** — doing the same or a similar task again.","تكرار المهمة"],["**Pre-task planning time** — a few minutes to prepare improves fluency and complexity (task-based research).","وقت التخطيط قبل المهمة"]],"traps":[["Fluency work uses familiar language — no new items.","الطلاقة بلغة مألوفة"]],"src":"Nation & Newton, Teaching ESL/EFL Listening and Speaking; Ellis, Task-based Language Learning and Teaching"},"en.spk.peer":{"k":"Speaking","t":["Repair strategies, role-play and peer correction","استراتيجيات الإصلاح وتصحيح الأقران"],"pts":[["**Repair strategies** — formulaic phrases to fix breakdowns: asking for repetition ('Sorry, could you say that again?'), asking for help or clarification, paraphrasing a missing word.","عبارات جاهزة لإصلاح انقطاع التواصل"],["**Role-play** — two or three learners act out an extended dialogue; the teacher is a **facilitator**: sets up roles, monitors, supports and gives feedback afterwards instead of taking over.","المعلم ميسّر يراقب ويؤجل التصحيح"],["**Peer correction** — learners point out and fix each other's errors before the teacher steps in; it builds **learner autonomy**, cooperation and awareness of errors.","يبني الاستقلالية والتعاون والوعي بالأخطاء"],["Peer correction works best in controlled practice, with clear guidance and a supportive atmosphere; the teacher still confirms the correct form.","مع توجيه وجو داعم"]],"src":"Harmer, The Practice of English Language Teaching; Scrivener, Learning Teaching"},"en.rd.fluency":{"k":"Reading","t":["Reading fluency, silent and oral reading","طلاقة القراءة والقراءة الصامتة والجهرية"],"body":[["Reading fluency means **accuracy, rate and prosody** together, and it begins with **direct instruction**. Learners with **dyslexia** mainly need to improve **word recognition**.","الطلاقة دقة وسرعة وتنغيم، وتبدأ بالتعليم المباشر، وعسر القراءة يحتاج تمييز الكلمات."]],"pts":[["**Repeated reading** — reading the same text several times.","قراءة النص نفسه مرات"],["**Timed reading** — tracking words per minute.","قياس الكلمات في الدقيقة"],["**Silent reading** — faster, focused on meaning, builds fluency (speed with comprehension).","الصامتة أسرع وتبني الطلاقة"],["**Oral reading** — checks decoding and pronunciation; slower, splits attention between sound and meaning.","الجهرية للدقة، وأبطأ"]],"src":"Grabe & Stoller, Teaching and Researching Reading; Nation, Teaching ESL/EFL Reading and Writing"},"en.rd.arab":{"k":"Reading","t":["Arab learners reading English","المتعلم العربي وقراءة الإنجليزية"],"body":[["Written Arabic usually omits short vowels and runs right to left, so Arab learners often need explicit work on English **vowel letters** and on left-to-right **word recognition**.","العربية تحذف الحركات وتُكتب من اليمين، فيحتاج الطالب تدريباً صريحاً على الحروف الصائتة واتجاه القراءة."]],"src":"Swan & Smith, Learner English (Arabic speakers chapter)"},"en.voc.activities":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["Vocabulary activities and assessment","أنشطة المفردات وتقويمها"],"pts":[["'Odd one out', crosswords, puzzles, matching and bingo are **vocabulary** activities; a crossword develops **lexical** knowledge.","الشاذة والمتقاطعة والمطابقة = مفردات"],["Teaching **affixes** increases both vocabulary and syntactic ability.","السوابق واللواحق"],["Vocabulary assessment checks meaning, pronunciation, spelling, part of speech and usage.","ماذا نقيس في المفردات"],["Digital flashcard tools such as **Quizlet** are widely used for vocabulary practice.","بطاقات رقمية للتدريب"]],"traps":[["Odd one out = vocabulary, not listening or reading.","الشاذة = مفردات"]],"src":"Thornbury, How to Teach Vocabulary; Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language"},"en.voc.assoc":{"k":"Vocabulary","t":["Word association, lexical sets and review","ترابط الكلمات والمجموعات المعجمية والمراجعة"],"pts":[["**Association** — words linked in memory around one topic (tree → apples, leaves, green); not synonymy or antonymy. Brainstorming associations helps recall.","كلمات مترابطة حول موضوع"],["**Lexical set** — words from one topic or semantic field taught together (sports: archery, fencing, rowing, scuba diving).","كلمات موضوع واحد معاً"],["Review routine: **write** the word, **look** at it, **practise** using it, **repeat** the review over days.","اكتب، انظر، تدرّب، كرّر"],["**CCQs** test the core meaning, often as either/or questions: 'furious' → 'A bit angry or very angry?'","أسئلة ثنائية لفحص المعنى"]],"ex":[["The teacher writes *Ramadan* in the middle of the board and students add *fasting, iftar, dates, family, charity* — a word-association map that later becomes a writing plan.","خريطة ترابط حول رمضان تتحول لخطة كتابة."],["After teaching *furious*: *Is he a bit angry or very angry? (Very.) Would he smile? (No.)* — two quick CCQs.","سؤالان سريعان يكشفان فهم المعنى."]],"src":"Thornbury, How to Teach Vocabulary; Gairns & Redman, Working with Words"},"en.gr.reported":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Teaching reported speech","تدريس الكلام المنقول"],"pts":[["After a past reporting verb, tenses usually **shift back**: present → past, will → would, past simple → past perfect; tomorrow → the next day, here → there.","الرجوع خطوة في الزمن والظرف"],["Backshift is **optional** when the statement is still true: 'He said the sun **rises** in the east.'","اختياري إذا بقي صحيحاً"],["**say** vs **tell**: tell needs a person object ('She told **me**…'); say does not ('She said that…').","tell تحتاج مفعولاً"],["Reported questions: statement word order, no do/does/did ('He asked where I **lived**', not 'where did I live'); yes/no → **if/whether**.","ترتيب الخبر، والأداة if/whether لأسئلة نعم/لا"],["Practice: messenger/relay games, reporting a classmate's interview or survey answers.","ألعاب نقل الرسائل"]],"src":"Swan, Practical English Usage; Thornbury, How to Teach Grammar"},"en.gr.articles":{"k":"Grammar","t":["Articles and countable/uncountable nouns","أدوات التعريف والمعدود وغير المعدود"],"pts":[["**a/an**: first mention of a singular countable noun; choice by **sound**: an hour, a university.","a/an حسب الصوت"],["**the**: already known or mentioned, unique (the sun), or made specific ('the book on the desk').","the للمعروف والمحدد"],["**Zero article**: general statements with plural or uncountable nouns.","بلا أداة للتعميم"],["Uncountable in English: information, advice, furniture, news, equipment, homework — no 'informations/advices'; use **partitives**: a piece of advice, a loaf of bread.","لا جمع لها، ونعدّها بالكميات"],["Quantifiers: many / a few + countable; much / a little + uncountable. Practise with shopping lists, recipes, 'How much / How many' role plays.","قوائم تسوق ووصفات"]],"traps":[["Arab learners overuse 'the' for general meaning ('The honesty…') because Arabic uses 'al-' for generic and abstract nouns.","«ال» العامة من العربية"]],"src":"Swan, Practical English Usage; Swan & Smith, Learner English"},"en.gr.passrel":{"k":"Grammar","t":["The passive and relative clauses","المبني للمجهول وجمل الصلة"],"pts":[["Passive = **be + past participle**; use it when the agent is unknown, unimportant or obvious, or to focus on the action/result — common in processes, news and science.","متى نستخدم المجهول"],["Only transitive verbs have a passive: '*The accident **was happened**' is wrong (happen is intransitive).","لا مجهول للازم"],["Relative pronouns: **who** (people), **which** (things), **that** (people/things in defining clauses), **whose** (possession), **where** (place), **when** (time).","ضمائر الوصل"],["**Defining** clauses identify the noun: no commas, 'that' allowed, object pronoun can be omitted ('the book (that) I bought'). **Non-defining** add extra information: commas, no 'that'.","المحددة وغير المحددة"],["Resumptive pronoun ('the book that I read **it**') comes from Arabic's returning pronoun; practise with sentence combining and definition games ('It's a person who…').","الضمير العائد من العربية"]],"src":"Swan, Practical English Usage; Thornbury, How to Teach Grammar; Swan & Smith, Learner English"},"en.l15-behav":{"k":"شرح موسّع","t":["Behaviorism","السلوكية"],"body":[["Behaviorists see learning as **habit formation**: a stimulus produces a response, and reinforcement makes the response stronger. The mind is treated as a 'black box' — only observable behavior counts.","التعلم عادة تتكوّن: مثير ← استجابة ← تعزيز، والمهم السلوك الظاهر فقط."],["In language teaching this led to [[Skinner|en.skinner]]'s view of language as verbal behavior and to the **Audio-Lingual Method**: repetition drills, imitation and avoiding errors so bad habits do not form.","ومنها جاءت الطريقة السمعية الشفهية: تكرار وتقليد وتجنّب الخطأ."]],"traps":[["Blank slate (tabula rasa) = behaviorism, not nativism.","الصفحة البيضاء = السلوكية"]]},"en.l16-stages":{"k":"شرح موسّع","t":["Stages of Second Language Acquisition","مراحل اكتساب اللغة الثانية"],"body":[["Krashen & Terrell's **Natural Approach** describes three early stages — **preproduction**, **early production** and **speech emergence**. Teacher-education materials commonly add **intermediate fluency** and **advanced fluency**, giving five.","ثلاث مراحل في الأصل، وتضيف مواد إعداد المعلمين مرحلتين فتصير خمساً."]],"pts":[["**Preproduction**: learners understand more than they say; they point, gesture, nod or draw. Teacher: TPR, pictures, yes/no questions; do not force speech.","يفهم ولا يتكلم؛ لا تجبره على الكلام"],["**Early production**: one- or two-word answers and memorised chunks. Teacher: either/or and who/what/where questions, labelling, lists.","كلمة أو كلمتان: أسئلة اختيار وتسمية"],["**Speech emergence**: simple sentences and short conversations with frequent errors. Teacher: how/why questions, short stories, dialogues.","جمل بسيطة وأخطاء كثيرة"],["**Intermediate fluency**: complex sentences, opinions, fewer errors. Teacher: 'What would happen if…?', compare and explain, writing tasks.","جمل مركبة وآراء"],["**Advanced fluency**: near-native performance in academic contexts — the level linked to Cummins' **CALP**, which takes years.","قرب الطلاقة الأكاديمية"]],"ex":[["Teacher: 'Is this a mosque or a school?' Student: 'Mosque.' An either/or question suits early production; 'What would happen if there were no traffic lights?' waits for intermediate fluency.","سؤال الاختيار يناسب الإنتاج المبكر، وسؤال «ماذا لو» يُؤجَّل للطلاقة المتوسطة."]],"traps":[["Pointing and nodding without speaking = **preproduction** (silent period).","الإشارة بلا كلام = ما قبل الإنتاج"]]},"en.l16-gll":{"k":"شرح موسّع","t":["The Good Language Learner","المتعلم الجيد للغة"],"body":[["'Good language learner' studies (e.g. Rubin, 1975) asked what successful learners do differently. The findings fed directly into strategy research such as Oxford's.","دراسات المتعلم الجيد بحثت ما يفعله الناجحون، ومنها انطلقت أبحاث الاستراتيجيات."]],"pts":[["Guesses willingly and accurately.","يخمّن بلا تردد"],["Wants to communicate and is not inhibited by mistakes.","يحب التواصل ولا يخاف الخطأ"],["Attends to both form and meaning.","ينتبه للشكل والمعنى"],["Practises and monitors his own speech and that of others.","يتدرب ويراقب كلامه"]]},"en.l17-disc":{"k":"شرح موسّع","t":["TESOL and Related Disciplines","علاقة تعليم الإنجليزية بالعلوم الأخرى"],"pts":[["**Psycholinguistics**: how language is processed and acquired in the mind (memory, input processing). **Sociolinguistics**: language in society (register, varieties, attitudes). **Neurolinguistics**: language and the brain.","النفسي والاجتماعي والعصبي"],["**Educational psychology** informs motivation, learning theories and assessment; **anthropology** informs culture teaching; **corpus linguistics** informs word lists and coursebooks; **computer science** informs CALL and AI tools.","علوم تغذي التدريس"],["**CLIL**: hard (strong) CLIL = a subject taught in English; soft (weak) CLIL = an English course that uses subject topics.","التدريس عبر المحتوى"],["**Cross-curricular links**: reading science texts, solving maths word problems in English, writing about history or geography — content gives language a real purpose and supports academic language (CALP).","روابط بين المواد"]],"traps":[["Corpus linguistics feeds word-frequency lists and coursebooks.","المدونات ← قوائم الكلمات"]]},"en.l18-defs":{"k":"شرح موسّع","t":["Classic Definitions of Language","تعريفات اللغة الكلاسيكية"],"pts":[["**Sapir**: a purely human and non-instinctive method of communicating ideas, emotions and desires by means of voluntarily produced symbols.","سابير: بشرية غير غريزية، برموز إرادية"],["**Hall**: the institution whereby humans communicate and interact with each other by means of habitually used oral-auditory arbitrary symbols.","هول: مؤسسة تواصل برموز صوتية سمعية اعتباطية"],["**Chomsky**: a set (finite or infinite) of sentences, each finite in length and constructed out of a finite set of elements.","تشومسكي: مجموعة جمل من عناصر محدودة"]],"traps":[["'Non-instinctive' and 'voluntarily produced symbols' point to **Sapir**.","غير غريزية = سابير"]]},"en.l18-hockett2":{"k":"شرح موسّع","t":["More of Hockett's Design Features","المزيد من سمات هوكيت"],"pts":[["**Interchangeability**: any speaker can be both sender and receiver.","تبادل الأدوار"],["**Total feedback**: speakers hear and can monitor their own messages.","التغذية الراجعة الكاملة"],["**Specialisation**: speech signals serve communication, not another biological function. **Semanticity**: signals have fixed associations with meanings.","التخصص والدلالية"],["**Vocal-auditory channel**: produced by the vocal tract, received by the ear. **Rapid fading**: spoken signals disappear quickly.","القناة والزوال"],["Later additions: **prevarication** (lying, talking about the false or imaginary), **reflexiveness** (using language to talk about language), **learnability** (any human language can be learned).","إضافات لاحقة"]],"traps":[["Using language to talk about language = **reflexiveness**.","الانعكاسية"]]},"en.l18-brame":{"k":"شرح موسّع","t":["British and American English","البريطانية والأمريكية"],"pts":[["The main differences lie in **vocabulary** (lift/elevator, petrol/gasoline), **spelling** (colour/color) and **pronunciation**; grammar differs very little.","مفردات وإملاء ونطق"],["British English uses past forms in **-t** more often (learnt, burnt, spilt, dreamt); American English prefers **-ed** (learned, burned, spilled).","-t / -ed"],["Plurals such as **oxen** and **children** keep the Old English plural ending **-en**.","جمع -en قديم"]],"traps":[["'colour' is British; Americans write **-or**.","colour بريطانية"]]}};
